design:
## Context

The preview computes what a `PRODUCE` order makes as `men / man_months`. The unit's level in the
producing skill never enters it, and neither do the tools it carries. Both are in the game's own
arithmetic.

`rules/tableiteminfo`: *"The higher the skill of the unit, the more productive each man-month of
work will be. Thus, five men at skill level one are exactly equivalent to one man at skill level 5
in terms of base output."* Its worked example is explicit - six level-1 men make **two** of a
three-man-month item, six level-2 men make **four**, six level-3 men make **six** - so the
man-months a unit brings are `men * level`. The same page gives eight items a production bonus
(`+1 bonus when producing iron` on a pick, `+2 bonus when producing grain` on a bag), which adds to
the same rate.

What it costs today: every producing unit's ITEMS cell, the ITEMS and SILVER hovers, the production
cost in the SILVER column and the `not-enough-silver` / `not-enough-items` warnings are computed
from a figure too small by a factor of the unit's level - 3 to 5 for a mature faction.

`plan_build` (`crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs:4160`) is documented as *"`men * level`, capped by
what the structure still wants"*, so this is an omission in `PRODUCE` alone and not a house
convention. It came from `ah-19l2.2`'s own plan, which wrote the arithmetic as
`wanted = men / man_months` and worked its example wrongly; the code matches that plan exactly.

Corroboration from a real report: `tests/fixtures/reports/neworigins-3.0.0-g3-f42-t42.rep:514` says
`MinersA (5105): Produces 20 iron [IRON]`, and line 1456 shows that unit as 8 orcs with
`mining [MINI] 5 (450)`. The app says 8. The correct figure for *this bead* is 40; the report's 20
is the region's iron pool shared between the four units mining that hex, which this bead does not
model - see **Out of scope** and `ah-256d`.

**This bead is P0 and reached the queue with the buffer empty.** It is a wrong number on a surface
players act on, not a missing feature.

**Read `docs/ui/ah-vtwn-production-at-skill-level.html`** (merged in #791) before starting. It
records the three decisions the navigator took, each with the variants that were rejected and why.

**One thing changed under this plan while it was being written.** `ah-qct4` merged as #790 and
moved `semantics::produce` from `actor.unit.men, &actor.unit.items` to
`actor.early_men(), actor.early_items()`, and added `productionMenLeft` and its
`productionMenSentence`. Every line number below was read from that commit (`ae426d8`) or later.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs` - the arithmetic

The whole of the new arithmetic lives here, beside `plan_production` (`:3071`), and both surfaces
call it. That is not a preference: `ah-ycuj`'s corpus test
(`crates/core/tests/silver_agrees_with_the_warning.rs`) exists because the SILVER column and the
`not-enough-silver` warning drifted apart once, and one shared function is what stops it happening
again. Nothing in this bead may compute a production rate anywhere else.

**New public surface.** Write it exactly as this:

```rust
/// What a unit brings to one month of one recipe.
///
/// A struct rather than four more positional arguments, for the reason [`UnitFacts`] is one: the
/// call site should read as a description of the unit.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]
pub struct Workforce {
    /// The headcount, as the calling surface reads it. The two surfaces read different ones and
    /// that is deliberate - see the module's known traps - so this is the caller's to supply.
    pub men: i64,
    /// The unit's level in the skill that makes the recipe. `0` for a skill it has not got.
    pub level: i64,
    /// The per-man bonus of the tool the unit holds for this item. `0` where it holds none.
    pub tool_bonus: i64,
    /// How many of that tool the unit holds. `0` where it holds none.
    pub tools: i64,
}

impl Workforce {
    /// `men * level + min(men, tools) * tool_bonus`.
    ///
    /// The level term is the rules': "five men at skill level one are exactly equivalent to one
    /// man at skill level 5 in terms of base output" (`rules/tableiteminfo`). The `min` is not -
    /// the same page states each tool's bonus and never says how many men one tool serves, so
    /// one tool to one man, spare tools idle, is the navigator's reading of the game, recorded in
    /// `docs/ui/ah-vtwn-production-at-skill-level.html`. Do not "correct" it to `men * (level +
    /// tool_bonus)`: that was one of the two readings it was chosen over.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn man_months(self) -> i64 {
        self.men
            .saturating_mul(self.level)
            .saturating_add(self.men.min(self.tools).saturating_mul(self.tool_bonus))
            .max(0)
    }
}

/// The workforce a unit brings to one recipe, built the one way both production surfaces must
/// build it.
///
/// `men` is the caller's because the two surfaces read different headcounts; everything else is
/// computed here exactly once, so the level and the tools cannot drift between them.
#[must_use]
pub fn workforce_for(
    ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
    skill: &SkillEntry,
    tag: &str,
    men: i64,
    skills: &[Skill],
    held: &[ItemAmount],
) -> Workforce;

/// The tool a unit holds that best helps it produce `tag`, as `(per-man bonus, how many held)`.
///
/// `(0, 0)` where the unit holds no such tool, where there is no ruleset, and where nothing in the
/// catalogue boosts that tag - which is most items. The highest bonus wins; ties go to the lower
/// item tag, which `Ruleset::items`' own `BTreeMap` order gives. Deterministic on purpose, and
/// unobservable in this ruleset: no item in it is boosted by two different tools.
#[must_use]
pub fn tools_for(ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>, tag: &str, held: &[ItemAmount]) -> (i64, i64);

/// The per-item production bonuses one item's data-page description states.
///
/// Every `[TAG] by <n>` in the sentence beginning "This item increases the production of", in the
/// order written; empty for a description without that sentence, which is every item that is not
/// a tool.
///
/// Reads the prose rather than a scraped field for the same reason [`required_riding`] does: the
/// ruleset carries each item's description whole, nothing else in it states this, and adding a
/// scraped field would drag `packages/ruleset` and a regenerated `config/public/ruleset.json` into
/// a P0.
fn production_bonuses(description: &str) -> Vec<(String, i64)>;
```

**`producing_skill` moves here from `semantics.rs:6565` and becomes `pub`.** It is
`recipe_for` (`:3004`) extended to name the skill as well as the recipe, and it already answers the
question the level lookup needs - *which* skill makes this item, preferring one the unit has. Three
callers must agree on that, so there must be one function:

- change its first parameter to `ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>` (it is `&Ruleset` today) and return
  `None` for `None`;
- keep `skills: Option<&[Skill]>` exactly as it is - `check_produce_orders` distinguishes `None`
  from an empty list and that distinction must survive the move;
- `check_produce_orders` (`semantics.rs:6427`) then calls it as
  `producing_skill(Some(ruleset), &tag, skills)`;
- `semantics.rs` imports it from `crate::orders::silver` in place of `recipe_for`, in the same
  `use` block it already takes twenty other names from (`semantics.rs:36`) - **`producing_skill`,
  `workforce_for` and `Workforce` all three**, since `price_production`'s new signature names the
  struct.

Nothing new crosses into TypeScript: `Workforce` is Rust-only and `ProductionPlan` has never been
`ts_rs`-exported, so `crates/core/tests/generated_ts.rs` needs no regeneration and
`packages/core-client/src/generated/` does not change. `ProductionCap` is untouched.

**`recipe_for` (`:3004`) is then unused and is deleted**, along with its entry in `semantics.rs`'s
import list. Both of its callers switch to `producing_skill`. That is a small behaviour change worth
naming: `recipe_for` takes the first recipe it finds, `producing_skill` prefers the skill the unit
actually has and otherwise the lowest required level. It makes the number agree with the warning
that talks about it, which is the point.

**`plan_production` (`:3071`) and `price_production` (`:2886`)** take a `Workforce` in place of
`men: i64`:

```rust
pub fn plan_production(recipe: &Production, work: Workforce, held: &[ItemAmount])
    -> Option<ProductionPlan>;
pub fn price_production(recipe: Option<&Production>, work: Workforce, held: &[ItemAmount])
    -> (Priced, Option<ProductionPlan>);
```

Inside `plan_production`, after the two `?` guards that reject an unpriceable recipe and before
anything else is computed:

```rust
    // A unit below the recipe's minimum level makes nothing at all, whatever its headcount:
    // `rules/tableiteminfo` states a minimum level for every recipe. Returned as an empty plan
    // rather than `None`, because the recipe *is* priceable - this unit simply makes none of it,
    // and `None` would put a `?` in the column where a 0 belongs.
    if work.level < i64::from(recipe.level) {
        return Some(ProductionPlan::default());
    }
    let wanted = (work.man_months() / man_months) * outputs;
```

`ProductionPlan`, `ProductionCap` and everything downstream are unchanged: `wanted` keeps its
meaning ("how many its men alone would make"), so `UnitSilver::production_wanted` and the whole TS
side need no new field. Update `ProductionPlan::wanted`'s doc comment, which says
`men / man_months` in as many words.

`recipe.level` is `u32` and `Production` is at `crates/core/src/movement/rules.rs:508`; the recipe's
required level is already carried and is read today only by `check_produce_orders`.

### `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs:1167` - the SILVER column

```rust
let found = (lookups.item_tag)(item).and_then(|tag| {
    producing_skill(ruleset, &tag, Some(facts.skills))
        .map(|(skill, recipe)| (tag, skill, recipe))
});
let work = found.as_ref().map_or(Workforce::default(), |(tag, skill, _)| {
    workforce_for(ruleset, skill, tag, facts.late().men, facts.skills, facts.items)
});
let recipe = found.as_ref().map(|(_, _, recipe)| *recipe);
let (priced, plan) = price_production(recipe, work, facts.items);
```

The `tag` is carried alongside because `workforce_for` needs it to find the tools, and
`(lookups.item_tag)` allocates - calling it twice is both wasteful and a second chance to disagree
with itself.

Keep `facts.late().men` and `facts.items` exactly as they are - the comment above that line explains
why the headcount is late and the materials early, and this bead does not touch either.

### `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs:4104` - the ITEMS ledger

```rust
let tag = resolve_item(item, hex, actor, ruleset);           // unchanged, `:4096`
let found = tag
    .as_deref()
    .and_then(|tag| producing_skill(ruleset, tag, Some(&actor.unit.skills)));
let work = found.map_or(Workforce::default(), |(skill, _)| {
    workforce_for(
        ruleset,
        skill,
        tag.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),
        actor.early_men(),
        &actor.unit.skills,
        actor.early_items(),
    )
});
let (priced, plan) =
    price_production(found.map(|(_, recipe)| recipe), work, actor.early_items());
```

`tag` is already a local here (`:4096`) and is used again further down for the produced
`ItemMovement`, so it is borrowed rather than moved; the `unwrap_or_default()` arm is unreachable,
because `found` is `Some` only where `tag` was.

**`actor.unit.skills`, not `actor.skills()`, and that is a decision.** `actor.skills()` is the list
after this month's gifts of men have been merged in, and it is what `plan_build` and
`produce-without-skill` read. Production reads the report's own list instead, because that is the
list the SILVER column has (`UnitFacts.skills` is `&ordered.unit.skills`, `semantics.rs:978` and
`:2937`) and the two surfaces must read the same one or `ah-ycuj`'s corpus test is the thing that
finds out. Moving every reader to the merged list is `ah-dna4`, filed for it. **Do not change
`UnitFacts.skills` in this bead**: `readiness`, `ENTERTAIN` and `CAST` all read it too.

### `packages/shared/src/unitTooltip.ts` - the one sentence

`capSentence` (`:194`) takes `could: "its men" | "its level"`. Widen it to
`could: "its skill and tools" | "its level"` - `"its men"` has no other caller - and change
`productionCapSentence`'s argument at `:220` from `"its men"` to `"its skill and tools"`. The
sentence then reads, exactly:

> `This unit has materials for 5 swords, not the 46 its skill and tools could make.`

Nothing else in the sentence moves: `countOf`, the `has` word and the verb are untouched, and
`castCapSentence` (`:299`) keeps `"its level"`.

Three doc comments quote the old wording and must be corrected with it: `:189`, `:206`, and the
`SILVER_NOTES` ordering comment at `:631` ("that sentence quotes 'the N its men could make'").

### `tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts:4391` - the one browser walk that breaks

The test *"a produced item marks the ITEMS cell as a projection"* (`:4391`) is built on a premise
this bead reverses. Its own comment (`:4386`) says so: *"Unit 18642 is a one-man leader with no lumberjack skill, and
that is deliberate: the projection is taken from the recipe, the men and the holdings, and never
from whether the unit can actually do it."* After this bead that unit produces nothing and the cell
is not marked, so the walk fails.

No own unit of the default turn-71 fixture can produce anything - all five carry only observation,
stealth and manipulation - so the fixture must change. The suite already solves this exact problem
one test below, for `CAST` (`:4436`): `await loadReport(page, "Cast smoke", readReport("g5f21t23"), "regions")`.
Follow it, with the fixture this file already imports at `:64`:

```ts
await loadReport(page, "Produce smoke", F42_T42, "regions");
await selectHex(page, "1:36,4");
await selectUnit(page, "5105");
await fillOrders(page, "PRODUCE iron");
```

`MinersA (5105)` is 8 orcs with `mining [MINI] 5 (450)` in `mountain (36,4) in Slounspifra`
(fixture lines 1456-1459 and 1330), holds no picks, and iron takes no materials - so it makes 40
and nothing caps it. Assert `toContainText("IRON")` and the existing
`toHaveAttribute("title", /this unit will produce/)`, and rewrite the doc comment: the projection
now *does* depend on whether the unit can do it, and the old comment states the opposite.

`selectHex` takes the region id as the accessible name `hex <id>` (`tests/smoke/gameSetup.ts:142`);
`1:` is the surface level, as `1:7,53` and `1:36,44` in this same file already are.

## Increments

Each opens with a failing test. Run `cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core` after each Rust increment and
`pnpm --filter @atlantis/shared test` after the TypeScript one.

**1. A unit's PRODUCE counts its skill level.**

RED: `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs`, in the existing `mod production_tests` beside
`ten_men_make_two_catapults_at_four_man_months` (`:3432`), add:

