## Known traps

- **`crates/core/tests/silver_agrees_with_the_warning.rs` is the net under this whole bead.** The
  SILVER column and the ITEMS ledger price the same order twice, through two arms that share only
  their inputs, and they have drifted before (`ah-abwx`, PILLAGE). Give both call sites their
  workforce through `workforce_for` and nothing else.
- **The two surfaces already read different headcounts, on purpose.** `facts.late().men` in the
  column, `actor.early_men()` in the ledger, with the reason in the comment at `silver.rs:1156` and
  in `ah-qct4`. `Workforce.men` is the caller's field precisely so this bead does not have to have
  an opinion about it. Do not unify them.
- **Most existing `PRODUCE` tests build units with no skills at all**, and after this bead such a
  unit makes nothing. `semantics.rs`'s seven `with_men(unit("2391"), 8)` sites and `effects.rs`'s two
  skill-less smith fixtures (`:1563`, `:1584`)
  are the two known clusters, and a test that silently starts asserting "nothing was produced" is
  worse than one that fails. Read every `PRODUCE` test's unit before trusting a green run.
- **`docs/ui/…` and the fixture reports are the arbiters of every number in this plan, not
  memory.** `carpenters()` (`semantics.rs:15452`) really is `CARP` 5 and the catapult really is
  four man-months at level 4; the `wanted` in those tests goes to 12, not to 10 or 50.
- **No own unit of the turn-71 smoke fixture can produce anything.** All five carry only
  observation, stealth and manipulation. The smoke walk must move to `F42_T42`, which the file
  already imports.
- **`bd show <id> --json` returns an array**, and `dependencies` uses `dependency_type`. Irrelevant
  to the code, relevant to reading this bead back.
- From `.cerebro/traps.md`: **the smoke suite rebuilds the web bundle with the service worker
  disabled**, so a `test:pwa` run straight after `test:smoke` fails with timeouts that look like a
  broken worker. This bead touches one smoke test, so run `test:smoke` last or rebuild in between.
- From `.cerebro/traps.md`: **never ask for an effect-level test in `packages/shared`.** The only
  TypeScript change here is `unitTooltip.ts`, a pure module with a plain `.test.ts`, so this does
  not bite - but do not "improve" the coverage by reaching for a `.test.tsx` that observes an
  effect.

## Context

Every silver figure this application shows, and the pillage warning beside it, is priced from the
unit **as the report printed it** — the headcount, the item list and the skills, untouched by
anything this month's orders do. `UnitFacts` (`crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs:615`) is that
reading, and it is built in two places, both straight off `ordered.unit`: `forecast_hex`
(`orders/semantics.rs:865`) for the SILVER column, and `hex_facts` (`orders/semantics.rs:2046`) for
the checks. So a unit formed this month and given ten combat-ready men is warned it has none; a unit
given men taxes, works, entertains and pays maintenance as though they had not arrived.

**The turn's own order is what makes this answerable, and it also says the obvious fix is wrong.**
`rules/sequenceofevents` runs, in order: GIVE and TAKE; PILLAGE and TAX; `Spells are CAST`; SELL then
BUY; WITHDRAW; movement; STUDY, PRODUCE, BUILD, ENTERTAIN, WORK; then *"Maintenance costs are
assessed"*. Two distinct pictures of one unit follow from that:

- **The early picture** — the report plus this month's GIVE and TAKE. PILLAGE, TAX and CAST are
  settled here, before the market opens.
- **The late picture** — everything, market and withdrawals and production included. STUDY, PRODUCE,
  ENTERTAIN, WORK and maintenance are settled here.

The parent bead's mockup (`docs/ui/ah-dxfd-weapons-too.html`) proposed reading the **ledger's
end-of-month balance** and reordering `ledger_for` so item transfers settle before the silver is
priced. That is the late picture, and using it for PILLAGE would count a sword *bought* this month
toward a pillage the game resolves before the market opens. It is also unnecessary:
**the early picture already half-exists.** `apply_gifts_of_men` (`orders/semantics.rs:1476`, shipped
by `ah-z73s.2`) walks the hex's GIVE and TAKE orders and keeps a per-unit `Working` (`≈:1363`)
carrying `men`, `skills` and a running `held` map seeded from the whole item list
(`seed_working`, `≈:1373`) — and then publishes only the skills. Widening that walk from "gifts of
men" to "gifts of anything" and publishing what it already knows gives the early picture **before
`ledger_for` is ever called**, so the cycle that mockup called out is never met:
`region_wages` → `combat_ready_in` → `hex_facts` reads a projection that already exists.

The late picture is the ledger's `balance` (`orders/semantics.rs:1786`), which is what each unit
would hold once its orders had run, and it is complete at exactly the two moments the late terms are
priced: inside `charge_upkeep` (`:2066`, the last thing `ledger_for` does) and in `forecast_hex`,
which is handed the finished ledger.

**Blocker.** `ah-dxfd.1` — *The MEN column ignores men bought, taken or given as a whole class* — is
planned and merging. It states the rule *a headcount is the unit's man-tagged items, counted* once,
as `report::composition::men_in`, teaches `effects.rs` and the ledger to resolve `ALL MEN` and
`ALL ITEMS`, and teaches `apply_gifts_of_men` to stop doubting a TAKE of men and a gift of those two
classes. **This bead is written against the code that leaves, not against that plan** — read
`men_in`, `apply_gifts_of_men` and `classify_gift` as they actually stand.

`ah-3sp7` — *Giving a whole class of goods leaves a unit's silver and items uncounted* — is the
follow-up that teaches the scraper the remaining item classes. It is filed, unranked, and **this bead
does not wait for it**: everything here asks the catalogue which class an item is and falls back
where it cannot say, so it gets a better answer the day that bead lands, with no change here.

When this lands: a unit's tax, pillage and CAST arithmetic sees the men and goods this month's gifts
and takes move; its study, production, wages, entertaining and maintenance see the market as well;
and a hex where a transfer cannot be followed at all says so instead of quietly guessing.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` — the early projection

`apply_gifts_of_men` is **renamed `apply_transfers`** and follows every item tag rather than only the
man tags. Its doc comment must stop saying "gifts of men"; it is now the one walk that answers *what
does this unit hold, and who is in it, once this month's GIVE and TAKE orders have run*.

The change to the loop body is narrow, because the machinery is already there:

- `classify_gift` (`≈:1402`) today answers `NotMen` / `PeopleClass` / `Men(tag)` from a `&Selector`
  and the ruleset. It becomes a **tag-set** question — *which of this holder's tags does this
  transfer move?* — so it needs the holder's running `held` map and the `Amount` as well:
  `fn moves(held: &BTreeMap<String, ItemAmount>, what: &Selector, amount: &Amount, ruleset: &Ruleset) -> Moves`.
  Rename it with the shape, since it no longer classifies a gift but resolves one:
  ```rust
  /// Which of a holder's tags one transfer moves, and whether this walk can say at all.
  enum Moves {
      /// Every tag listed, in the holder's own item order.
      Tags(Vec<String>),
      /// A class the catalogue cannot classify (`rules/give` lists fifteen; `ah-3sp7` is the bead
      /// that teaches the scraper the rest), so what leaves the holder is not knowable.
      Unknowable,
  }
  ```
  - `Selector::Item(text)` → the one tag `ruleset.find_item(text)` resolves, uppercased; an
    unresolvable name is `Unknowable`.
  - `Selector::Class(name)` with `Amount::All { except: 0 }` → `MAN`/`MEN` is the holder's tags
    filtered by `Ruleset::is_man` (`movement/rules.rs:903`); `ITEM`/`ITEMS` is every tag the holder
    has, **silver included**, per `rules/give`'s *"the combination of all of the previous
    categories"*. Every other class is `Unknowable`.
  - `Selector::Class(_)` with any other `Amount` → `Unknowable`. `rules/give` gives `EXCEPT` and a
    stated quantity to the named-item forms alone.
  - `Selector::WholeUnit` → `Tags(vec![])`. `rules/give`: it *"gives the entire unit to the specified
    unit's faction"* — a change of ownership, and nobody's holdings move.
- `Working` (`≈:1363`) keeps `men`, `skills` and `doubted`, and its `held` becomes
  `BTreeMap<String, ItemAmount>` keyed by the uppercased tag so the holder's own `name` survives —
  see *Known traps* on `add_item`. Add `items_unknowable: bool` beside `doubted`; the two are
  independent and must stay so (a receiver whose reported headcount is an estimate has unknowable
  *skills* and perfectly knowable *holdings*; a `GIVE … ALL MAGIC` is the reverse, since it moves no
  men).
- The per-tag body that today runs once for one man tag runs once per tag in `Moves::Tags`, in the
  holder's item order. **The skills merge and the `men` counter run only for a tag
  `ruleset.is_man` accepts** — everything else moves amounts and nothing more. `merge_skills`
  (`orders/effects.rs:926`) still runs **before** the men are added to the receiver's count, exactly
  as it does today (`≈:1596`).
- `Moves::Unknowable` sets `items_unknowable` on both parties it can name — the holder always, and
  the resolved receiver where `resolve_give_target` (`≈:1432`) gives one. A `TAKE` whose source is
  `GiveTarget::Nowhere` **and** whose amount is `Amount::All { .. }` sets it on the taker: the goods
  are real and how many is not. A `TAKE FROM <unshown> <n> <tag>` is followed optimistically, exactly
  as the ledger already does (`apply`'s Take arm, `:2380`).

The publish loop (`≈:1615`) gains a second field beside `skills_after_gifts`:

```rust
/// One unit's people and goods once this month's GIVE and TAKE orders have run.
///
/// The mirror of [`SkillsAfterGifts`] and deliberately independent of it: what arrives can be exact
/// while the skills it brings are not, and the holdings can be unknowable while the skills are
/// untouched.
enum HoldingsAfterGifts {
    /// Nothing moved into or out of this unit, so the report's own lists stand. The overwhelming
    /// majority of units, and the reason this is not a `Vec` on every `Ordered`.
    Unchanged,
    Moved(Holdings),
    /// A transfer this walk cannot follow touched this unit.
    Unknowable,
}

struct Holdings {
    /// The report's items with this month's transfers applied, **in the report's own order**, with
    /// tags the unit did not hold appended in the order they arrived. Order is load-bearing - see
    /// *Known traps*.
    items: Vec<ItemAmount>,
    /// Derived from `items` by `report::composition::men_in` (`ah-dxfd.1`), which is the one place
    /// the rule "a headcount is the man-tagged items, counted" is written. **Not** derived for a
    /// unit whose `men_estimated` is set: re-deriving from a list the catalogue cannot fully read is
    /// the guess `classify_unit` refuses to make, under another name. Such a unit keeps the
    /// parser's `men` and `men_by_race`.
    men: i64,
    men_by_race: Vec<ItemAmount>,
}
```

The publish rule mirrors the skills one exactly (`≈:1615-1623`): `Unknowable` where
`items_unknowable` is set, otherwise `Moved` **only if the projected item list differs from the
report's**, and `Unchanged` where it does not — a transfer of zero, or a `GIVE` to a party that
resolves nowhere, must leave the unit reading exactly as it did.

`Ordered` (`≈:1289`) gains `holdings_after_gifts: HoldingsAfterGifts`, defaulting to `Unchanged`
at both construction sites (`≈:1320`, `≈:1337`), with two accessors beside `skills()` (`≈:1687`):

```rust
/// The unit's people and goods after this month's transfers, or `None` where the report's own
/// lists stand - which is both "nothing moved" and "what moved cannot be followed". The two are
/// the same fallback on purpose: a surface that does not consult `holdings_unknown` behaves
/// exactly as it did before this bead.
fn after_gifts(&self) -> Option<&Holdings>;

/// Whether a transfer this walk cannot follow touched this unit.
fn holdings_unknown(&self) -> bool;
```

**One hex reader, so the two entry points cannot diverge.** `review_turn` reads a hex and runs the
walk (`:394-396`); `item_effects` reads one and does not (`:1995`). Extract the pair into one
function and call it from both:

```rust
/// A hex, with this month's transfers already projected onto its units. The only way a `Hex` is
/// built for pricing: `UnitFacts` reads the projection, so a hex built without it is silently
/// priced on the report.
fn hex_with_transfers<'a>(
    region: &'a ReportRegion,
    ordered: &'a OrderedUnits,
    formed: &'a [Formed],
    ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
) -> Hex<'a>;
```

`item_effects` reads only `ledger.movements` and `ledger.uncounted`, neither of which the projection
touches, so this changes no output there. It is done anyway because one call site each is a property
a reader can check and a comment asking two callers to stay in step is not.

### `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs` — one struct, two pictures

`UnitFacts`' existing `men`, `men_by_race`, `items` and `skills` keep their names and **become the
early picture**. Their doc comments must say so. Two fields are added:

```rust
