## User-facing decisions

The whole interview, including what was rejected. Mockups committed with this bead:
`docs/ui/ah-dxfd.2-when-each-number-is-priced.html`,
`docs/ui/ah-dxfd.2-classes-and-the-residue.html`, `docs/ui/ah-dxfd.2-warning-wording.html` and
`docs/ui/ah-dxfd.2-doubt-sentence.html`.

**1. How many pictures of a unit, and which orders read which.** The bead as filed implied one:
"post-gift figures everywhere". Looking the turn sequence up showed two, and showed that
`ah-dxfd.1` — which makes the MEN cell show men bought from the market — would otherwise leave a
recruiting unit's MEN cell reading 15 while its SILVER cell priced its WORK on 10, the two cells of
one row disagreeing in the opposite direction to the fault this family exists to remove.
**Chosen: both pictures**, with every term declaring which it takes.
*Rejected:* the early picture only, with a follow-up bead for the market. Smaller and it would have
shipped sooner, but it leaves the row contradicting itself and leaves food bought this month not
paying maintenance.

**2. Whether the item classes can be classified at all.** The first draft of this plan assumed they
could not, because `ItemKind` carries only `Man`/`Mount`/`Monster`/`Ship`/`Equipment`. **The
navigator refused that premise and was right.** `rules/give` names fifteen classes; the committed
data page states most of them in its own sentences — *"This is a type of armor."*, *"This is a
tool."*, *"This is a trade good."* — and `kind` plus the `weapon` block already carry five more. Only
NORMAL, ADVANCED, MAGIC and SPECIAL are stated nowhere. **Chosen: a bead of its own, `ah-3sp7`,
filed at P4 for triage**, with a note that the engine's own source
(https://github.com/rmstdope/Atlantis) is where the remaining four live and where the rest should be
checked before the mapping is settled.
*Rejected:* folding it into this bead — it reaches a TypeScript scraper, a regenerated
`ruleset.json` and a Rust struct on top of a bead that had just grown to carry both pictures.
*Rejected:* making this bead depend on it — the pillage regression this family was filed for would
then sit behind an unranked scraper bead.

**3. What happens when a transfer cannot be followed** — the four unclassifiable classes, and a
`TAKE … ALL` from a unit the hex does not show. **Chosen: the hex's combat-ready sum goes
unanswerable**, so the pillage warning goes quiet and a pillaging unit's SILVER cell shows `?` —
exactly what an estimated headcount in the hex already does.
*Rejected:* falling back to the report for that unit, which is today's behaviour. It leaves the total
as arithmetic on a figure the application has just declared uncountable, and leaves the giver's own
SILVER cell saying "cannot be counted" while the pillage warning counts it anyway.

**4. The pillage warning's own sentence.** Its number becomes a forecast, on a line that reads like a
statement of fact. **Chosen: leave it exactly as it is** — `cannot pillage here: needs {needed}
combat ready men, this region has {ready}{because}`, unchanged, and `because_clause` unchanged with
it. The warning only ever fires because a `PILLAGE` was ordered, so its number was always about the
month being written.
*Rejected:* *"…this region will have 9 once this month's gifts have run…"*, which grows the common
case — most pillage warnings involve no gift — with a clause about gifts that did not happen.
*Rejected:* *"…this region has 9 after this month's gifts (19 before)…"* only when the gifts moved
the figure, which is two shapes of one sentence and a "before" figure computed purely to be printed.
**No string in the Rust findings changes in this bead.**

**5. The sentence a pillaging unit reads when the hex cannot be added up.** This one corrects a
defect that already ships: `price_pillage` reuses `SilverDoubt::EstimatedMen` — *"This unit's
headcount is an estimate, so its month cannot be priced."* — under a comment claiming *"its sentence
stays true"*. It is not true today, because the sum goes unanswerable when **any** own unit in the
hex is a guess, so a pillager whose MEN cell reads a plain `12` can already be told its headcount is
an estimate. **Chosen: a new `SilverDoubt::UnknownCombatReady`, wired to
`"unknown-combat-ready"`, saying exactly:**

> The combat ready men in this region cannot be added up, so what a pillage earns cannot be said.

It is true of every cause, present and future, claims nothing about the unit reading it, and fixes
the shipping case as well as the one this bead adds.
*Rejected:* keeping `EstimatedMen`, which keeps a sentence the MEN cell two columns away contradicts,
and keeps a comment asserting the opposite of the code.
*Rejected:* *"Another of your units here cannot be counted, so the combat ready men in this region
cannot be added up."* — it follows the house pattern for a neighbour-caused doubt and points at what
the player can act on, but "cannot be counted" covers a guessed headcount and an unfollowable order
at once by being vague about which, and it would need rewording again the first time a third cause
appears.

**6. Not asked, and why.** No layout, control, heading or empty-state text is added or changed. The
cells whose contents change — SILVER and the pillage finding — already have their formatting and
their hover. The parent bead's interview settled that the SILVER cell gains **no** predicted marking
(it is already and only ever a forecast, so there is no reported value for an italic-brass mark to
contrast with) and that a merged skill level showing lower after gifted or bought men is shown
plainly with the ordinary `was:` hover; both are recorded in `ah-dxfd.1`'s plan and **must not be
re-opened**.

## Out of scope

- **The four classes the sources never state** — NORMAL, ADVANCED, MAGIC and SPECIAL — and the
  scraper work behind the other eleven. `ah-3sp7`. This bead asks the catalogue and falls back where
  it cannot say.
- **`UnitFacts::held`, the unit's opening silver.** It stays `ordered.holding(SILVER)`, the report's,
  because it is the *starting balance* the forecast is built on. Making it post-order would
  double-count every term in the column.
- **The units-table preview** (`orders/effects.rs`). `ah-dxfd.1` owns that walker; this bead does not
  touch it, and the two remain separate walks by decision.
- **`GIVE <unit> UNIT`.** `rules/give`: it *"gives the entire unit to the specified unit's faction"* —
  a change of ownership, and nobody's holdings move.
- **A gift to a foreign unit.** `resolve_give_target` answers `Nowhere` for `Party::Foreign`, so the
  giver keeps what it gave. That is today's behaviour and this bead does not change it — the rules
  allow it between Friendly factions, and modelling it needs a view of declared attitudes this
  application does not have.
- **Men or goods crossing a hex boundary.** Both walkers require the other party to be in the same
  region, which is what the game requires too.
- **`TRANSPORT` and `DISTRIBUTE`.** `ah-bxgs` is the bead for those, and they settle in a phase of
  their own after WORK.
- **Movement.** A unit that leaves the hex still contributes to its old hex's combat-ready sum here,
  exactly as it does today.
- **STUDY raising a level this month.** `rules/sequenceofevents` puts STUDY in the month-long phase
  and its result reaches next turn's report, so there is no late skills list.
- **`men_estimated` units.** They keep the parser's headcount in both pictures and its `~`, and both
  derivations skip them.

## Validation

```bash
cargo test -p atlantis-core
pnpm run check:fast
```

Then, in the running application — the checks only a person can make. Load any committed report; each
step is one edit to the orders and one look at the units table.

**Pick units with no combat skill for steps 1 and 2.** A unit with Combat 1 counts every one of its
men whatever it is carrying, so the weapons make no difference to it and nothing below would show.

1. **A gift of weapons alone.** In a hex whose region line states a `Tax base`, find two of your own
   units: one holding more weapons than men, one holding men and no weapon. Order the second to
   `PILLAGE` and read the warning — it fires, and its tail says the pillaging unit has no weapon it
   can wield. Now have the first unit `GIVE` the second enough weapons to arm it. **The warning
   disappears**, because the region's count now includes men that have weapons to hold. Before this
   bead the gift changed nothing.
2. **A gift of men without the weapons for them.** Take a unit holding men and two kinds of weapon —
   one it can wield and one needing a skill it has not got. Write `FORM 1` / `PILLAGE` / `END` and
   give the new unit half the men and only the weapons nobody here can wield. **The warning's number
   falls**, and its tail names the new unit's useless weapons by name. Before this bead the number
   did not move at all: the men the application forgot to give were the same men it forgot to take
   away, and the error cancelled.
3. **TAX is priced before the market.** Give a unit five men from a faction-mate and order it `TAX`.
   Its SILVER income rises by five men's worth. Now instead have it `BUY 5` of the men the market
   sells and `TAX`: its income does **not** rise, because the game taxes before the market opens.
   The MEN cell shows the bought men in both cases.
4. **WORK is priced after the market.** Same unit, `BUY 5 HUMN` and `WORK`. Its wage income **does**
   rise, and its MEN cell and its SILVER cell now agree about how many men it has.
5. **Maintenance sees the food.** Take a unit carrying `consuming` and no food, and give it grain from
   a faction-mate in the same hex. Its Upkeep row falls by what the grain covers.
6. **An unfollowable transfer.** In a hex where one unit pillages, have another write
   `GIVE <the pillager> ALL MAGIC`. The pillage warning **disappears**, and the pillager's SILVER cell
   shows `?` whose hover reads *"The combat ready men in this region cannot be added up, so what a
   pillage earns cannot be said."*
7. **And it is narrow.** Change that order to `GIVE <the pillager> ALL MEN`. The warning comes back
   and the men are counted — that class is resolvable, and this is the check that the change did not
   silence more than it should.

## Known traps

- **`.cerebro/traps.md` — never ask for an effect-level test in `packages/shared`.** Triggered, and
  already obeyed: the only TypeScript here is one entry in `unitTooltip.ts`, a pure module whose
  `say()` is a function of its arguments, tested by `unitTooltip.test.ts` with no rendering. Do not
  add a `*.test.tsx` for this.
- **`.cerebro/traps.md` — a persisted setting's old default must be migrated rather than clamped.**
  Not triggered: no setting is added or changed.
- **`.cerebro/traps.md` — WebKit's clipped-text driver, the smoke/PWA interaction, `vite preview
  --strictPort`.** Not triggered: no browser suite runs for this bead.
- **Never hand `combat_ready_in` a `LateHoldings`.** It is reached from `region_wages` (`:744`),
  which `ledger_for` calls at `:1951` **before** the intent loop that fills the balance — so a late
  picture does not exist there, and making one exist means reordering `ledger_for`, which is the
  cycle this whole design avoids. `combat_ready_in` passes `None` and reads only `readiness`, which
  is an early term. If a future term inside it needs the late picture, the answer is that the term
  is in the wrong place, not that the argument should be threaded.
- **Every line number here is a hint; the symbol is the fact.** `semantics.rs` is over 18,000 lines
  and `ah-dxfd.1` moves parts of it. Numbers were derived against `1724ecc`; find things by symbol.
- **`ah-dxfd.1` is the blocker and may still read `in_progress` when you start.** Read the code, not
  the bead: `composition::men_in`, the widened `classify_gift`, and whether `apply_gifts_of_men`
  still doubts a TAKE of men are all things this plan assumes it left behind, and all things a
  three-line grep settles.
- **`add_item` matches tags with `==` and `find_item` with `eq_ignore_ascii_case`
  (`effects.rs:1007-1017`, `:986-997`).** That is why `Working::held` becomes a map of `ItemAmount`
  rather than of amounts: carry the holder's own `name` and `tag` through, and never synthesise
  either.
- **The projection keeps the report's item order.** `because_clause`'s `nearest_miss` is chosen by
  `NearMiss::beats` (`:1958-1962`), which resolves a tie by keeping the first item seen — so an
  alphabetical `BTreeMap` order would change which item a warning names, silently, in a tie. Build
  `Holdings::items` by walking the report's list and appending arrivals, not by iterating the map.
- **The late holdings' order does not matter, and no consumer may come to depend on it.** They come
  off `Ledger::balance`, a `BTreeMap`, so they are alphabetical by tag. `own_food_pass` filters by
  tag, `plan_production` looks tags up, `pool_wants` reads only `men` — none of them cares. Say so in
  `LateHoldings::read`'s doc comment.
- **`charge_upkeep` mutates `balance` as it goes** (`:2107-2110`), subtracting each unit's charged
  upkeep. Build the `LateHoldings` **before** that loop, or later units are priced against a balance
  earlier ones have already drawn down. Silver only, so today it would not change an item list — but
  it is exactly the kind of thing that stops being true.
- **`balance_of` does not upper-case its tag, while `credit` and `charge` do** (`:2960-2966` against
  `:3246-3263`). `LateHoldings::read` walks `balance`'s own keys, which `credit`/`charge` wrote and
  which are therefore already upper-cased. Do not upper-case them again and do not build one from an
  item list.
- **`UnitFacts` is taken by value, destructured in `forecast_unit` (`:819`) and read again
  afterwards** (`:995`, `:1043`). That works only because every field the destructure binds is
  `Copy`, so it copies rather than moves and `facts` stays whole. `Option<LateFacts<'a>>` and
  `bool` keep it so; putting a `Vec` or a `String` on `UnitFacts` would break the destructure with
  a partial-move error reported two hundred lines away from its cause.
- **`Working` is two different structs.** `effects::Working` (`effects.rs:527`) is the preview
  walker; `semantics::Working` (`≈:1363`) is this walk's per-unit state. They are unrelated, and
  `ah-dxfd.1` and this bead touch one each.
- **The generated TypeScript is written by `cargo test`, not by a build step.** Run
  `cargo test -p atlantis-core` before `pnpm run check:fast`, or `scripts/checkGenerated.ts` fails on
  a union that has not been rewritten yet, and commit what the test wrote.
- **Combat 1 hides the whole weapon path.** `readiness` returns `ready = men` outright for a unit
  whose Combat is 1 or better, or which knows a damaging spell, without reading a single item
  (`:2010-2016`). A fixture built to test that goods are followed **must give its units no combat
  skill**, or it passes identically before and after this bead. The parent bead's mockup writes
  its example as `min(19, 35)` for a unit holding `COMB 1`; that is the picture the arithmetic
  makes, not the branch the code takes.
- **`Ledger::balance` is keyed by unit id**, so two report units sharing an id are conflated in
  the late picture. `forecast_hex` already documents the same conflation for its own by-id
  lookups (`:945-947`); this adds no new one, and `LateHoldings::read` should say so rather than
  leave the next reader to discover it.
- **`ITEM`/`ITEMS` includes silver.** `rules/give`: *"the combination of all of the previous
  categories"*. A unit giving `ALL ITEMS` gives its `SILV` away too, and the walk must move it. Do
  not filter silver out.
- **A class carries no `EXCEPT` and no quantity.** `rules/give` gives those to the named-item forms
  alone; the class form is `GIVE [unit] ALL [item class]` and nothing else. A class arriving with
  either is a shape the rules do not define — answer `Unknowable` rather than inventing a reading.

## Context

A unit given no month-long order spends the month doing nothing, and one unit is easy to miss among
many. This warns about it.

**The bead's premise is wrong, and it changes the work.** It says *"the orders crate has no notion of
order duration today — nothing in `crates/core/src/orders/` distinguishes a month-long order from an
instant one, so the classification is new work"*. It does. `Intent::MonthLong` exists for exactly
this (`intents.rs:57-61`, *"An order that takes the whole month"*), and `Ordered::is_busy`
(`semantics.rs:348-370`) already answers *"is this unit's month already spoken for"* — it is what
stops the teaching check offering a working unit as a spare teacher. So the classification is not new;
it is **one variant short** (`Intent::Teach`, deliberately excluded from `is_busy` because teaching is
itself the thing that check is about) and it is written as a `matches!` rather than an exhaustive
`match`, which is a documented footgun this bead should close rather than copy.

**The real difficulty is elsewhere, and the bead does not mention it.** The app does not model every
Atlantis order. `read_order` (`intents.rs:205-334`) yields an intent for about twenty keywords; the
grammar knows a hundred (`grammar.rs`), and `ASSASSINATE`, `STEAL`, `TRANSPORT`, `DISTRIBUTE`,
`SACRIFICE`, `ATTACK` and others parse fine but produce **no intent at all**. `read_intents` then
drops those lines silently (`intents.rs:170-180` pushes only on `Some`), and `UnitIntents` keeps no
record that a line was there. So today a unit ordered to assassinate is, to any check, indistinguishable
from a unit ordered to do nothing — and a naive version of this check would warn about every one of
them. That is why this bead has to record what it could not read before it can warn about what it
did read.

**Measured, not guessed.** Against the committed turn-71 template — orders written by the navigator
and accepted by the server — the agreed rule warns about **2 of 27 units**, 14451 and 13432, and both
have no orders at all. Both are parked cargo units. The navigator was shown that and chose to warn
about them anyway.

### The verification failed, and this plan is why

Merged, then failed verification on 2026-08-19 (at `origin/main` 1de7528). The navigator: *"Lots of
orders pass for month long, but should not: NAME, ADDRESS, ARMOR, ATTACK, …"* — examples, not a list.

**The defect was not the month-long set. It was the escape hatch below.** This plan said: stay silent
whenever a unit holds any line we could not read. Almost every non-month order in Atlantis yields no
intent — `NAME`, `ADDRESS`, `ARMOR`, `ATTACK` and forty-odd others — so *virtually any unit with any
order at all went silent*, and the forgotten unit this check exists to catch stayed silent with them.
The bead's own purpose, inverted, in the false-negative direction, which is the worse one: a warning
that never fires is invisible.

The fix is to classify at the **keyword** level rather than the intent level, from the rules
(https://atlantis-pbem.com/rules) rather than from what the app happens to model. Two passages settle
almost everything — the enumerated list of month-long orders, and the Sequence of Events, which puts
everything else under *Instant orders*. The navigator settled the two the page leaves open. That
shrinks the unjudgeable set from "most of the game" to "keywords the app does not recognise at all".

Two of this plan's original premises were wrong and are corrected below:

- **`ASSASSINATE` is not a full-month order.** The rules are explicit: *"STEAL and ASSASSINATE are not
  full month orders, and do not interfere with other activities."* It was this plan's worked example
  of an unread month-spender, and it was pinned in a test.
- **`CAST` is not a full-month order.** *"a CAST order is not a full month order; a mage may still
  MOVE, STUDY, or use any other month long order."* `spends_the_month` returned true for it.

Branch from `origin/main`; every line number here is that tree.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### 1. Record the lines that could not be read — `crates/core/src/orders/intents.rs`

`UnitIntents` (`:116-121`) gains one field:

```rust
pub struct UnitIntents {
    pub unit_id: String,
    /// The `unit NNNN` line that opened the block.
    pub line: usize,
    pub intents: Vec<PlacedIntent>,
    /// Order lines whose keyword this reader does not recognise at all, and `TURN` blocks that
    /// were skipped.
    ///
    /// **Not "every line that yielded no intent"** - that was ah-dwk6's first design and it failed
    /// verification. The app turns about twenty keywords into intents; the rules describe about
    /// sixty, and most of the rest leave the month free (`NAME`, `ADDRESS`, `ARMOR`, `ATTACK` …).
    /// Treating all of them as unknown made a unit holding any order at all unjudgeable, so the
    /// idle-unit check went silent on exactly the units it exists to find.
    ///
    /// So a line is unread only when its keyword is in neither `FREE_ORDERS` nor the month-long
    /// set - which after this bead means a typo, or an order the game has and this list has not.
    /// Carries line numbers so a message could name one; nothing does yet.
    pub unread: Vec<usize>,
}
```

Populated in `read_intents` (`:145-201`), in the `Event::Order` arm at `:161-180`: where `read_order`
returns `None` **and the command is not in `FREE_ORDERS`**, push `line.number` onto `unread`. A
recognised free order is dropped exactly as today — it yields no intent because no check reads it,
and that is not the same as not being understood. **Also** push the line of a `TURN` block opened at
this depth — next month's orders are machinery this reader deliberately skips (`:139-143`). A `FORM`
block is **not** unread: it already yields `Intent::Form` (`:181-198`), and the orders inside it
belong to a unit that does not exist yet.

Every existing construction of `UnitIntents` must gain the field; there is exactly one, at `:158-162`.

**The list itself**, beside `read_order` in `intents.rs`, taken from the rules and from the
navigator's two rulings on the cases the page leaves open:

```rust
