## Context

An order's item argument is resolved three different ways, by three functions that search different
places, so a **legal spelling of a legal order can move men in one column of a row and not in
another**. Measured by reading `origin/main` at `ae426d8`, on a hex holding `8 orcs [ORC]` and
`8 leaders [LEAD]`:

| the order a player writes | units table | `early_men` | ledger |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GIVE 901 ALL ORCS` - the report's own plural | moves | moves | moves |
| `GIVE 901 ALL LEAD` - the tag | moves | moves | moves |
| `GIVE 901 ALL LEADER` - **the catalogue's singular** | **nothing** | moves | moves |
| `GIVE 901 ALL wood_elves` - **the report's plural of a multi-word race** | moves | **nothing** | moves |

Why, exactly:

- `effects::find_item` (`crates/core/src/orders/effects.rs:1451`) searches **the holder's own
  inventory only**, by tag and by the name the *report* prints - which for men is a plural,
  `leaders`. `LEADER` matches neither `LEAD` nor `leaders`, so the units table does nothing.
- `Ruleset::find_item` (`crates/core/src/movement/rules.rs:1214`) searches **the catalogue only**,
  where the name is singular, `wood elf`. `item_spellings` strips a trailing `s`/`es`, and
  `wood elves` strips to `wood elv`, so `moves()` (`semantics.rs:1659`) answers `Unknowable` and
  `early_men`/`early_items` fall back to the report's own figures.
- `semantics::resolve_item` (`semantics.rs:4699`) tries the catalogue and then walks the hex's
  inventories, so it is the union of the other two and gets both right.

`resolve_item` has a fault of its own that no catalogue exposes today, recorded in its own doc
comment (`semantics.rs:4725`): its inventory walk is **item-major** - each item is asked about every
spelling before the next item is reached - where `item_spellings` requires **spelling-major**, every
spelling tried across everything before the next spelling. A holder carrying both `pearl` and
`pearls` resolves `pearls` to the wrong one.

The fix is one resolver that searches the catalogue **and** the item lists in front of it, spelling
by spelling, with all three callers using it.

**This is the spelling half of `ah-vcp8`.** Its sibling `ah-vcp8.2` fixes what a GIVE's *target*
means, and depends on this bead so the two do not collide in `effects::give` and
`semantics::transfer`. Nothing about the target is this bead's business: a `GIVE` to a unit that is
not ours keeps every behaviour it has today.

**Already fixed elsewhere and not here:** `item_spellings` stripping its suffixes case-sensitively,
so `ALL ORCS` in the case the report itself prints tags in resolved nowhere. `ah-qct4` landed that
as `#790`.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### `crates/core/src/movement/rules.rs` - one spelling, one catalogue lookup

`Ruleset::find_item` (`:1214`) loops the spellings itself, which is exactly what a resolver
interleaving the catalogue with an inventory cannot do. Split its inner half out and leave the outer
half calling it, so the match test is written once:

```rust
impl Ruleset {
    /// The catalogue entry one **exact** spelling names - no plural rule and no underscores, both
    /// of which the caller has already applied.
    ///
    /// The per-spelling half of [`Ruleset::find_item`], separate so a resolver that must
    /// interleave the catalogue with a unit's own inventory can try one spelling against both
    /// before moving to the next (`orders::items::item_named`). Written once so the two cannot
    /// come to disagree about what "names this item" means.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn item_spelled(&self, spelling: &str) -> Option<&ItemEntry> {
        self.items.values().find(|item| {
            item.tag.eq_ignore_ascii_case(spelling) || item.name.eq_ignore_ascii_case(spelling)
        })
    }
}
```

`find_item` then becomes that call inside its existing `item_spellings` loop, and keeps its
signature, its doc comment and all fourteen of its other callers
(`movement/mode.rs:129,295,338,373`, `rules.rs:1130`, `orders/grammar.rs:750`, and the tests at
`rules.rs:1460-1539`). **Do not delete `find_item`**: those callers want the entry from the
catalogue and nothing else.

### `crates/core/src/orders/items.rs` - new module, the one resolver

A module of its own rather than a function bolted onto `rules.rs`: this reads a *report's*
inventories as well as the ruleset, and `movement/rules.rs`'s own header says it is "the movement
ruleset, as scraped from a game's own rules and data pages". Add `pub mod items;` to
`crates/core/src/orders/mod.rs`, in the existing alphabetical list between `intents` and `lexer`.

```rust
//! Resolving an order's item argument to a canonical tag.
//!
//! One function, because three of them disagreed: the units-table preview searched a holder's
//! inventory, the transfer walk searched the catalogue, and the ledger searched both - so a legal
//! spelling of a legal order moved men in one column of a row and not another (`ah-vcp8.1`).

use crate::movement::rules::{item_spellings, Ruleset};
use crate::report::model::ItemAmount;

/// The canonical, upper-cased tag an order's item argument names.
///
/// Searches the catalogue and every item list the caller can see, **spelling-major**: each
/// spelling [`item_spellings`] offers is tried against the catalogue and then against everything
/// `seen` yields, and only when none of them answers is the next spelling tried. That order is
/// `item_spellings`' own requirement - an entry whose name ends in `s` must not be beaten by
/// another entry matching its stripped form - and honouring it in one place is the whole point of
/// this function.
///
/// The catalogue answers first within a spelling, because its tag is the canonical one; a report's
/// inventory carries the same tags, so where both answer they answer the same thing. An item the
/// catalogue does not carry is still found in a holding, which is how an advanced item nobody has
/// scraped still resolves.
///
/// `seen` is a closure rather than a slice because each caller has a different shape of thing to
/// walk - one unit's list, a `BTreeMap`'s values, or a chain across a hex - and it is called once
/// per spelling, at most three times.
///
/// `None` where nothing answers, and for an empty argument.
#[must_use]
pub fn item_named<'a, I: Iterator<Item = &'a ItemAmount>>(
    ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
    text: &str,
    seen: impl Fn() -> I,
) -> Option<String> {
    let written = text.replace('_', " ");
    item_spellings(&written)
        .into_iter()
        .flatten()
        .find_map(|spelling| {
            ruleset
                .and_then(|ruleset| ruleset.item_spelled(spelling))
                .map(|entry| entry.tag.clone())
                .or_else(|| {
                    seen()
                        .find(|item| {
                            item.tag.eq_ignore_ascii_case(spelling)
                                || item.name.eq_ignore_ascii_case(spelling)
                        })
                        .map(|item| item.tag.clone())
                })
        })
        .map(|tag| tag.to_ascii_uppercase())
}
```

`item_spellings` is already `pub` (`rules.rs:375`) and `Ruleset::items` is a `pub BTreeMap`
(`rules.rs:694`), so nothing else needs opening up.

### `crates/core/src/orders/effects.rs` - the units-table preview

`find_item` (`:1451`) gains the catalogue:

```rust
/// The index of the item this text names, accepting the same spellings the game does.
///
/// The catalogue as well as the holding, through the one resolver every surface uses
/// (`orders::items::item_named`): a player may write `LEADER`, which the catalogue knows and a
/// unit's own `8 leaders [LEAD]` matches neither way round (`ah-vcp8.1`).
fn find_item(ruleset: &Ruleset, items: &[ItemAmount], text: &str) -> Option<usize> {
    let tag = item_named(Some(ruleset), text, || items.iter())?;
    items
        .iter()
        .position(|item| item.tag.eq_ignore_ascii_case(&tag))
}
```

A tag the catalogue knows but this unit does not hold still answers `None` at the `position`, which
is what it answered before - so nothing that was silent becomes noisy.

Both call sites pass `&self.ruleset` (an `Arc<Ruleset>`, which derefs):

- `:1274`, inside `tags_moved`'s `Selector::Item` arm - the one that matters, since it takes the
  order's own text.
- `:1216`, inside `apply_transports` - called with an already-resolved tag, so its answer cannot
  change; it goes through the same function and needs the argument.

### `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` - the transfer walk and the ledger

**`moves()` (`:1649`)**, whose `Selector::Item` arm is the `early_men`/`early_items` half:

```rust
Selector::Item(text) => match item_named(Some(ruleset), text, || held.values()) {
    Some(tag) => Moves::Tags(vec![tag]),
    None => Moves::Unknowable,
},
```

`held` is the `&BTreeMap<String, ItemAmount>` the function already takes, and `values()` yields
