## Context

A region's `Products` line states how much of each resource the hex yields this month, and every
unit producing there shares it. `rules/tableiteminfo`, verbatim: an item that lists no raw material
*"may be produced directly from the land … Shown on the description of a region is a list of the
items that can be produced, and the amount of each that can be produced per month … If the units in
a region attempt to produce more of a commodity than can be produced that month, then the amount
available is distributed among the producers"*. The rules stop there and never state the division.

`rules/sequenceofevents` separates the two kinds of `PRODUCE` outright — *"Manufacturing PRODUCE
orders (those that produce items from other items, such as using the weaponsmith skill to make
swords out of iron)"* run in one phase, *"Primary PRODUCE orders (those that produce items from
region resources, such as using the mining skill to produce iron)"* in a later one. **Only the
second draws on the hex.** In this ruleset the two are exactly disjoint: 19 recipes have no
material input (`IRON MITH ADMT WOOD IRWD YEW STON ROOT FUR FLOA FISH TURT HERB MUSH HORS CAME WING
GRAI LIVE`) and the other 34 all have one. `check_production` (`semantics.rs:6526`) already applies
that same test — `recipe.inputs.is_empty()` — before it says `produce-not-here`.

The app models no pool at all. `plan_production` (`silver.rs:3250`) caps a run by the unit's
workforce, its silver and its materials, and by nothing else. `ah-vtwn` (closed, merged as #792)
fixed *how much one unit's men can make* and deliberately left this alone, so the preview now
overstates every primary production in the game.

**The corpus settles the division, and it is `split_pool`.** `mountain (36,4)` in the committed
turn-42 report (`tests/fixtures/reports/neworigins-3.0.0-g3-f42-t42.rep:1341`) states
`Products: 34 livestock [LIVE], 36 iron [IRON], 16 stone [STON], 9 mithril [MITH], 6 admantium
[ADMT]`, and six own units produce there. Dividing each figure in proportion to what each unit asked
reproduces **all six** of the report's own `Produces` lines exactly:

| Unit | Line | Workforce | Asks | Pool | `split_pool` | Report says |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Miners (1795) | :1362 | 8 orcs, mining 3, 8 picks | 32 | 36 iron | 16 | 16 (:512) |
| MinersA (5105) | :1456 | 8 orcs, mining 5 | 40 | *(shared)* | 20 | 20 (:514) |
| Stoneworkers (2693) | :1365 | 10 orcs, quarrying 2 | 20 | 16 stone | 16 | 16 (:516) |
| Farmers (3493) | :1372 | 17 orcs, farming 2 | 34 | 34 livestock | 34 | 34 (:510) |
| MinersM (3826) | :1379 | 2 orcs, mining 4, 2 picks | 10 | 9 mithril | 9 | 9 (:518) |
| MinersA (7671) | :1403 | 2 orcs, mining 5 | 10 | 6 admantium | 6 | 6 (:520) |

Three things fall out of that table and each one is a decision this plan then makes:

- **A lone producer is capped too.** Stone, mithril and admantium each have one producer, and each
  is cut to the hex's figure. This is the *market* precedent (`ah-t2pn.3` settles a line for a lone
  trader, which is what caps `BUY 200` where 100 exist), **not** the tax precedent, where one
  claimant is uncontended.
- **A pool that covers everybody divides nothing.** Farmers asks 34 of 34 and keeps 34, which is
  `split_pool`'s own `total <= pool` arm.
- **The pick bonus is inside the asks and is load-bearing.** Without it Miners asks 24, and 24
  against 40 gives 13 rather than the 16 the report states. `ah-vtwn`'s `min(men, tools)` reading is
  confirmed by this data as much as the split is.

Every one of the 19 land recipes is `man_months: 1, outputs: 1`, so "proportional to the ask in
items" and "proportional to man-months" are the same number here. The ask is nevertheless taken in
**items** (`ProductionPlan::wanted`), because that is the unit the pool is stated in.

**Read `docs/ui/ah-256d-region-resource-pool.html`** (PR #798) before starting. It records the six
decisions the navigator took, each with the variants that were rejected and why, and it carries the
table above.

