## User-facing decisions

The whole interview, `docs/ui/ah-256d-region-resource-pool.html` (PR #798), navigator, 2026-08-29.
Two rounds; the second was opened by what the first left undecided.

**Round 1, question 1 — does contention raise a Problems entry?** Offered A (the cap is quiet: the
ITEMS figure and its hover carry the whole story, nothing in the Problems panel), B (reuse
`region-pool-oversubscribed`, the toggle already covering tax, wages, entertainment and markets),
and C (the same entries under a new toggle of their own). **Chose A.** B was rejected because a busy
mining hex is oversubscribed on purpose — the mockup's own example produces five entries from one
ordinary hex, every turn, for something the player did deliberately — and because that toggle is
all-or-nothing with the tax, wage and market warnings, which *are* mistakes. C was rejected as a new
code, a new Settings row and a new piece of vocabulary bought for a fact the figure and its hover
already carry. **Consequence for the implementation: no new `codes::` entry, no new `ContendedPool`
variant, no `PoolOverrun`, no change to `SettingsDialog`'s warning list, and `production_shares_for`
takes no overruns argument.**

**Round 1, question 2 — the cap sentence.** Offered W1 (`This region has iron for 20, not the 40 its
skill and tools could make.`), W2 (`This unit takes 20 of the region's 36 iron, not the 40 …`) and
W3 (`Your units here produce 72 iron between them and this region has 36, so this unit takes 20 of
the 40 …`). **Chose W1.** W2 was rejected because a lone producer exhausting a hex reads as "16 of
the region's 16 stone", which is both true and odd; W3 because its contended form runs to about 130
characters where the other three reasons run to about 70. W1's cost was named and accepted: it never
says *why* 20, so a small hex and a crowded one read identically.

**Round 1, question 3 — a hex that yields none of the item.** Offered 0 (the same arithmetic with a
pool of nothing) and leaving it uncapped as today. **Chose 0.**

**Round 1, question 4 — foreign factions.** Only our own units are visible, so every figure is an
upper bound. Offered saying nothing, saying it when a foreign unit stands in the hex, and saying it
always. **Chose to say nothing**: the whole preview is a forecast and no other figure in it carries
a caveat.

**Round 2, question 1 — what the hover says when the hex yields none of it.** Opened because
question 3 above settled the number and not the words, and there is no `Products` entry to take the
noun from. Offered Z1 (`This region has no floater hide, not the 10 …`, the catalogue's word, as the
`produce-not-here` message beside it uses), Z2 (`This region produces none of it, not the 10 …`) and
