## Known traps

- **`crates/core/tests/silver_agrees_with_the_warning.rs` is the net under this whole bead.** The
  SILVER column and the ITEMS ledger price the same order twice through arms that share only their
  inputs, and they have drifted before (`ah-abwx`, PILLAGE). Give both call sites their share
  through `region_share_of` and nothing else.
- **The settlement must read the *early* picture in both passes, and that is not the picture the
  SILVER column prices with.** `ledger_for` runs at `semantics.rs:405` and `forecast_hex` at `:433`,
  with `apply_recruits` between them — so anything the settlement reads must be untouched by
  `apply_recruits`. It writes `skills_after_gifts` and `men_after_orders` and nothing else, so
  `early_men()`, `unit.skills` and `early_items()` are all safe and are what the ask must be built
  from. **Do not "improve" it to `actor.skills()` or to `facts.late().men`**: `actor.skills()` is
  the gift-merged list `apply_recruits` rewrites, and `facts.late().men` exists only on the SILVER
  side. The share is a cap, so the SILVER column pricing a slightly larger `wanted` against the same
  share is correct and expected.
- **`market_shares_for` is computed twice per hex, once in each pass, and so is this one.** The
  comment at `semantics.rs:2687` says why that is accepted rather than overlooked. Put
  `production_shares_for` at exactly the two sites `market_shares_for` occupies and nowhere else; a
  third call site is a third answer.
- **A lone producer is capped, and a lone taxer is not.** `pool_shares_for` skips a pool with fewer
  than two claimants; `market_shares_for` settles for one. Production follows the **market**, and
  the corpus is why: Stoneworkers, MinersM and MinersA (7671) are each alone in their resource and
  each is cut to the hex's figure. Copying the tax rule here would leave half the defect in place
  and every test above would still pass except those three.
- **`ProductionCap` and `UnitSilver` are `ts_rs`-exported under `cfg(test)`**, so
  `packages/core-client/src/generated/ProductionCap.ts` and `UnitSilver.ts` are regenerated by
  `cargo test` and must be committed. `crates/core/tests/generated_ts.rs` is what fails if they are
  not.
- **The region's word is not the catalogue's.** `HORS`, `HERB`, `FLOA`, `TURT`, `MUSH`, `WING` and
  `CAME` all differ — the `Products` line writes `horses`, `herbs`, `floater hides`, and the
  catalogue writes `horse`, `herb`, `floater hide`. The sentence uses the region's. Do not reach for
  `Plurals`: it is built only from items some unit in the report holds more than one of, so it is
  absent for exactly the resources nobody is carrying.
- **Only a recipe with no material inputs draws on the pool.** `recipe.inputs.is_empty()`, the same
  test `check_production` (`semantics.rs:6526`) already applies, with its comment explaining why: a
  sword is made *from* iron and can be made wherever there is iron to hand. Keying on the `Products`
  line alone would be equivalent in this ruleset and is not the rule; keying on the item's class
  would not be.
- **`bd show <id> --json` returns an array**, and `dependencies` uses `dependency_type`. Irrelevant
  to the code, relevant to reading this bead back.
- From `.cerebro/traps.md`: **never ask for an effect-level test in `packages/shared`.** The only
  TypeScript change here is `unitTooltip.ts`, a pure module with a plain `.test.ts`, so this does
  not bite — but do not "improve" the coverage by reaching for a `.test.tsx` that observes an
  effect.
- From `.cerebro/traps.md`: **the smoke suite rebuilds the web bundle with the service worker
  disabled**, so a `test:pwa` run straight after `test:smoke` fails with timeouts that look like a
  broken worker. This bead runs neither by default; if you run `test:smoke` by choice, run it last.

## Context

Found by the navigator during Psylocke's verification of `ah-1mpx.3` on 2026-08-28, at
`origin/main` 937a1e9, and filed at P1.

The units pane's filter box (`UnitTableDock.tsx:556-562`, `aria-label="Filter units"`, placeholder
`filter units…`) is plain component state that **nothing ever resets**. `ah-1mpx.2` gave the pane a
source rail down its left edge — *This hex*, *All my units*, then each Army — so the list under that
box can now be replaced entirely without the box noticing. Filter *All my units* down to one unit to
find it, then click the Army you were building: the Army looks empty, because a filter you have
stopped thinking about is still narrowing a list it was never typed for. The rail's own count beside
the Army disagrees with the table, and the hint reads `— Northern Host, 12 units, 0 shown` — those
are the two tells, and only if you know to look for them.

**What lands.** One rule, in one place: *the filter box empties whenever the table becomes a list of
something else.* Which changes count as "something else" was settled with the navigator across two
rounds and is written out in full in *User-facing decisions*; the short form is the rail, the hex
while *This hex* is the source, and the fallback when the Army on screen is deleted — and **not** a
new turn's report, **not** a hex click while an Army is the source, and **not** re-clicking the entry
you are already on.

The load-bearing implementation fact, which is why this is not a one-line `useEffect`: **the rail
hands back a brand-new object every time an Army entry is clicked** (`UnitSourceRail.tsx:81`,
`onSource({ kind: "army", armyId: army.id })`), while the two built-in entries hand back the module
constants `HEX_SOURCE` and `OWN_SOURCE` (`:56`, `:63`). So object identity says "the source changed"
for a re-click of the Army you are already reading and "nothing changed" for a re-click of *This
hex*. An effect keyed on `[source]` would therefore clear the box on one of those and not the other,
which is the asymmetry the navigator explicitly asked to have removed.

**Not blocked, and blocking nothing.** `ah-y9hx` (P2, *Clicking a unit under All my units does not
travel the map to its hex*) would make a unit click move the selected hex. That is safe against this
design by construction — the hex only enters the rule while *This hex* is the source, and under
*All my units* it does not — but it is worth knowing the two beads meet.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

Two files changed, two test files added to. Nothing new is created.

### 1. `packages/shared/src/workspace/unitSource.ts` — one new export

This module is already "the whole vocabulary of *which list is the dock showing*, and every rule that
depends on it", kept pure precisely so it can be tested (its own header comment says so). The new
rule belongs here and nowhere else — **`packages/shared` has no jsdom**, so a rule that lives in the
component is a rule nothing in this package can pin (see *Known traps*).

Add, after `sourceStillThere` (which ends at line 41):

```ts
/**
 * What the table is a list *of* right now. Two calls that answer the same string are showing the
 * same list; any other pair means the list underneath has been replaced.
 *
 * The hex enters the answer for `This hex` **and only for `This hex`**, which is the whole of the
 * asymmetry the caller needs: clicking (7,51) while `This hex` is the source replaces every row,
 * and clicking it while an Army is the source replaces nothing at all - an Army is not about the
 * hex, which `ah-1mpx.2` settled deliberately.
 *
 * Deliberately a function of the source and the hex alone. It cannot see the report, so a new
 * turn's report arriving cannot change it - which is exactly the answer the navigator chose for
 * that case, held in place by the signature rather than by a test.
 *
 * `regionId` is null when no hex is selected, and that is a list of its own: `hex:` and
 * `hex:1:7,53` differ, so selecting the first hex of a session replaces "nothing" with a list.
 */
export function listShown(source: UnitSource, regionId: string | null): string {
  switch (source.kind) {
    case "hex":
      return `hex:${regionId ?? ""}`;
    case "own":
      return "own";
    case "army":
      return `army:${source.armyId}`;
  }
}
```

The three arms carry distinct prefixes, so no region id and no Army id can collide across kinds.

Reuse `sameSource` (`:22-27`)? **No — and this is deliberate.** `sameSource` answers "are these two
sources the same", which is a different question from "is this the same list": it cannot see the hex,
so it would call `This hex`@(7,53) and `This hex`@(7,51) the same, and the navigator asked for those
to differ. `sameSource` stays exactly as it is, used by `UnitSourceRail.tsx:80` and
`sourceStillThere`; do not change it and do not route this rule through it.

### 2. `packages/shared/src/workspace/UnitTableDock.tsx` — the wiring

`filter` is `useState("")` at line 202 and `setFilter` is called in exactly one place today,
line 559, from the input's `onChange`. `source` is `useState<UnitSource>(initialSource)` at line 209.
`regionId` is already derived at line 228: `const regionId = hex?.regionId ?? null;`.

**Insert immediately after line 228**, above the `armyIds` memo at line 230:

```ts
