## Known traps

- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom, by decision (`ah-nass`).** Its component tests render with
  `renderToStaticMarkup`, which runs no effects, attaches no refs, fires no timers and never
  re-renders. A `*.test.tsx` here that clicks a rail entry and expects the filter box to have
  changed is a test the harness cannot run — four beads paid about half an hour each discovering
  that. The rule goes in `unitSource.ts` and is tested there; the wiring is proved by the smoke
  suite. `packages/shared/src/testing/README.md` has the pattern.
- **Do not reach for `key={...}` to force a reset.** Putting `key={listShown(source, regionId)}` on
  `<UnitTableDock>` in `AppShell.tsx:3819` would look like a one-line fix and would throw away the
  sort, the scroll position, the column-drag refs, the hover tooltip state and the rail's edit mode
  along with the filter. `AppShell` deliberately puts no key there.
- **Do not key the rule on `source` identity.** `UnitSourceRail.tsx:81` builds a fresh
  `{ kind: "army", armyId }` on every click, while `:56` and `:63` hand back the module constants
  `HEX_SOURCE` and `OWN_SOURCE`. `useEffect(() => setFilter(""), [source])` therefore clears on a
  re-click of an Army and not on a re-click of *This hex*, which is the asymmetry Q3 exists to
  remove. The smoke walk's last three lines are the guard against this being reintroduced.
- **An effect would paint the wrong picture once.** A passive effect runs after the commit, so the
  new list gets one frame narrowed by the old text before being corrected — on a virtualised table
  filtered down to nothing, that frame reads as "No unit matches that filter." flashing over a list
  that is about to be full. The render-phase adjustment is React's own documented answer and costs
  no paint.
- **WebKit's driver answers `""` for text it considers clipped**, so a Chromium assertion can pass
  while the native shell shows nothing. Not expected to bite here — the assertions are on an input's
  `value` and on a pane's text, neither of which is clipped — but `native` is the job that would say
  otherwise.
- **The smoke suite rebuilds the web bundle with the service worker disabled**, so a `test:pwa` run
  straight after `test:smoke` fails with timeouts that look like a broken worker. Not needed for
  this bead; if it is run, run it on its own.
- **`packages/shared/src/unitTable.ts` is detected as binary by `grep`** — use `grep -a` if
  `filterUnits` needs looking at. It does not need changing.

## Context

A unit whose block names the same goods on two `BUY` lines is shown buying them **twice**, and
claims twice its share of the market line while doing it. Both halves have been true since the
market settlement landed, and one running total closes both — the buying-side twin of `ah-vw8e`,
which fixes exactly this shape on the selling side and deliberately scoped this out.

**The unit buys goods that do not exist.** A merchant holding 100 silver, against a `For Sale` line
of *5 grain at $12*, writing `BUY ALL grain` twice, is shown buying **8 grain** and spending **96
silver**. Neither surface takes what the first line bought out of what the second may buy:

- The **ledger** defers both lines into `Ledger::buy_all` and settles them in `settle_buy_all`
  (`crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs:3552`), calling `price_buy_all` with
  `deferred.available` — this unit's *whole* settled share — each time. Only the silver balance
  falls between the two calls, so a rich unit takes its whole share twice.
- The **SILVER column** does the same in `forecast_unit`'s deferred pass
  (`crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs:1301`), against a `Deferred::BuyAll { available, .. }`
  captured once per line (`silver.rs:1199`).
- The **exact form** has the same fault without needing the deferred pass: `buy`
  (`semantics.rs:3525`) and `forecast_unit`'s `Amount::Exact` arm (`silver.rs:1184`) each resolve
  `allowed` from `share_of` / `market_share` per line, so `BUY 5 grain` twice against a share of 5
  buys 10.

A shipped test pins the wrong behaviour outright:
`two_buy_all_orders_spend_in_document_order` (`silver.rs:5519`) asserts `expense == Some(96)` and
`at_month_end == Some(4)` for two `BUY ALL grain` against a line of 5, and its comment — *"100 buys
five at 12 up to the market's 5, leaving 40; the second buys three more"* — states the defect as
though it were the design.

**The unit claims the line twice.** `market_shares_for` (`semantics.rs:650`) answers the buying side
with `Amount::All { .. } => pool` — *an unbounded order attempts to buy everything there is* — and
then sums a unit's claims per tag with `claims[index] = claims[index].saturating_add(want)`. Two such
lines claim `2 * pool`. Against a faction-mate buying the same goods, `split_pool` divides the line
in proportion to the claims, so the doubled unit takes two thirds where it should take a half; a
*lone* unit doing it pushes the line into a `region-pool-oversubscribed` finding it cannot deserve,
since it was never going to buy more than the line holds.

`rules/buy` is what settles the shape of the fix. It says a `BUY ALL` means *"the unit will attempt
to buy as many as it can afford"*, and that where demand exceeds supply *"the available items will be
split among the buyers in proportion to the amount each buyer attempted to buy"* — **per buyer**,
against one purse. A unit's two unbounded lines are one attempt against one purse and one line, not
two, so an unbounded claim is counted once per unit rather than once per line.

**What changes when this lands.** A block's `BUY` lines for one item are priced against a running
per-tag total, so a second line can only buy what the lines above it left — of the market line and of
this unit's settled share of it alike. The settlement counts an unbounded claim once per unit. A
`BUY` line that finds nothing left carries a new **`nothing-left-to-buy`** warning, on the exact form
as well as the unbounded one, and the `BUY ALL` hover names the unit's own earlier order instead of
blaming the market or a faction-mate that may not exist.

The chosen design, the two follow-up rounds and everything rejected are in
`docs/ui/ah-lauy-bought-twice.html`, `docs/ui/ah-lauy-round2-wording.html` and
`docs/ui/ah-lauy-round3-partial.html` (PR #734).

**`ah-vw8e` lands first and touches the same four places.** It is a blocker on this bead for that
reason, not because anything here needs its behaviour. Everything cited below is verified against
`main` as it stands *before* `ah-vw8e`; *Known traps* says precisely what that bead adds beside each
piece of this one.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### 1. `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs` — `price_buy_all` learns what the unit already bought

`price_buy_all` (`silver.rs:2425`) is called by **both** surfaces and stays that way. It gains a
fifth parameter and its `capped_by` gains a case.

```rust
/// Which limit settled a `BUY ALL`.
pub enum BuyAllCap {
    Silver,
    Market,
    Shared,
    /// This unit's own earlier `BUY` lines had already taken these goods, wholly or in part
    /// (`ah-lauy`). Wins over `Silver`, `Market` and `Shared` whenever it applies: it is the one
    /// cause the player can fix by deleting a line.
    AlreadyBought,
}
```

`BuyAllCap` derives `Serialize`/`Deserialize` with `#[serde(rename_all = "kebab-case")]` and
`ts_rs::TS` under `cfg(test)` (`silver.rs:2386`), so the new variant reaches TypeScript as
`"already-bought"` and regenerates `packages/core-client/src/generated/BuyAllCap.ts` on
`cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core`. Append it **last**, after `Shared`.

`BuyAllPlan` (`silver.rs:2403`) gains one field:

```rust
pub struct BuyAllPlan {
    pub bought: i64,
    pub affordable: i64,
    /// This unit's settled share of the line - what it could have had were it rich enough, and
    /// **not** reduced by its own earlier lines. `already_bought` carries that separately, because
    /// the sentence has to name the share the unit started with rather than what is left of it.
    pub available: i64,
    pub market_has: i64,
    /// How many of these goods this unit's own earlier `BUY` lines already took out of `available`
    /// this month (`ah-lauy`).
    pub already_bought: i64,
    pub capped_by: BuyAllCap,
}
```

and the function itself:

```rust
#[must_use]
pub fn price_buy_all(
    silver_available: i64,
    price: i64,
    // This unit's settled share of the line, or the whole line where nothing was settled. Never
    // reduced by this unit's own earlier lines - that is `already_bought`'s job.
    available: i64,
    market_has: i64,
    // How many of these goods this unit's own earlier `BUY` lines have already taken out of
    // `available` this month (`ah-lauy`).
    already_bought: i64,
) -> (Priced, BuyAllPlan) {
    let affordable = if price <= 0 { 0 } else { (silver_available / price).max(0) };
    let available = available.max(0);
    // What is left of this unit's share once its own earlier lines have had theirs.
    let left = (available - already_bought).max(0);
    let bought = affordable.min(left);
    let capped_by = if already_bought > 0 && (left == 0 || bought < affordable) {
        // The unit's own earlier line is what bit - either it emptied the share outright, or the
        // remainder stopped this line short of what its silver would have paid for. It wins even
        // the case where the purse is empty too (the navigator's round-2 Q3): "it holds 0 silver"
        // is true of every spent-up `BUY ALL` and hides the duplicate line, which is the one thing
        // the player can act on.
        BuyAllCap::AlreadyBought
    } else if bought < affordable {
        if available < market_has { BuyAllCap::Shared } else { BuyAllCap::Market }
    } else {
        BuyAllCap::Silver
    };
    (
        Priced { spends: bought.saturating_mul(price), ..Priced::default() },
        BuyAllPlan { bought, affordable, available, market_has, already_bought, capped_by },
    )
}
```

**Rust does not accept doc comments on function parameters** — the two annotations above are ordinary
`//` comments, as `forecast_hex` and `apply` already write theirs.

`already_bought == 0` reproduces today's three-way `capped_by` exactly, which is why every shipped
test of this function stays green with a `0` threaded through it.

