## Known traps

- **`ah-vw8e` lands first, in four of the same places.** It adds `sold: BTreeMap<(String, String), SoldSoFar>`
  and `dead_sales: Vec<DeadSale>` to `Ledger` — put `bought` and `dead_buys` beside them, and name all
  four in `ledger_for`'s initialiser. It adds a `claimed` map to `market_shares_for`'s intent loop for
  the *selling* arm — the `unbounded` map here is a separate local for the *buying* arm and neither
  replaces the other. It appends `nothing-left-to-sell` to `codes::ALL` (already on `main`) and a
  Markets switch to `SettingsDialog.tsx`; append after them. And it removes `price_sale` in favour of
  `price_sale_line`, which this bead does not touch. Rebase on `main` before starting and read the
  merged shape rather than the quotations above where the two disagree.
- **`bd show <id> --json` returns an array**, so read a bead's design with
  `jq -r '(if type=="array" then .[0] else . end).design'`. The pretty renderer reflows Markdown and
  mangles tables.
- **`available` must not be decremented, and `already_bought` must not be folded into it.** The
  sentences name the share the unit *started* with — "all 3 it can have here", "the other 3 of the 5
  this market has" — so `BuyAllPlan::available` stays the settled share and the running total travels
  beside it. This is the single easiest thing to get wrong here, and it is the mirror image of
  `ah-vw8e`'s own trap in the same paragraph of its plan.
- **The exact arm subtracts only from a settled share; the unbounded arm subtracts from the fallback
  too.** The exact arm's `None` fallback is *what the unit asked for* — not a quantity of goods, so
  there is nothing there for an earlier line to have spent down. The unbounded arm's fallback is the
  market line itself, which is a quantity of goods, and a unit cannot buy the line twice whether a
  share was settled or not. Collapsing the two is how this bead comes back.
- **The running total is subtracted at settle time, not at capture time.** Both surfaces resolve
  every exact `BUY` before any `BUY ALL`, so a `BUY 3 grain` written *below* a `BUY ALL grain` is
  priced before that `BUY ALL` settles. `Deferred::BuyAll` and `DeferredBuy` therefore carry
  `share: Option<i64>` and not a pre-reduced number.
- **`market_shares_for`'s claim fixup must run before the `wanted` sum.** The overrun is decided from `claims`, so raising a claim after that sum
  changes the shares without changing the finding, and the two go out of step.
- **A shipped test asserts the defect.** `two_buy_all_orders_spend_in_document_order`
  (`silver.rs:5519`) pins `expense == Some(96)`. It is not a regression net to preserve; rewrite it,
  comment and name included.
- **`ALL` is not `EXCEPT` on the buying side.** `Amount::All { except }`'s `except` is read on the
  selling side and ignored here — `rules/buy` has no `BUY ALL … EXCEPT` form. Do not start
  subtracting it.
- **`cargo test` rewrites the generated bindings as a side effect**, so a green `cargo test` with a
  dirty tree means the committed bindings were stale. `pnpm run check:generated`
  (`scripts/checkGenerated.ts`) is what turns that into a failure; run it before opening the PR.
- **`resolve_item` uppercases and `market_answer` does not resolve the same way.** Key every running
  total on `(lookups.item_tag)(item)` / `offer.tag.to_ascii_uppercase()`, which is what
  `market_shares_for` keys on, so two spellings of one item share one total.

## Context

A unit that receives men this month can be left at a **lower** skill level than its report showed.
Skill knowledge is held per man and merging averages it — `rules/give`, the paragraph above
*Studying*: "If units are merged together, their skills are averaged out. No rounding off is done;
rather, the computer keeps track for each unit of how many total months of training that unit has in
each skill." Unskilled arrivals therefore dilute what a unit knows, and `rules/buy` says so for
recruits in as many words: "Recruit 5 barbarians into the current unit. (This will dilute the skills
that the unit has.)"; `rules/economy_recruiting`: "New recruits will not have any skills or items."

`ah-z73s.1` shipped the arithmetic and the predicted Skills cell, and `ah-z73s.2` (closed) put the
six skill-reading checks on a post-transfer view. So **the drop is already computed and already
shown** — and it is invisible: `LUMB 2 (105)` reads like any other predicted value, and a player
reinforcing a skilled unit has no reason to hover the one cell they were not worried about.

This bead adds **one advisory code and its Settings toggle**, emitted when this month's arrivals of
men leave a unit at a lower level in a skill than the report gave it. Nothing else changes: the
Skills cell keeps the marking it already has, and no existing message moves.

**Three routes bring men, and all three are followed by the time this check runs.**

- `GIVE` and `TAKE` — `apply_transfers` (`semantics.rs:1636`), shipped by `ah-z73s.2` and widened by
  `ah-dxfd.2`. A `TAKE` of men is followed exactly as a `GIVE` is; the test
  `men_taken_rather_than_given_are_judged_on_what_arrives` (`semantics.rs:20799`) pins it, and
  `rules/take` is why — a `TAKE` is "a GIVE order with the direction reversed".
- Recruiting — `apply_recruits`, which **`ah-z73s.3` creates**. See the seam note below.

**`ah-z73s.3` is this bead's blocker and is not merged as this plan is written** (PR #725, open).
