### `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` — the check

Two free functions and the check itself, placed beside `check_transfer_targets`
(`semantics.rs:6468`), which is the other check about transfers of men.

```rust
/// `1 man` or `5 men`.
///
/// The message names people rather than the race the order moved, so no item catalogue and no
/// `Plurals` table is involved: `counted_item` (`semantics.rs:5058`) is for item *names* and would
/// answer `4 humans` here, which is not what the navigator agreed.
fn men_count(count: i64) -> String {
    if count == 1 {
        "1 man".to_string()
    } else {
        format!("{count} men")
    }
}

/// Which orders brought the men, e.g. `giving 5 men to this unit, taking 2 more and buying 4 more`.
///
/// The first route present is written in full with its own preposition; every later one is
/// `<verb> N more`, which carries the noun forward so "men" is said once. The order is fixed -
/// given, taken, bought - and matches the game's own: `rules/sequenceofevents` puts *Give orders*
/// (which is `GIVE` and `TAKE` alike) before *Market orders*.
fn arrivals_clause(arrivals: Arrivals) -> String
```

`arrivals_clause` builds a `Vec<String>` from whichever of the three counts is above zero:

| Route | First in the sentence | Later in the sentence |
|---|---|---|
| `given` | `format!("giving {} to this unit", men_count(given))` | `format!("giving {given} more")` |
| `taken` | `format!("taking {} into this unit", men_count(taken))` | `format!("taking {taken} more")` |
| `bought` | `format!("buying {} into this unit", men_count(bought))` | `format!("buying {bought} more")` |

and joins them: one clause as it stands; two as `"{a} and {b}"`; three as `"{a}, {b} and {c}"`.
A later clause is always bare `N more` — never `1 man more` — so `men_count` is used **only** for the
first clause.

```rust
/// A unit left at a lower skill level than its report showed, because men joined it this month.
///
/// Skill knowledge is per man and merging averages it (`rules/give`), so unskilled arrivals dilute
/// what a unit knows. `ah-z73s.1` already computes the drop and already shows it in the Skills
/// cell; this says so in words, because `LUMB 2 (105)` reads like any other predicted value and a
/// player reinforcing a skilled unit has no reason to hover it.
///
/// One finding per skill for the whole month, on the **receiving** unit, with **no line**: the
/// orders that caused it can sit in three different blocks, and pointing at one of them would blame
/// an order that is only part of the cause.
fn check_arrivals(hex: &Hex<'_>, options: &CheckOptions, findings: &mut Vec<Finding>) {
    if !options.emits(codes::ARRIVALS_LOWER_A_SKILL) {
        return;
    }

    for ordered in &hex.units {
        let arrivals = ordered.arrivals;
        if arrivals.total() <= 0 {
            continue;
        }
        // A unit whose arrivals this month cannot be followed is not judged at all: this module's
        // rule is that a warning that is wrong is worse than one that is missing, and `skills()`
        // answering `None` is the one doubt signal there is.
        let Some(after) = ordered.skills() else {
            continue;
        };
        // Only a skill the *report* showed can fall; one that arrived cannot go below 0.
        for skill in &ordered.unit.skills {
            let before = skill.level;
            let now = level_in(after, &skill.tag);
            if now >= before {
                continue;
            }
            let name = &skill.name;
            findings.push(ordered.finding(
                hex,
                codes::ARRIVALS_LOWER_A_SKILL,
                format!(
                    "{} lowers its {name} from {before} to {now}",
                    arrivals_clause(arrivals),
                ),
                None,
            ));
        }
    }
}
```

Reused, all verified in the tree:

- `Ordered::skills() -> Option<&[Skill]>` (`semantics.rs:1942`) — the post-transfer, post-recruit
  view. `None` is `SkillsAfterGifts::Unknowable`; `SkillsAfterGifts::Unchanged` returns
  `&self.unit.skills`, so a unit whose merge changed nothing compares equal and says nothing without
  a special case.
- `level_in(skills, tag) -> u32` (`semantics.rs:1887`) — 0 for a skill the list has not got, which
  is what a skill diluted out of the list reads as. `merge_skills` **drops** a skill whose merged
  points truncate to 0 (`effects.rs:1201`), so this is the case that produces `from 1 to 0`.
- `Ordered::finding(hex, code, message, at)` (`semantics.rs:2059`) — with `at: None` it sets
  `unit_id: Some(...)` and leaves `line`, `column_start` and `column_end` `None`, which is exactly
  the anchoring the navigator chose. `formed` is carried for free.
- `Skill { name, tag, level, points }` (`report/model.rs:145`). `name` is the lowercase human name —
  `cannot produce {label}: needs {name} {required}` at `semantics.rs:5897` renders it the same way.
  A skill diluted out of the merged list still has its name here, because this loop walks the
  **report's** list.

**Do not use `finding_at_block`** (`semantics.rs:2046`): it would set `line` to the receiver's block
line, which is the anchoring the navigator considered and rejected.

Call it from the per-hex check sequence, immediately after `check_transfer_targets`
(`semantics.rs:478`):

```rust
        check_transfer_targets(hex, &located, &options, &mut findings);
        check_arrivals(hex, &options, &mut findings);
```

No pre-work gate is needed. The check reads only fields `apply_transfers` and `apply_recruits`
already wrote for their own reasons, so with the toggle off it costs one `emits` call per hex.

**Where state lives.** Nowhere new. `Arrivals` is a `Copy` record on `Ordered`, owned by `Hex`, owned
by `review_turn` for the length of the call, written by the two passes that already walk the hex and
read by this check and by `check_sailing`'s crew record. `Ordered::unit` keeps pointing at the
**reported** unit throughout, which is what makes `unit.skills` the before and `skills()` the after.

**Which layer.** All of it is `crates/core`, except the toggle's copy, which is
`packages/shared`. Nothing in `core-client`, `browser-core` or either shell needs an edit: the wire
carries `disabled_codes: Vec<String>` and the shells compute that list generically from
`ADVISORY_CHECK_CODES`.

