## User-facing decisions

Five rounds. Rounds 1–2 were taken while planning `ah-z73s.1` and `ah-z73s.2` and are recorded in
this bead's notes; rounds 3–5 are this session's. Mockups, all merged in #728:
`docs/ui/ah-z73s4-wording.html`, `docs/ui/ah-z73s4-both-and-toggle.html`,
`docs/ui/ah-z73s4-mixed-effect.html`, `docs/ui/ah-z73s4-take.html`. The earlier rounds are drawn in
`docs/ui/ah-z73s2-finding.html` and `docs/ui/ah-z73s3-recruits.html`.

### Carried in from the earlier rounds, and not re-opened

- **The finding lives in the Problems panel.** *Rejected:* marking it only in the units table — the
  drop reads like any other predicted value and a player reinforcing a unit has no reason to hover
  the cell.
- **The Skills cell keeps the ordinary predicted marking** (italic brass, `was:` hover). It is not
  coloured as a warning; the finding carries the whole signal. *Rejected:* a Skills cell that can be
  predicted-normal or predicted-warning — a distinction often truncated off-screen, and no other
  cell warns about itself.
- **The gift sentence, verbatim:** `giving 5 men to this unit lowers its lumberjack from 3 to 2`.
  The count is the men arriving, the skill is named and never tagged, and both levels are given.
- **One finding per skill for the whole month, on the receiving unit.** *Rejected:* one finding per
  gift on each `GIVE` line — "several entries about one unit ending at one level, and the named unit
  is not the one whose block the line is in". *Rejected:* one finding for the month on the last
  gift's line — "the line is arbitrary; the last gift is no more to blame than the first, and
  reordering the document silently moves the warning".
- **Several skills falling at once are several findings**, each using the agreed sentence unchanged.
  *Rejected:* one finding naming every skill — "a new sentence shape, and it grows without limit".
- **The toggle sits in the Orders group, after `give-target-not-here`.** *Rejected:* Studying/Teaching
  — "nothing in that group is a GIVE".
- **Default ON.** It fires only when arriving men actually cost a level, which is uncommon.
- **The giving unit is never mentioned.** Per `rules/give`, dividing evenly among people leaves its
  points per man unchanged, so a transfer never alters the source's own skills.
- **A unit whose merged skills cannot be worked out says nothing**, exactly as `ah-z73s.2` defines
  doubt.

### Round 3 — the recruiting sentence, the zero, both routes, the toggle

- **The recruiting sentence, verbatim:** `buying 4 men into this unit lowers its lumberjack from 1
  to 0`. Chosen (R2) because it names the order the player actually wrote, so the line to look at is
  obvious without one being pointed at. *Rejected:* R1, `recruiting 4 men into this unit` — the
  rules' own word, and it mirrors the gift sentence, but it is the app's word rather than the
  order's. *Rejected:* R3, `4 recruits joining this unit lower its …` — the verb would have to agree
  with the count, which the gift sentence never has to do.
- **A level of 0 reads as `0`, in both sentences.** Chosen (Z1) — nothing already agreed changes, and
  there is nothing conditional to build. *Rejected:* Z2, `to none` everywhere — closer to what the
  Skills cell shows, but it moves a sentence already approved. *Rejected:* Z3, a clause of its own
  (`costs it its lumberjack entirely`) — a third and fourth shape for a rare case, and the player is
  not told what they had.
- **A unit given men *and* buying men gets one entry naming both routes.** Chosen (M2), which keeps
  one entry per skill for the month exactly as decided, states the month's real drop, and names both
  causes so the player knows fixing one is not enough. *Rejected:* M1, one entry per route in the
  game's order — two entries about one unit ending at one level, the very thing already rejected.
  *Rejected:* M3, a route-neutral `9 men joining this unit lower its …` — says the least about why.
- **One Settings toggle, retitled.** Chosen (T1): title `Arrivals that lower a skill`, description
  `Men joining a unit, given or bought, that leave it at a lower skill level than the report gave
  it.` The description's two verbs are deliberately the two verbs the messages use. *Rejected:* T3,
  two toggles one per route — each could then be silenced alone, but the both-at-once entry belongs
  to neither and needs a third rule. *Rejected:* T2, keeping the agreed title `Transfers that lower a
  skill` — "Transfers" does not cover recruiting, and the title is the part a player scanning the
  list actually reads. *Rejected* for the description: `by gift or by recruiting` (the word
  "recruiting" appears in no message the toggle switches) and `A GIVE or BUY of men …`.

### Round 4 — the joined sentence, the short BUY, the anchor

- **The joined sentence, verbatim:** `giving 5 men to this unit and buying 4 more lowers its
  lumberjack from 3 to 1`. Chosen (A1) — `more` carries the noun forward so "men" is said once, and
  only one singular has to be got right. *Rejected:* A2, `and buying 4 men` — says "men" twice and
  needs the singular rule in two places. *Rejected:* A3, `9 men joining this unit lower its … : 5
  given and 4 bought` — a fourth shape with a colon clause no other message uses.
- **The count is what actually arrives, not what the order asked for.** Chosen (B1): a `BUY 4 PEAS`
  the unit can only half afford says `buying 2 men`. It agrees with the Skills cell, the ITEMS column
  and the units table, and it is the number the arithmetic used. *Rejected:* B2, the ordered figure —
  the two levels are computed from the settled number, so the sentence and its own arithmetic would
  disagree. *Rejected:* B3, silence when the `BUY` is short — silent in exactly the case where the
  drop is most surprising.
- **The finding stays lineless.** Chosen (C1), even though a `BUY` *is* written in the receiver's own
  block: one rule for every sentence whatever the routes, and it never points at a line that is only
  part of the cause. The consequence was put to the navigator and accepted: the entry sorts below
  every finding that has a line, and is never underlined in the editor. *Rejected:* C2, anchoring on
  the receiving unit's block line — for a pure gift it points at a block containing no `GIVE`.

### Round 5 — a `TAKE` is a third route

- **A `TAKE` gets a verb of its own, and the joined sentence lists whichever routes brought men.**
  Chosen (E1). Verbatim: `taking 5 men into this unit lowers its lumberjack from 3 to 2`; two routes,
  `giving 5 men to this unit and taking 2 more lowers …`; three, `giving 5 men to this unit, taking 2
  more and buying 4 more lowers …`. It keeps the rule chosen three times already — name the order the
  player wrote — and is bounded at three clauses. *Rejected:* E2, counting taken men as given — two
  clauses at most and nothing new to test, but a player searching for a `GIVE` to this unit will not
  find one. *Rejected:* E3, a route-neutral shape whenever a `TAKE` was involved — a fourth shape
  appearing only for the rarest route, so one unit reads two different ways in consecutive months.
- **Both routes are named even when one of them raised the level.** Chosen (D1): a unit given five
  *skilled* lumberjacks (2 → 3) that then buys nine peasants (3 → 1) reads `giving 5 men to this unit
  and buying 9 more lowers its lumberjack from 2 to 1`. It is the only option whose two numbers are
  the report's figure and the month's figure, which is what every sentence here promises, and both
  orders are load-bearing — deleting either alone still leaves the unit at 1. *Rejected:* D2, naming
  only the routes that lowered it while keeping the month's numbers — the buy alone took it from 3 to
  1, so the numbers would no longer describe the step named. *Rejected:* D3, naming only the lowering
  route with that route's own step — the intermediate 3 appears nowhere the player can see.

