  numbers will have moved and names may have changed in review. Confirm each with `grep` before
  relying on it, and keep the property rather than the spelling.
- **`check:fast` does not run the browser suites**, and `tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts` asserts exact
  problem counts. `docs/retrospectives/ah-dwk6.md:47-50` records this as the *second* sighting of a
  new default-on advisory breaking exact-count smoke specs. Run
  `pnpm exec playwright test --project=web` before opening the PR.
- **`ALL`'s length is hardcoded** (`semantics.rs:138`) and changes whenever a code lands. Read the
  current number rather than trusting one written down here. Adding a constant without
  bumping it is a compile error, which is the good case; adding it to the array without adding the
  constant is not.
- **A new default-on check trips other fixtures.** Several existing tests will start seeing this
  finding and need `disabling_all(&[…])` (`semantics.rs:18344`). Silence it in *those* tests; never
  make it default-off to keep them quiet.
- **`merge_skills` matches tags case-sensitively** (`effects.rs:1196`, `skill.tag == tag`) while
  `level_in` uses `eq_ignore_ascii_case` (`semantics.rs:1890`). Walking the report's own list and
  looking each tag up with `level_in` is safe on both counts; do not invent a tag spelling.
- **`merge_skills` drops a skill whose merged points truncate to 0** (`effects.rs:1201`), so the
  merged list is not a superset of the report's. That is precisely why the loop walks
  `ordered.unit.skills` and reads the merged level with `level_in`, which answers 0 for an absent
  entry — and it is what produces the agreed `from 1 to 0`.
- **`SkillsAfterGifts::Unchanged` returns `&self.unit.skills`** (`semantics.rs:1944`), so a unit
  whose merge changed nothing compares equal to itself and emits nothing. Do not add a separate
  `Unchanged` branch to the check; it would be dead code that looks load-bearing.
- **`Ordered::holding` reads `self.unit.items`, not `early_items()`** (`semantics.rs:2022`). It is a
  pre-transfer accessor despite the post-transfer one existing beside it. This check needs neither,
  but the pair is easy to mistake for each other.
- **The doubt signals are two, and they are independent.** `SkillsAfterGifts::Unknowable` (set from
  `Working::doubted`) and `HoldingsAfterGifts::Unknowable` (set from `Working::items_unknowable`)
  answer different questions and are documented as deliberately independent. This check reads
  `skills()` and must not consult `holdings_unknown()`.
- **`Amount::All` on a `BUY` credits nothing**, by `ah-z73s.3`'s decision: such a unit is left
  `Unknowable` and therefore silent here. Do not try to price it.
- **`skill_pts` names a skill after its own tag** (`semantics.rs:20334`, `name: tag.to_lowercase()`),
  so a fixture built with `with_skill_pts` produces `lowers its lumb from 3 to 2`. That is why
  `with_named_skill_pts` exists. Every test that asserts a message must use it, and a test that
  asserts `lumb` has pinned the fixture rather than the behaviour.
- **A class selector of `MEN` is followed, not doubted.** `moves` (`semantics.rs:1488`) resolves
  `GIVE 3100 ALL MEN` to the man tags the source holds; only a non-people class, an `EXCEPT` or a
  stated quantity on a class form, or an item the ruleset does not know answers `Moves::Unknowable`
  — and that sets `items_unknowable`, which is the **holdings** doubt signal, not the skills one.
  Do not reach for a class selector to write a "cannot be followed" test.
- **`Moves::Unknowable` and `Working::doubted` are different things.** The first sets
  `items_unknowable` and leaves `skills_after_gifts` alone; only `doubted` produces
