## Known traps

- **The clamp must not move a shipped warning, and increment 8 is what proves it.** All four capped
  skills (`WOLF`, `BIRD`, `DRAG`, `SUBA`) have `costs: []`, so `charged` times anything is nothing.
  If a `not-enough-silver` or `not-enough-items` test needs its expectation changed, the clamp has
  been applied in the wrong place — most likely to `charged` or to `wanted` instead of to `made`.
- **`made`, `made_certain`, `wanted` and `charged` are four different numbers.** The arrival is
  `made`; the consumption is `plan.materials`, which is `charged` times the per-item amount; the
  hover's low end is `made_certain`; the cap sentence's second number is `wanted`. Collapsing any
  two of them deletes something. `ah-ofpb.4`'s *Known traps* has the three warnings that catch the
  `charged` floor.
- **The family is arithmetic, never a spell list.** A level 5 `CRRI` mage makes exactly one ring
  (20 × 5 = 100%), and a level 5 `CRSF` exactly two. Any `match` on a set of skill tags is wrong,
  and increment 5 exists to catch it.
- **`CREATION_STATED`'s guard, and now `CONTROL_STATED`'s, must run only on a paragraph that stated
  a creation.** `data/BIRD` level 1 is about scouting and says "can control small birds" while
  creating nothing; an unconditional guard throws on it and the scrape dies with a message about a
  cap that does not exist.
- **`data/BIRD` spells its multiplier as a word** — "squared, times two", not "times 2". It is the
  only cap sentence on the page that does, which is what `wordOrNumber` is for. The same entry is
  the only one with a `level_offset`, and both facts come from the same sentence.
- **`packages/core-client/src/index.ts`'s preview types are hand-written, not generated**
  (`:327-384`). `cargo test` will not update them, and nothing fails until the field is read at
  runtime and comes back `undefined`. `ah-ofpb.1` names this trap too.
- **`cargo test` rewrites `packages/core-client/src/generated/UnitSilver.ts`**, which CI checks
  (`.github/workflows/ci.yml:265-269`) — a forgotten commit fails the build, which is the good
  outcome. `packages/ruleset/src/generated` is checked by `pnpm run check:generated` instead.
- **`config/public/ruleset.json` is regenerated, never hand-edited.** `committed.test.ts` fails with
  the exact command in its message.
- **`pnpm run atlantis` needs `node_modules`.** The main checkout has none, so a rule lookup there
  dies with `tsx: command not found`. Run it from your own worktree after `pnpm install`.
- **`ProductionCap` is `ts(export)`ed**, so adding `Room` widens a TypeScript union and
  `unitTooltip.ts`'s `capSentence` stops being exhaustive. That is a compile error and it is the
  point; do not silence it with a fallback that says "materials".
- **The default smoke fixture has no mage.** `g7f95t71`'s five own units carry observation, stealth
  and manipulation and nothing else, and none of those three has a `cast` entry. A cast's count comes
  from the caster's level, so every `CAST` on that report projects nothing — which looks exactly like
  a bug in this bead. `g5f21t23` unit 1294 is the one used instead, and the survey behind that choice
  is in *Increments* step 20.
- **A skill's tag is not the item's tag.** `CRRU` creates `RUNE`, `SWIN` creates `CLOU`, `SUSK`
  creates `SKEL`, `RAIS` creates `UNDE`, `SUDE` creates `DEMO`. The three that coincide — `WOLF`,
  `DRAG`, `BIRD`→`EAGL` (which does not) — make it easy to write a test that passes for the wrong
  reason. `CastOutput.tag` is the item, always.
- **From `.cerebro/traps.md`: never ask for an effect-level test in `packages/shared`.** That
  package has no jsdom. Everything this bead tests there is a pure function — `formatItems`,
  `itemsTooltip`, `capSentence` — and `UnitTableDock.test.tsx` renders with
  `renderToStaticMarkup`, which is why increment 19 asserts on markup and not on behaviour. No
  other trap in that file applies: no persisted setting, no `vite preview`, and the one smoke test
  added here does not run the PWA suite after it.

## Context

A unit may write `BUY ALL GRAI` instead of a number. `rules/buy`: *"If the second form is specified,
the unit will attempt to buy as many as it can afford"*, and where a line is oversubscribed *"the
available items will be split among the buyers in proportion to the amount each buyer attempted to
buy"*.

**Half of this is already shipped, and that is what makes it a bug rather than a gap.** The SILVER
column resolves `BUY ALL` today: `forecast_unit` pushes `Deferred::BuyAll { price, market_has }`
(`silver.rs:1080`) and settles it against a running total in the deferred pass (`silver.rs:1174`).
The ledger does not: `buy`'s `let Amount::Exact(count) = amount else { … }` (`semantics.rs:2717`)
records the line in `Ledger::uncounted` and returns, moving no goods and charging no silver.

So for a unit holding `356 SILV, 3 SWOR` in a hex whose market carries `30 grain [GRAI] at $18`:

| Cell | Says today |
|---|---|
| SILVER | `14` — the unit spent 342 of its silver |
| ITEMS | `356 SILV, 3 SWOR + ?` — the unit still has all its silver, and no grain arrived |

**Two cells on one row, contradicting each other.** That is the failure class `ah-lu0f`, `ah-abwx`
and `ah-ycuj` were each filed for, and the one `ah-agbm` built the shared pricing seam to prevent.
It also reaches further than the display: the ledger's `balance` is what `check_sailing`,
`check_movement`, `report_shortfalls` and `charge_upkeep` read, so every later judgement about that
unit's month is made against silver the purchase has already spent.

**The bead's title was wrong about `SELL ALL`, and this plan does not touch it.** `SELL ALL` is
resolved on both surfaces already — `silver.rs:968` (`Amount::All { except } => (unit_holds -
except).max(0)`) and `semantics.rs:2867` (`Amount::All { except } => holds - except`). It has a
different defect, filed as **`ah-q7jd`**: the column resolves it against the *report's* holding and
the ledger against the *running* balance, so a `GIVE` earlier in the month makes them disagree. Out
of scope here; see *Out of scope*.

**When this lands**, a `BUY ALL` reads in the ITEMS column exactly as a stated `BUY` does, the two
cells on the row agree, the ledger charges what the purchase costs, `buy all NOMA` moves the MEN
column, and a hover on either cell says which limit produced the number — the unit's silver, the
market's stock, or the player's own units competing for one line.

