#### 2. `semantics.rs` — the ledger settles it after the unit's month is counted

`Ledger` (`semantics.rs:1802`) gains one field:

```rust
    /// `BUY ALL` lines, per unit, in document order, read but not yet settled.
    ///
    /// `rules/buy` gives a `BUY ALL` as many as the unit can afford, and what it can afford is
    /// not known until the rest of its month has been applied - `credit_tax` runs *after* a
    /// unit's intents in `ledger_for`, so settling in document order would price the purchase
    /// against a balance that has not been credited its tax. `forecast_unit` defers it for the
    /// same reason, and the two must defer to the same figure or the SILVER and ITEMS cells go
    /// back to contradicting each other.
    pub(crate) buy_all: BTreeMap<String, Vec<DeferredBuy>>,
```

```rust
/// One `BUY ALL` the ledger has read and not yet settled.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub(crate) struct DeferredBuy {
    pub placed: PlacedIntent,
    /// The canonical tag, uppercased, as `Ledger::balance` keys it.
    pub tag: String,
    pub price: i64,
    /// This unit's settled share of the line, or the line itself where nothing was settled.
    pub available: i64,
    /// The whole line.
    pub market_has: i64,
}
```

`buy`'s `Amount::All` arm (`semantics.rs:2717-2727`) **records instead of giving up**. Its current
comment asserts the opposite of what this bead establishes and **must be deleted, not left beside
the new code**:

```rust
    let tag = offer.tag.to_ascii_uppercase();
    let available = standing.share_of(&tag, MarketSide::Buying).unwrap_or(offer.amount);

    let Amount::Exact(count) = amount else {
        // `BUY ALL` is settled once the unit's whole month is counted - see `Ledger::buy_all`.
        ledger.buy_all.entry(who.clone()).or_default().push(DeferredBuy {
            placed: placed.clone(),
            tag,
            price: offer.price,
            available,
            market_has: offer.amount,
        });
        return;
    };
```

Note the two lines above the `else` moving up: `tag` and `available` are wanted by both arms now,
and the `Exact` arm below uses `.unwrap_or(*count)` today. **That fallback differs on purpose and
must not be unified**: `share_of` returns `None` only where nothing was settled, and the bounded
form then falls back to the ask while the unbounded one falls back to the line. Keep two
`unwrap_or`s; the `Exact` arm's stays exactly as it is.

The settle function, new, immediately after `buy`:

```rust
/// Settles this unit's `BUY ALL` lines, in document order, once the rest of its month has been
/// applied.
///
/// Called from `ledger_for` right after `credit_tax`, which is the moment the unit's silver
/// balance matches `forecast_unit`'s `running` - report holding, plus every credit, less every
/// eager charge, and **not** the late income, which `charge_upkeep` nets off the fee rather than
/// crediting (`ah-uwa3`).
fn settle_buy_all(ledger: &mut Ledger<'_>, hex: &Hex<'_>, actor: &Ordered<'_>)
```

Its body, per line, in document order:

1. **A doubted unit is left uncounted.** `forecast_unit` skips its whole deferred pass when either
   side is doubted, so there is no figure on the column to agree with:
   ```rust
       if ledger.doubted.contains(who) {
           ledger.uncounted.entry(who.clone()).or_default().push(deferred.placed.line);
           continue;
       }
   ```
2. **A `BUY ALL` followed by a `GIVE` of all this unit's silver is left uncounted**, keeping today's
   ` + ?`. The reason is in *Known traps*; the test is:
   ```rust
       /// Whether a later order gives away every silver this unit has, which the ledger settles
       /// eagerly and the column defers - so the two would disagree about what is left to buy
       /// with. Only a *later* one matters: written first, both surfaces leave nothing.
       fn gives_all_silver_after(
           hex: &Hex<'_>,
           actor: &Ordered<'_>,
           ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
           after: usize,
       ) -> bool
   ```
   `true` when any `placed` on `actor.intents` with `placed.line > after` is an
   `Intent::Give { what: Selector::Item(text), amount: Amount::All { .. }, .. }` whose `text`
   resolves through `resolve_item` to `SILV`. A `Selector::Class` gift doubts the unit already, so
   step 1 has taken it.
3. Otherwise settle:
   ```rust
       let (priced, plan) = price_buy_all(
           balance_of(ledger, who, SILVER), deferred.price, deferred.available, deferred.market_has);
       charge(ledger, who, SILVER, priced.spends, &deferred.placed);
       credit(ledger, who, &deferred.tag, plan.bought);
       if plan.bought != 0 {
           ledger.movements.push(ItemMovement {
               unit_id: who.clone(),
               tag: deferred.tag.clone(),
               name: item_name(&deferred.tag, hex, ledger.ruleset),
               delta: plan.bought,
               from_unshown: None,
               produced: false,
           });
       }
   ```
   **No movement when nothing is bought**, which is the navigator's Z2 answer in code: the cell is
   unchanged, unmarked and upright, and the hover carries the whole message.

`ledger_for` (`semantics.rs:1976-1993`) calls it in the unit loop, **after** `credit_tax`:

```rust
        credit_tax(&mut ledger, hex, ordered, pillaged);
        settle_buy_all(&mut ledger, hex, ordered);
```

Borrow note: `settle_buy_all` reads `ledger.buy_all` and writes `ledger.balance`, so take the
unit's `Vec<DeferredBuy>` out first — `let Some(lines) = ledger.buy_all.remove(who) else { return };`
— and walk the owned vector. Removing rather than borrowing also makes the pass unrepeatable by
construction.

`forecast_hex`'s `Lookups` (`semantics.rs:816-861`) gains the closure, beside `counted_by_name`:

```rust
