## Context

`sailing_levels_after_orders` (`semantics.rs:5641`) returns `None` the moment men of any race arrive
at a unit aboard a fleet, and `check_sailing` (`semantics.rs:5745`) sums those `Option`s, so one
arrival silences `fleet-undercrewed` for the whole fleet. The doc comment says why: "the game merges
given men into the receiving unit and recomputes its skill, which this application does not model;
counting them at the receiver's own level would turn two unskilled gnolls into two sailors".

**That reason is gone.** `ah-z73s.1` shipped the merge arithmetic (`effects::merge_skills`), and
`ah-z73s.2` shipped a post-gift view of a unit's skills on `Ordered` — `SkillsAfterGifts`,
`Ordered::skills() -> Option<&[Skill]>`, and the `apply_gifts_of_men` walk that writes it. This bead
spends that machinery on the crew check and closes the last arrival route the walk does not follow.

**Two things have moved since `ah-z73s.2`'s plan was written, and both matter here.**

- **A `TAKE` of men from a unit *in this hex* is now merged, not doubted.** `ah-dxfd.1` taught
  `apply_gifts_of_men` to resolve a `TAKE`'s source (`semantics.rs:1541-1560`); only
  `GiveTarget::Nowhere` / `Discard` — a source the hex does not show — still doubts the taker. The
  existing test `doubt_carries_to_the_next_unit_along` (`semantics.rs:19058`) says so in its own
  comment. `ah-z73s.2`'s plan and this bead's own description both predate that and say a `TAKE`
  doubts; they are out of date, not a specification.
- **`GIVE <unit> ALL MEN` / `ALL ITEMS` is now resolved**, also by `ah-dxfd.1`
  (`GiftConcerns::PeopleClass`, `semantics.rs:1572-1587`). The doc comment on that variant still
  says "any receiver here is doubted rather than guessed at" and is stale; read the code.

So after `ah-dxfd.1` exactly **one** arrival route is unfollowed: **recruiting**. `rules/buy`:
"Recruit 5 barbarians into the current unit. (This will dilute the skills that the unit has.)";
`rules/economy_recruiting`: "New recruits will not have any skills or items." The units table
already models it — `effects::settle_headcounts` (`effects.rs:1096`) merges recruits in at zero
points — so today the Skills cell of a recruiting unit reads `LUMB 0 (21)` while the Problems panel
judges the same unit at the level its report printed. The navigator chose to close that
(decision 1 below), which is why this bead touches `apply_gifts_of_men` as well as the crew check.

**Why the order of the three merges is the game's own.** `rules/sequenceofevents` lists *Give
orders* (GIVE, TAKE), then *Market orders* (SELL, BUY), then *Movement orders* (ADVANCE, MOVE,
SAIL), then *Month long orders* (TEACH, STUDY, PRODUCE, BUILD). So gifts and takes merge first,
recruits second, and both are complete before SAIL and before every one of the six skill-reading
checks runs. Nothing here has to reason about ties.

**What changes for the player.** A fleet whose crew changes this month is judged instead of passed
over — which cuts both ways, and the surprising direction is the one that matters: unskilled men
joining a skilled crew *lower* its sailing level, so a raft the report shows with exactly the
sailors it needs can be warned it will not sail. `rules/give`: "If units are merged together, their
skills are averaged out."

**Blocked-on:** nothing. `ah-z73s.2` and `ah-dxfd.1` are both merged (`f9227f7`, `c5acad5`); this
plan was written against `639ffce` and every symbol in it was read there.

