## User-facing decisions

Mockups, committed with this bead: `docs/ui/ah-z73s3-scope.html`, `docs/ui/ah-z73s3-wording.html`,
`docs/ui/ah-z73s3-wording-2.html`, `docs/ui/ah-z73s3-recruits.html`.

**1. Which arrivals the checks follow. Chosen: gifts, takes and recruiting — B.** The navigator's
reason: the units table already dilutes a recruiting unit's skills and the Problems panel does not,
which is one screen giving two answers about one unit. The deciding fact, found while the question
was open: both surfaces read the *same* ledger for recruits (`effects.rs:167-176` builds the units
table from `semantics::item_effects`), so this is one walk read twice rather than two walkers taught
to agree.
*Rejected:* **follow gifts only, and leave a recruiting unit's fleet unjudged** — smaller and
touching one check, but it leaves the contradiction on screen and leaves the same silence in the six
other skill warnings.

**2. What the crew sentence says when men joined. Chosen: a third shape, using the clause "once the
men joining the crew this month are counted".** The full sentence, verbatim:

> `Raft [218] is short of sailors: 2 sailing levels aboard, 0 once the men joining the crew this month are counted, it needs 2, so it will not sail`

The shape was chosen first (option Q of `ah-z73s3-wording.html`: show the report's figure, then the
judged one, then a clause) and the clause's words second (option Q2 of `ah-z73s3-wording-2.html`).
The navigator's reasons: the sentence must carry the report's number beside the judged one, because
the player is looking at a figure the report never printed; "joining" is true of a gift, a take and
a purchase alike, where "transfers of men" would call a `BUY` a transfer; and it says "the crew"
rather than "it", which would read as the fleet when the men actually join a unit inside it.
*Rejected:* **P, the plain existing fallback** — "the drop row says 0 about a raft the report says
has 2 of the 2 it needs, and offers nothing to explain the gap".
*Rejected:* **R, naming the men who joined** — explains the surprise best, but two new strings, and
it mentions only half of what moved when men join and leave at once.
*Rejected:* **S, one before-and-after shape for every changed crew** — simplest rule, but it
rewrites a sentence that shipped and was verified after two failures on `ah-j0e`, and loses the
words that named the cause.
*Rejected:* **Q1, "once this month's transfers of men have run"** — the words first chosen, dropped
once B put recruiting inside the same sentence.
**Not spelled out, deliberately:** a fleet where men both joined and left gets the joining clause
and no mention of the departure. The navigator accepted that rather than adding a fifth shape for
the rarest case; the level fell because of dilution, not because of the man who left, so a sentence
naming both would carry numbers that do not add up for a reader.

**3. `BUY ALL`. Chosen: a unit with any `BUY ALL` has its skills left unjudged.** The navigator's
reason: it follows the rule set on `ah-z73s.2`, that the player never sees a skill warning computed
from a figure the application knows is incomplete. Accepted cost, stated in the question: `BUY ALL
SWORDS` brings no men and is silenced too, because `PEASANTS` names no catalogue item and the two
cannot be told apart here. It goes away when `ah-jown` resolves `BUY ALL` into a quantity.
*Rejected:* **judge it as if it recruited nobody** — exactly today's behaviour and no unit loses a
warning, but "it is the confident wrong number this module refuses to produce, and now knowingly".

**4. The six other skill warnings. Chosen: not one message string changes.** A recruiting unit will
now be told `cannot produce lumber: needs lumberjack 1, has no lumberjack` where its report says
lumberjack 1, with nothing on the line to bridge the two — the same answer the navigator gave for
gifts as decision 3 of `ah-z73s.2`, for the same reason, applied to the same six warnings.
*Rejected:* **adding the joining clause to all six** — re-opens a decision already taken, where the
clause was rejected as six strings becoming twelve, and would roughly double this bead.

**5. `ah-z73s.4` widens to recruiting.** The navigator chose that the drop advisory should explain a
level lowered by recruiting as well as by a gift, with its own sentence shape along the lines of
*"recruiting 4 men into this unit lowers its lumberjack from 1 to none"*. **That is a change to
`ah-z73s.4`, not work for this bead** — see *Out of scope*. The decision has been appended to that
bead's own notes, so it does not depend on anyone reading this plan.
*(A correction to `docs/ui/ah-z73s3-recruits.html`, which calls `ah-z73s.4` "planned but not built":
it is interviewed but not planned — it carries its interview in `notes` and has no `design` and no
`planned` label. That makes the widening cheaper than the mockup claimed, not dearer, so the answer
