## User-facing decisions

Six questions over three rounds, all with the navigator. The mockups are committed and merged:

- `docs/ui/ah-1mpx.6.2-r4-scan.html`
- `docs/ui/ah-1mpx.6.2-r5-states.html`
- `docs/ui/ah-1mpx.6.2-r6-order.html`

The epic `ah-1mpx.6` carries rounds 1–3, which settled where derived skills appear, how they are
marked, how far back the scan reads, and the mixed-case export sentence. **Nothing below reopens any
of that**, and nothing in this bead may either.

**S — exporting while the scan is still running. Chosen: S2.** A notice line while the scan runs,
and `Export…` disabled until it lands; both vanish on their own and the real notice replaces the
line in the same slot. *Rejected:* **S1**, write what we have and say nothing — the notice would
assert that no battle we have seen involved units that were in battles we had read, and the file
would go out short with nothing ever telling the player. **S3**, putting the wait in the button's
caption — the foreign notice would then be absent until the scan landed, so the caveat list visibly
jumps, and the button already says `Exporting…` for a different wait. *Consequence for the
implementer:* the button keeps exactly two captions.

**W4 — the notice outside the mixed case. Chosen: W4-B**, three sentence shapes. *Rejected:*
**W4-A**, one template with the numbers dropped in, which produces "the other 0 go out with none" in
two of four cases. **W4-C**, keeping today's sentence for the none case — it says "a report never
shows you those" one dialog away from a units table that is now showing exactly those, which is the
claim the whole family exists to stop making.

**N — a turn imported while the game is open. Chosen: N1**, it folds in immediately. *Rejected:*
**N2**, scanning only at game open, which leaves the units table confidently showing turn 68 while
turn 72 is on screen and asks the player to close and reopen the game, which the application asks
nowhere else.

**V — when the waiting line shows at all. Chosen: V1**, only while the scan is running *and* the
file contains at least one foreign unit. *Rejected:* **V2**, whenever the scan is running, which
blocks an all-own-units export for a reason that has nothing to do with those units.

**F — a stored turn that will not load or parse. Chosen: F1**, carry on with the turns that did read
and name how many were missed. *Rejected:* **F2**, log silently, which leaves "4 of them" quietly a
floor rather than the answer. **F3**, treating one bad turn as a whole-scan failure, which throws
away thirty good turns.

**P — the plurals. Chosen: P1**, every number agrees with its own noun. *Rejected:* **P2**, keeping
the count in every slot, which produces "the other 1 goes out with none".

**O — an own unit with no skills of its own. Chosen: O1**, foreign units only. *Rejected:* **O2**,
filling any empty skill list, which can put roster skills from an old turn on a unit whose current
and complete truth is in the report on screen, and which fills a unit that no sentence in the dialog
counts.

**L — the order of the notice lines. Chosen: L1.** `remembered`, then the scan's line in the foreign
slot (`scanning` while the scan runs, otherwise `foreign` followed by `unread-turns` when there are
any), then `empty-side`. Markers: brass (`text-warn`) for `foreign` and `unread-turns`, grey
(`text-ink-dim`) for `scanning` alongside `empty-side`. *Rejected:* **L2**, all the scan's lines at
the bottom, which puts the waiting line and the foreign line on screen together saying contradictory
things.

