rather than as a template that has had numbers pushed into it. The smoke test pins the characters;
only a person can say the sentence is right.

## Known traps

- **`applyLoadedTurn` is not the only way a turn arrives.** The restore effect sets `parsed` and
  `rawReport` directly, and that is how a turn appears every time the application is reopened — the
  commonest case there is. `AppShell.tsx:1824` already carries this warning for the Armies refresh;
  effect B above is keyed on `rawReport` for exactly the same reason. Hooking N1 into the import
  handler would leave it silently never running on the path most players take.
- **The scan and the import race, and the merge rule is the only thing that makes it safe.**
  `withRosterSkills` keeps whichever observation has the **greater or equal** turn, so neither the
  background scan nor a fold-in can undo the other whichever finishes first. Changing that
  comparison to "always replace" reintroduces the bug, and it will not show up in a fast test — the
  scan has to be slow for it to bite. Increment 5's fourth test is the one that catches it.
- **Do not re-implement the projection in TypeScript.** `parsed.battles[].attackers[].skills` is
  reachable from JS (`ah-1mpx.6.1` put `skills` on the generated `BattleUnit`), so walking it here
  looks cheaper than calling `client.rosterSkills`. It is a second implementation of report order,
  attackers-before-defenders and omit-the-empty, and two implementations of one question is how two
  answers appear. `ah-1mpx.6.1`'s own design says the same thing about `body`.
- **`XBOW` is both the crossbow skill's tag and the crossbow item's tag** (`data/skills`,
  `data/tableiteminfo`). `ROSTER_SKILL_TAGS` maps a skill name to a skill tag and is never consulted
  about an item, so this does not bite here — but it will bite anyone who reuses the table for items.
- **A `Skill` is not a `BattleSkill`.** `SkillInfo` (`packages/core-client/src/generated/SkillInfo.ts`)
  is `{ name, tag, level, points }`; a roster discloses no study points, and writing `points: 0`
  would make a recovered skill indistinguishable from a real one nobody has studied. That is why
  `DerivedSkill` is its own type and why `ArmyMemberRecord.skills` is never written to.
- **`exportReadiness` counts a member twice when one Army is on both sides.** That is stated
  behaviour (`armyExport.ts:153`), and the recovered count must be computed over the same
  `members` list so the two numbers cannot disagree. Do not build the recovered count from a `Set`
  of unit ids.
- **The tag table is per-ruleset in principle and fixed in practice.** If a ruleset ever renames
  `combat` or `riding`, `ROSTER_SKILL_TAGS` is the one place to change, and a name it does not know
  is dropped rather than exported with a wrong tag. `ah-1mpx.6.1`'s corpus test is what guarantees
  no sixth name reaches it today.
- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom.** A component test there renders with `renderToStaticMarkup`,
  runs no effects and cannot click (`packages/shared/src/testing/README.md`). Everything about the
  disabled button, the marker colour and the dialog's behaviour belongs in
  `tests/smoke/armies.spec.ts`.
- **`bd` and cargo find their configuration by walking up.** Branch in a worktree under
  `.cerebro/worktrees/`, never outside the repository and never in the main checkout.

## Context

In the magic study tree's **Whole graph** view, which tier a skill sits in is right, but where it
sits *inside* that tier is not: the column is sorted alphabetically by name and every box is nailed
to a row, packed from the top. Nothing pulls a skill towards what it stands on, so a lineage that is
short in tiers can still be long in pixels. The navigator's example, found while verifying
`ah-gjbs.2`: summon balrog's lineage runs force/spirit -> demon lore -> summon imps -> summon demon
-> summon balrog, and summon imps sits at row 29 of the 38-row middle tier while summon balrog sits
at row 0 of the 2-row last tier. The lit path sweeps 783px down a 1095px drawing, and at a zoom
where the names can be read the two ends are not on screen together - which is the one thing the lit
path exists to show.

Measured over the shipped ruleset, **ten of the seventy skills** have a lineage whose bounding box
cannot be fitted into the graph dialog's real box (1464 x 743 screen pixels) at any zoom step in the
`names` band.

This bead changes only *where a skill sits inside its tier*. Tier assignment - the longest-path
depth `magicTree.ts` computes, giving 4 / 15 / 38 / 11 / 2 - is `ah-gjbs.2`'s and stays exactly as it
is. No skill moves between tiers, no edge changes, the lighting does not change, and the drawing
keeps the same width.

**What was tried and rejected before the design was settled**, because it is the obvious reading of
the bead's own description and it does not work: re-ordering within a tier while keeping every box
on a row. Measured, it cuts the tangle a lot (total vertical line-length 31,401px -> 25,056px) and
leaves summon balrog *worse* (783px -> 891px). A tier of 38 rows and a tier of 2 rows cannot line up
while every box must sit on a row; the rows themselves are the constraint. The navigator was shown
this as a rendered picture and chose against it.

The chosen design: re-order each tier **and** let a box slide freely down its column towards
whatever it stands on and whatever stands on it, keeping at least one row's clearance from its
neighbours. `docs/ui/ah-geka-graph-order.html` is the design, generated from the real ruleset.

After the change, measured on the shipped ruleset:

| | summon balrog's lineage | lineages that fit at a names zoom | total up-and-down in the 102 lines |
|---|---|---|---|
| today | 783 px | 60 of 70 | 31,401 px |
| after | 429 px | **70 of 70** | 11,104 px |

The drawing goes from 1366 x 1100 to **1366 x 1095**, so the dialog box, the opening `fitGraph` step
(-3) and every gesture are untouched.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

Two files. Everything about position is derived inside `buildMagicGraph`; nothing else in the
codebase holds a coordinate.

### `packages/shared/src/workspace/magicGraphLayout.ts`

This is where all of the work is. Today `buildMagicGraph` (line 152) does two passes in this order:

1. lines 174-188: for each depth, sort the column by `name.localeCompare` and set
   `node.y = PAD_TOP + rank * (NODE_HEIGHT + NODE_GAP)`; push a `GraphTier`.
2. lines 190-220: build `at` (tag -> node, which now has coordinates) and derive the edges from it.

**That order has to be swapped**, because seating now needs to know which skills stand on which.
The new order is: collect the nodes -> build the adjacency from `tree` (tags only, no coordinates)
-> seat -> build the edges from the seats. The edge-building block itself (lines 200-219) does not
change at all, including its `[...skill.within, ...skill.crossing]` concatenation and the comment at
lines 197-199 explaining why reading either list alone silently drops edges. Reuse that same
concatenation when building the adjacency.

Existing constants to build on, all already exported at lines 30-39:
`NODE_WIDTH = 208`, `NODE_HEIGHT = 22`, `NODE_GAP = 5`, `TIER_PITCH = 268`, `PAD_LEFT = 26`,
`PAD_TOP = 50`, and the module-private `PAD_BOTTOM = 24`.

**New exported constant:**

```ts
/**
 * Vertical distance between one box's top and the next's, in world units. The closest two skills
 * in a column may ever sit; a box slides freely otherwise.
 */
export const ROW_PITCH = NODE_HEIGHT + NODE_GAP;
```

**New module-private constants:**

```ts
/** How far left of a column's left edge the tier's spine runs, in world units. */
const SPINE_GUTTER = 8;
/** Where a spine starts: just under the tier's `N skills` line, which sits at y = 41. */
const SPINE_TOP = 46;
/** Passes that pull from one side at a time, prerequisites then dependents, alternating. */
const ALTERNATING_SWEEPS = 4;
/** Passes that pull from both sides at once, run after the alternating ones. */
const SETTLING_SWEEPS = 4;
```

Those two sweep counts were chosen by measurement, not by taste: the layout has converged by then
(six and eight alternating passes give an identical drawing) and it is the setting with the least
total vertical line-length of any tried. Do not tune them.

**`GraphTier` (line 72) gains one field:**

```ts
export type GraphTier = {
  depth: number;
  title: string;
  count: number;
  /** Left edge of the column, in world units. */
  x: number;
  /**
   * The hairline from under the tier's heading down to the foot of its last box, in world units.
   * A column may now start hundreds of units below its heading, and without this a reader at the
   * top of the drawing sees `FOUNDATIONS` with nothing under it. It runs in the gutter left of the
   * column, so it never passes behind a box or a line.
   */
  spine: { x: number; top: number; bottom: number };
};
```

`GraphNode` (line 44) does not change. Its `y` is still "top-left of the box, in world units"; it is
simply no longer a multiple of `ROW_PITCH` from `PAD_TOP`. The comment at line 162 - "Placed below,
once the column is sorted: a seat is an index within its own tier" - is now wrong and must be
rewritten.

**New exported pure function, and the one piece worth reading twice:**

```ts
/**
 * `wanted` placed in the order it was given, at least `ROW_PITCH` apart, as close to `wanted` as
 * that allows.
 *
 * `wanted` must already be ascending in the order the column is to be drawn. This is isotonic
 * regression by pool-adjacent-violators over `wanted[i] - i * ROW_PITCH`: it minimises the total
 * squared distance from where each box wanted to be, subject to the boxes not overlapping. A
 * simpler "push the next one down if it collides" pass would shove a whole run of boxes below where
 * any of them wanted to be, every time the first of the run was crowded.
 */
export function settle(wanted: readonly number[]): number[];
```

Implementation, exactly:

```ts
export function settle(wanted: readonly number[]): number[] {
  const blocks: { total: number; count: number; mean: number }[] = [];
  wanted.forEach((value, index) => {
    blocks.push({ total: value - index * ROW_PITCH, count: 1, mean: value - index * ROW_PITCH });
    while (blocks.length > 1 && blocks[blocks.length - 2].mean > blocks[blocks.length - 1].mean) {
      const upper = blocks.pop()!;
      const lower = blocks.pop()!;
      const merged = { total: lower.total + upper.total, count: lower.count + upper.count, mean: 0 };
      merged.mean = merged.total / merged.count;
      blocks.push(merged);
    }
  });
  const placed: number[] = [];
  for (const block of blocks) {
    for (let index = 0; index < block.count; index += 1) {
      placed.push(block.mean + placed.length * ROW_PITCH);
    }
  }
  return placed;
}
```

`settle([])` returns `[]`; `settle([n])` returns `[n]`.

**The seating pass**, a module-private function called from `buildMagicGraph`:

```ts
/**
 * Where every skill sits inside its own tier.
 *
 * Sets `node.y` on every node of `columns` and returns each column re-ordered top to bottom. The
 * topmost box in the whole drawing ends at y = 0; `buildMagicGraph` adds `PAD_TOP`.
 *
 * The ordinary barycentre sweep a layered drawing does, with the coordinates carried through it
 * rather than thrown away between passes: a skill wants to sit at the mean height of its
 * neighbours, and `settle` is what turns a column of wants into a column of seats.
 */
function seatColumns(
  columns: readonly (readonly GraphNode[])[],
  standsOn: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>,
  carries: ReadonlyMap<string, readonly string[]>
): GraphNode[][];
```

`standsOn` maps a tag to the tags of its prerequisites; `carries` is its reverse. Both are built
from `tree` before any seating, and both hold only tags the tree actually has - the same
`at.get(need.tag) === undefined` guard the edge loop already uses at line 202, applied to tags.

The procedure, which the numbers below depend on being followed exactly:

1. **A skill is loose** when `standsOn` and `carries` are both empty for it - nothing to pull it
   anywhere. Take every loose skill out of its column for the whole of steps 2 to 5. In the shipped
   ruleset that is `MANI` alone.
2. **Seed:** each column sorted by `name.localeCompare`, `y = index * ROW_PITCH`.
3. **`ALTERNATING_SWEEPS` passes.** Pass 0, 2, ... pull from prerequisites and visit the columns
   left to right; pass 1, 3, ... pull from dependents and visit them right to left.
4. **`SETTLING_SWEEPS` passes.** Each pulls from prerequisites *and* dependents together, one mean
   over both lists, visiting the columns left to right.
5. **One pass over one column** is: for each skill in the column, `wanted` is the mean of the
   current `y` of every tag in its reference list for this pass, or the skill's own current `y` when
   that list is empty; sort the column ascending by `wanted`, ties by `name.localeCompare`; call
   `settle` on the sorted wants; write the results back. **Read `y` live, not from a snapshot taken
   at the start of the sweep** - a column must see what the column before it in this sweep just did.
   That is what a Sugiyama sweep is, and the numbers below are its numbers.
6. **Normalise:** subtract the smallest `y` so the topmost connected box sits at 0.
7. **Loose skills** go back, at the foot of their own column, after everything else, in
   `name.localeCompare` order among themselves. The first sits `2 * ROW_PITCH` below the lowest
   connected box in that column - one blank row of clear air - and each further one `ROW_PITCH`
   below the last. A column with no connected skills at all starts them at 0.
8. **Round to whole world units**, walking each column top to bottom: `y = Math.round(y)`, then
   `y = Math.max(y, previousY + ROW_PITCH)`. `ROW_PITCH` is an integer, so this keeps the separation
   exact and leaves the markup free of `470.40000000000003`. Normalise once more afterwards.

Then in `buildMagicGraph`: `node.y = PAD_TOP + seatY`, the tier's `x` as today, and

```ts
spine: {
  x: x - SPINE_GUTTER,
  top: SPINE_TOP,
  bottom: Math.max(...column.map((node) => node.y + NODE_HEIGHT))
}
```

**The height formula changes** (line 227). It is `PAD_TOP + tallest * (NODE_HEIGHT + NODE_GAP) +
PAD_BOTTOM` today, which counts a trailing gap that is not there. It becomes the real extent:

```ts
height: nodes.length === 0
  ? 0
  : Math.max(...nodes.map((node) => node.y + NODE_HEIGHT)) + PAD_BOTTOM
```

That is 1095 rather than 1100 for the shipped ruleset. `width` (line 226) does not change.

