## Known traps

- **`Math.min(...[])` is `Infinity` and `Math.max(...[])` is `-Infinity`.** The empty-ruleset case is
  real and already tested: `buildMagicTree` returns no branches for a ruleset scraped before `magic`
  was added, and `it("draws nothing for a ruleset with no magic skills")` pins that the graph comes
  back with zero nodes, zero tiers and zero size. Every normalise, every height and every
  `spine.bottom` in the new code spreads an array that is empty in exactly that case. Guard the whole
  seating pass on there being any nodes at all, and keep `width`/`height` returning 0.
- **The order of the two passes in `buildMagicGraph` is now load-bearing.** The edge loop at lines 200-219 reads
  coordinates off `at`, so it must stay *after* seating; the adjacency the seating needs must be
  built from `tree` *before* it, from tags only. Getting this backwards gives an edge list drawn
  against the seats the nodes had before they moved, and the drawing looks almost right.
- **`within` and `crossing` are one list.** `magicTree.ts` never exposes the raw prerequisite list,
  and every depth-1 skill's foundations are filed under `within` on purpose. Read either alone when
  building `standsOn` and edges vanish silently - the comment at lines 197-199 is there because it
  has already been paid for once.
- **Read `y` live during a sweep.** Snapshotting the whole map at the start of a pass and averaging
  over the snapshot is a different algorithm with different output, and it converges more slowly.
  The reference numbers above are from the live read.
- **This package has no jsdom** (`ah-nass`, and `packages/shared/src/testing/README.md`). Component
  tests render with `renderToStaticMarkup` and reach into the markup with `findByTestId` or string
  slicing; `MagicGraphDrawing` exists as the hook-free half precisely so a test can enter it. Test
  the spine through `MagicGraphDrawing`, never `MagicGraphView`.
- **A static render sets no `--graph-scale`**, which is why `constant()` at `MagicGraphView.tsx:61`
  carries a `, 1` fallback. `SPINE_STYLE` must go through `constant` like every other stroke, or the
  hairline thickens as the reader zooms in.
- **Floating point in a pinned drawing.** Step 8's rounding is not cosmetic: without it `node.y`
  reaches the DOM as `470.40000000000003`, every snapshot of the markup becomes fragile, and the
  minimum-separation assertion has to be written with an epsilon. Round, then re-enforce the
  separation - in that order, since rounding two values that were exactly `ROW_PITCH` apart can
  bring them 1 unit closer.
- **`stroke-edge-soft` has no other use in the codebase yet.** It is generated by Tailwind v4 from
  `--color-edge-soft` in the `@theme` block at `packages/shared/src/theme.css:36-41`, the same way
  `stroke-edge` is. If the hairline comes out invisible, check that the class name reached a file
  Tailwind scans before assuming the colour is wrong.
- **The graph is a picture, deliberately.** The root `<svg>` is `role="img"` with a label pointing at
  the Branches view, and every skill group is `aria-hidden` (`MagicGraphView.tsx:194-197, 308`). The
  spine is decoration inside that picture and needs no label of its own; do not give it one, and do
  not make anything here focusable.

