## User-facing decisions

Discussed with the navigator over three rounds. Mockups (scratch, not committed):
`ah-yqzq-a-plain.html`, `ah-yqzq-b-pill.html`, `ah-yqzq-c-underline.html`, `ah-yqzq-r2.html`,
`ah-yqzq-r3-type.html`. **The committed drawing is `docs/ui/ah-yqzq-branch-rows.html`**, and it is
the one to build against.

**1 — The crossing `+TAG n` prerequisite. Chosen: a tinted violet pill (option B).**
The earlier mockup drew it as plain violet text with `margin-left:auto` and no border, but in the
application it is clickable — following it moves the tree to the skill it names — so a straight port
would have shipped a control that looks like a label. Rejected: **A**, exactly the mockup, plain text
with a hover underline and a focus ring only (narrowest, but silent about being clickable at rest);
**C**, plain text carrying a permanent dotted underline (an affordance costing no width, but a fifth
kind of decoration on a row that already has four). The pill was chosen because it puts the crossing
prerequisite in the same visual family as the standing chip beside it. Its violet is used nowhere
else in the app, so it never reads as a fifth standing state.

**2 — Where the pill sits. Chosen: right-aligned, as the mockup (P1).**
`ml-auto` on the first one, so the pills line up down the right edge of a card whatever the skill
names do. Rejected: **P2**, inline immediately after the standing chip, which keeps the pill closer
to the skill it qualifies but loses the column. Engineering (`ENGR`) is the one skill with two, and
they sit side by side at the right end on the normal 6px gap.

**3 — The skill name. Chosen: map blue, `--color-select` (N2).**
It is painted `text-accent` today and there is no such token, so it draws nothing and the door to the
dictionary is invisible. Map blue is what the app already uses for the active item, and it does not
collide with the four state colours — `select` appears on a `can study` row as a dashed *left edge*,
never as text. Rejected: **N1**, brass (`--color-brass-bright`), the earlier mockup's own emphasis
colour, declined because brass already means selection and emphasis in the chrome; **N3**, leave the
name in the row's ink and merely delete the dead class, declined because the name stays an invisible
door; **filing it as a separate bead**, declined because the dead class is on the very line this bead
rewrites.

**4 — Column width. Chosen: 21rem, as the mockup.**
`columns-[19rem]` today. Two extra rem is roughly one chip's width and is part of why the mockup's
rows fit where the shipped ones wrap. Rejected: keeping 19rem and relying on the flex row alone.

**5 — A locked row. Chosen: `opacity: 0.55` applied over full-strength ink (L3).**
32 of 70 skills are locked for a typical mage, so this is most of the tree. The fade is the mockup's
mechanism and it is what says at a glance how much of a branch is shut. What was decided is what it
fades *from*: the row's tag and gate are set to `text-ink` and the row is faded, which lands them at
**4.01:1** on the raised panel — the row visibly recedes and still reads when you look at it.
Rejected: **L1**, the earlier mockup exactly, which fades the row *and* paints the name `#5d6a79`
and the tag/gate `#4a5563`, stacking to 1.60:1 and 1.33:1; **L2**, fading today's `text-ink-dim`, at
2.61:1 throughout. The navigator saw all three drawn with the measured ratios under each. The row's
own `text-ink-dim` stays on the element as the name's inherited colour before the name's own
`text-select` overrides it; the locked name ends at 2.55:1, which was accepted.

*Note for whoever reads this later:* none of the three reaches the 4.5:1 floor the rest of the app
holds, and `theme.test.ts` cannot see it, because opacity is not a token. This was chosen knowingly.
Do not "fix" it by removing the fade.

**6 — The light theme. Chosen: the five designed light counterparts, as drawn.**
The earlier mockup only ever drew the dark ground. The values are in *Files to change* and in the
committed mockup's table. Rejected: washing the fills further towards paper; and dropping the fill
on light entirely, keeping border and ink only — declined because the two themes would then differ
in kind rather than in value.

**7 — How small the tag, gate and chips go. Chosen: 9.5px, exactly the mockup (X1).**
The navigator was shown the three sizes side by side in the application's real font, and told that
`ah-v09e` lifted the scale to a 12px floor after an eye-strain report and that `theme.test.ts`
refuses absolute pixel type in a component. Rejected: **X3**, 9.5px for the tag and gate but 12px for
the chips, on the grounds that a chip carries a sentence and a tag carries four letters; **X2**, 12px
throughout, adding no rung below the floor. The skill name stays at the pane's 13px in all three.

**Words.** Nothing changes. Every string — `at 4, ceiling 5`, `at 3, held by pattern`,
`at 5, the highest there is`, `can study`, the tally, `also needs <name> at level <n>` — is what
`standingWords` and `tally` already produce, and the navigator was not asked to revisit any of them.

**One thing not asked about, and why.** The mockup draws skill names in Helvetica while the
application is monospace throughout (`body { font-family: var(--font-mono) }` in `theme.css`).
Making the names sans would be a change to the app's typeface, not to this view, and is far outside
the bead. Every mockup put in front of the navigator was drawn in the app's real mono, so the fit
