## Known traps

- **Two existing assertions go red on the locked row, and they are the point rather than a
  nuisance.** `MagicTreeDialog.test.tsx:239` and `tests/smoke/magic-standing.spec.ts:44` both assert
  that `magic-tree-skill-CRRI` carries `text-ink-dim`. Under the agreed fade the row carries
  `text-ink-dim opacity-55`, so the regex `/text-ink-dim/` still matches — but the *meaning* has
  changed, and an assertion that passes for the wrong reason is worse than one that fails. Amend both
  to assert `opacity-55`, which is the thing that now says "locked". Do not delete them.
- **A hex in a `.tsx` fails the suite.** `theme.test.ts`'s *keeps every colour in the token system*
  walks every non-test source under `packages/shared/src` for `#rrggbb`. Every chip colour goes
  through a token.
- **`text-[9.5px]` fails the suite too**, via *sets no pane type in absolute pixels*, which also
  catches `fontSize: "9.5px"` in an inline style. The rung is `--text-pane-xs` and the class is
  `text-pane-xs`.
- **A token added to `@theme` without a light counterpart fails** *gives every dark token a light
  counterpart*. Fifteen tokens, thirty lines.
- **Tailwind's scanner reads source text, so an interpolated class name is never emitted.** This is
  why `ROW_STYLE` spells every class out and why the depth indent is an inline `paddingLeft`. The
  same rule kills `bg-standing-${kind}-fill`.
- **The scanner also does not follow the workspace link.** `theme.css`'s `@source` lines name the
  shared package and both apps explicitly; nothing here needs changing, but a new class in a new file
  outside those globs would silently render unstyled.
- **`{" "}` between flex items is an anonymous flex item.** The row uses `{" "}` and `ml-1` for
  spacing today; both must go when `gap-1.5` arrives, or every row gains phantom gaps.
- **`min-w-0` on the name is required for it to wrap.** A flex item's default `min-width: auto`
  refuses to shrink below its content, so without it the name pushes the pills off the row instead of
  taking a second line — which is the narrow-window behaviour the navigator chose against.
- **An inline `paddingLeft` beats a `pl-` utility**, whatever Tailwind's layer ordering says, so the
  5px text offset is folded into the `calc()` rather than added as a class.
- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom, by decision (ah-nass).** Its tests are
  `renderToStaticMarkup`, so they can assert on class names and never on layout. Anything about what
  the browser actually does with those classes belongs in `tests/smoke/`.
- **`--text-pane-xs` must not carry `var(--ui-scale)`.** The root font size already multiplies every
  rem in the application; a multiplier in the token applies the Interface size setting twice (ah-ziv).
- **`opacity-55`, `text-pane-xs` and the `bg-/text-/border-standing-*` utilities all compile** under
  the Tailwind 4.3 in this repository — checked with `@tailwindcss/cli` against a probe file before
  this plan was written. `opacity-55` emits `opacity: 55%`. No arbitrary-value syntax is needed for
  any of them.
- **`bd show <id> --json` returns an array.** Read this plan back with
  `jq -r '.[0].design'`, not `.design`.

## Context

The units pane stopped meaning "the units in the selected hex" in `ah-1mpx.2`. It now has a source
rail down its left edge, and **four** sources: `This hex`, `All my units`, `Other factions`
(`ah-1mpx.5`) and one per Army. Three of those four list units standing in **different hexes**, and
each of the three carries a `Hex` column saying where — `extraColumnsFor`
(`packages/shared/src/workspace/unitSource.ts:99-112`) is the rule that gives them one.

Clicking a row in such a list selects the unit and leaves the map exactly where it was. So having
found one of your units among 38, or somebody else's among 214, there is **no way onward to the
ground it is standing on**: the `Hex` column is plain text, and the map must be searched by eye.
Every other list in the application already travels — the command palette, the turn-messages panel,
the problems panel and `Alt`+`Arrow` all go through `AppShell`'s `goToUnit`
(`AppShell.tsx:939-965`). The units table is the one that does not. Psylocke found it while
verifying `ah-1mpx.3`; it is pre-existing and cost a verification session a wrong step.

When this lands, a plain press on a row — and `Enter` on it — in `All my units`, `Other factions`
or an Army takes the map to the unit's hex, following it to another level if it is on one.
`This hex` is untouched, and so is every gesture that builds a multi-row pick.

**The mockup is `docs/ui/ah-y9hx-travel.html`** (merged in #770 and #772). It draws all seven cases
this plan implements. Read it before starting; it is the agreed design and it is not repeated in
full here.

**Two beads landed on this exact file after the first three rounds of the interview**, and both are
already on `main`:

- `ah-1mpx.5` added the `Other factions` source and `UnitSource`'s fourth member, `{ kind: "foreign" }`.
- `ah-1mpx.4` moved row selection from `click` to **`pointerdown`** and made a press build a *pick*:
  `Ctrl`/`Cmd`-click toggles one row, `Shift`-click extends a run, and the pick is dragged onto an
  Army. `UnitTableDock.tsx:2068` is now `onPointerDown={(event) => onPress(event, unit, rowTarget)}`
  and there is **no `onClick` on the row at all**.

A fourth interview round settled both. Everything below is written against `main` as it stands
after them.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### 1. `packages/shared/src/workspace/unitSource.ts` — the rule

This module is "which list the dock is showing, and **every rule that depends on it**", kept pure
precisely so it can be tested (`packages/shared` has no jsdom). The new rule belongs here and
nowhere else. Add, beside `extraColumnsFor`:

```ts
/**
 * Whether choosing a unit in this source should take the map to the unit's hex (`ah-y9hx`).
 *
 * True for every source that spans hexes, which is exactly the set `extraColumnsFor` gives a `hex`
 * column to: `All my units`, `Other factions` and an Army each list units standing in different
 * places, and the column that says where was the only route onward there was. False for
 * `This hex`, where every row is already in the hex on screen, so travelling there could only pull
 * the map back from wherever the player had dragged it.
 */
export function travelsOnSelect(source: UnitSource): boolean {
  return source.kind !== "hex";
}
```

`source.kind !== "hex"` rather than a four-arm `switch`: a fifth source that spans hexes should
travel by default, and the test below ties the answer to `extraColumnsFor` so the two cannot drift.

### 2. `packages/shared/src/workspace/UnitTableDock.tsx` — the wiring

Everything the component needs already exists; nothing new crosses the boundary from `AppShell`.

- **`onSelectUnit`** (prop, declared at `UnitTableDock.tsx:169-170`, "Selects a unit and opens its
  orders") is `AppShell`'s `goToUnit(unitId, null)` (`AppShell.tsx:3847`). `goToUnit` switches the
  level if the unit is on another one, then calls
  `selectRegion(regionId, unitId, { picked: true })` and `selectUnit(unitId)`. The `picked` flag is
  what makes the map travel **even when the target hex is the one already selected**
  (`workspaceStore.ts`, `pickEpoch`; `ah-lqct`) — which is decision H1, obtained for free.
- **`settleOn`** (`UnitTableDock.tsx:652-655`) is the single funnel for "this row became the
  cursor": `setPick(pickNext); selectUnit(rowTarget);`. **Do not put travel inside it.** It is
  called for `Shift`- and `Ctrl`/`Cmd`-modified presses too, and those must not move the map (P1).

Add `travelsOnSelect` to the existing `import { ... } from "./unitSource";` at the top of the file,
and one helper **immediately after `settleOn` (`:655`) and before `onRowKeyDown` (`:657`)** — both
`onRowKeyDown` and `pressRow` call it, and these are `const` arrow functions in the component body,
so it has to be declared above the first of them:

```ts
/**
 * Takes the map to a unit's hex, for a list that spans hexes (`ah-y9hx`).
 *
 * Called *after* `settleOn`, never instead of it. `onSelectUnit` is the shell's `goToUnit`, which
 * does nothing at all for a unit this turn's report does not have - a remembered Army member - so
 * a row that cannot be travelled to must already have been selected by the time this runs
 * (decision S1: such a row still highlights, and nothing is said).
 *
 * Deliberately not called from `moveSelection`, from Space, or from a press with a modifier held:
 * walking a list and building a pick must both leave the map alone.
 */
const travelTo = (unitId: string) => {
  if (travelsOnSelect(source)) {
    onSelectUnit?.(unitId);
  }
};
```

**`pressRow`** (`UnitTableDock.tsx:766-791`) becomes:

```ts
  const pressRow = (
    event: PointerEvent<HTMLTableRowElement>,
    unit: ReportUnit,
    rowTarget: string
  ) => {
    if (event.button !== 0) {
      return;
    }
    const modifiers = { shift: event.shiftKey, mod: isMac ? event.metaKey : event.ctrlKey };
    // A modified press is building the pick, not choosing a row, so it leaves the map where it is
    // (`ah-y9hx` P1): a five-unit pick across four hexes would otherwise throw the map four times,
    // and one Shift+click adding ten rows would send it to the last of them.
    const plain = !modifiers.shift && !modifiers.mod;
    const outcome = onPress(pick, unit.unitId, modifiers, rowIds);
    if (outcome.now) {
      settleOn(outcome.now, rowTarget);
      if (plain) {
        travelTo(rowTarget);
      }
    }
    if (!outcome.draggable || !byMouse(event)) {
      return;
    }
    const deferred = outcome.onRelease;
    // A deferred settle can only follow a plain press - `onPress` returns `onRelease` only after
    // its shift and mod branches have both returned - so it travels unconditionally, and for the
    // same reason a plain press does: a release that never became a drag *is* a plain click.
    beginDrag(
      event,
      unit,
      deferred
        ? () => {
            settleOn(deferred, rowTarget);
            travelTo(rowTarget);
          }
        : undefined
    );
  };
```

`isMac` is already in scope (`UnitTableDock.tsx:283`). `travelTo` is given `rowTarget`, not
`unit.unitId`: `rowTarget` is `silver?.formed?.formedBy ?? unit.unitId`, the id `settleOn` selects,
so what the map goes to and what the row selects can never disagree.

**`onRowKeyDown`** (`UnitTableDock.tsx:657-708`): `chose` takes a flag, and only `Enter` passes it.

```ts
    const chose = (travel: boolean) => {
      if (here === null) {
        return;
      }
      // Choosing a row from the keyboard collapses a pick exactly as a plain click does.
      settleOn(afterGesture(pick, { kind: "plain", unitId: here }, rowIds), here);
      if (travel) {
        travelTo(here);
      }
    };
```

and in the `keys` map, replacing the two existing entries:

```ts
