## User-facing decisions

The whole interview, over four rounds. `docs/ui/ah-y9hx-travel.html` is the agreed design; the
scratchpad option pages are not committed and are gone.

**Round 1 — what should a row click do?** Three options, mocked side by side.

- **Chosen: A, the row travels.** A click in a hex-spanning source selects the unit *and* takes the
  map to its hex. Reason given: one rule for the whole application, and nothing new to discover —
  the palette, turn messages, problems panel and `Alt`+`Arrow` already behave this way, and the
  units table was the only list that did not.
- Rejected: **B, the `Hex` cell becomes a link.** The row would keep selecting only and the
  `(15,63)` in the `Hex` column would become a button that travels. Rejected because it is a 79px
  target (`EXTRA_COLUMN_SHARES.hex`), a second thing to hit in a row you were about to click
  anyway, and unreachable by keyboard without breaking the table's one-tab-stop-per-row rule
  (the same reason the faction-name button carries `tabIndex={-1}`, Copilot #478).
- Rejected: **C, double click or `Enter` travels, single click selects.** Rejected as
  undiscoverable — nothing on screen says the double click exists, so a player who never tries it
  keeps the bug — and because double click on a row is used nowhere else in the application.

**Round 1 — does `This hex` change?** **No. `This hex` is untouched**, mouse and keyboard alike:
clicking or `Enter`ing a row there never moves the map, even when the player has panned away from
the selected hex. Rejected: one rule everywhere, which would have made a click in `This hex` snap
the map back.

**Round 1 — do the arrow keys travel?** **No.** `ArrowUp`/`ArrowDown`/`Home`/`End` move the
selection and leave the map alone. Rejected: arrows travel too, because holding `ArrowDown` through
38 units would redraw the map 38 times.

**Round 2 — a row whose unit this turn's report does not have** (a remembered Army member, greyed,
`Seen: turn 68`). **Chosen: S1 — the row still highlights, the map does not move, and nothing is
said.** Exactly today's behaviour for that row; the greyed text and the `Seen` column are the
explanation, and the `Remove` button — usually why the row was clicked — stays reachable. Rejected:
**S2**, a header status line reading "3080 is not in this turn's report — last seen turn 68.",
because it would fire on every click of a row that already says twice over that it is stale.

**Round 2 — a unit on another level.** **Chosen: L1 — travel follows the unit down, and the level
chip and the whole map change with it.** It is what `goToUnit` already does for every other caller,
and "where it stands" has to be honest or the rule is not one. Rejected: **L2**, never changing the
level from a row, which would bring the bug straight back for every unit not on the level being
looked at and make the units table the only list that refuses to follow a unit down.

**Round 2 — the unit is already in the selected hex, and the map has been dragged away.**
**Chosen: H1 — the map snaps back to it.** No exception: a row press always ends with the unit's
hex on screen. This is what `pickEpoch` and `selectRegion`'s `{ picked: true }` already do
(`ah-lqct`), so it needs no code of its own. Rejected: **H2**, moving only when the hex actually
changes, because two rows in one list would then behave differently for a reason nothing on screen
shows.

**Round 3 — how does a keyboard user reach the hex?** **Chosen: K1 — `Enter` travels; `Space` is
unchanged.** Without it the table has no keyboard route to a hex at all: it is one tab stop per row
with a roving `tabIndex`, so a keyboard player could find a unit and never reach its ground.
Rejected: **K2**, `Enter` and `Space` both travelling, because `Space` is also how a reader stops a
list scrolling and a mis-hit must not move the map. Rejected: **K3**, no keyboard travel at all,
which leaves a keyboard-only player with the bug exactly as reported — `Alt`+`Arrow` walks every own
unit in report order, not the row you arrowed to in a filtered, sorted list.

**Round 3 — `Enter` under `This hex`.** **Unchanged.** It keeps meaning exactly what it means today.

**Round 4 — `Other factions` (`ah-1mpx.5`, landed after round 1).** **Chosen: F1 — it travels, like
every other list that spans hexes.** One rule stated once, and finding where somebody else's army is
standing is most of what that list is for. Rejected: **F2**, only the player's own lists travelling,
because the `Hex` column would then say where a unit is in a list you cannot get to it from — which
is precisely this bug, left standing in one source.

**Round 4 — a modified press (`ah-1mpx.4`, landed after round 1).** **Chosen: P1 — only a plain
press travels.** `Ctrl`/`Cmd`-click and `Shift`-click build the pick and never move the map, so
assembling a force is one gesture with one outcome. Rejected: **P2**, every press travelling,
because a five-unit pick across four hexes would throw the map four times and one `Shift`-click
adding ten rows would send it to the last of them.

**Round 4 — `Enter` while a multi-row pick is standing.** **Chosen: it always travels**, collapsing
the pick to the cursor row exactly as a plain click does. One meaning for `Enter` in every state.
Rejected: `Enter` travelling only when no pick stands, which would make it mean two different things
one press apart.

**No new user-visible strings.** Nothing is added, removed or reworded anywhere on screen: every
label, heading, empty-state line and error message stays exactly as it is. The whole of this bead is
a change in what a gesture does.

## Out of scope

- **The `Hex` column does not become a link.** That was round 1's rejected option B, and adding it
  as well would put a second control in a 79px cell.
- **`This hex` gains nothing**, including a way to re-centre the map on the selected hex after
  panning. That is a real want and it is a different bead.
- **No smoke test for the stale Army member (S1).** The guarantee is structural — `settleOn` runs
  before `travelTo`, always — and it cannot be reached cheaply from a browser test: no own unit
  disappears between the consecutive fixtures the smoke suite uses (all 27 own units in `g7f95t71`
  are still own units in `g7f95t72`), so building one would need a `t72` → `t74` import across a
  missing turn, whose behaviour nothing currently pins. Keep the ordering comment in `travelTo`;
  it is what a later reader needs.
- **No change to `goToUnit`.** Its early return for a unit with no known region stays, and is what
  decision S1 relies on.
- **No new prop on `UnitTableDock`.** `onSelectUnit` already carries `goToUnit`.
- **The unit-id cell button is not rewired**, and `Escape`, the right-click menu, the drag-to-Army
  gesture and the bulk line are all untouched.

