## Known traps

- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom, and never will.** A component test there renders with
  `renderToStaticMarkup`: no effects, no refs, no timers, and `findByTestId`'s element walk cannot
  enter a component that uses hooks — which `UnitTableDock` does, heavily. Four beads paid about
  half an hour each discovering this. Do not write a `UnitTableDock.test.tsx` case that presses a
  row. The rule goes in `unitSource.ts` and the gesture goes in the smoke suite;
  `packages/shared/src/testing/README.md` has the pattern.
- **The row has no `onClick` any more.** `ah-1mpx.4` moved selection to `pointerdown`
  (`UnitTableDock.tsx:2068`) so that a press on a row already in the pick can defer what it means
  until it is known whether it became a drag. Wiring travel to a `click` handler would fire after
  the drag has already begun, and would not fire at all for the deferred-release path.
- **`settleOn` is shared with the modified presses.** It looks like the natural home for travel and
  it is the wrong one: `pressRow` routes `Shift`- and `Ctrl`/`Cmd`-modified presses through it, and
  P1 says those must not move the map. Increment 2's `Ctrl`-click test is the guard.
- **The units table windows its rows.** A unit far down a 27-row list is absent from the DOM
  entirely, so `getByTestId("unit-row-13401")` fails until the filter narrows to it. Assert counts
  or filter first; never assume a row is present because the list contains it
  (`workspace.spec.ts:2083-2086` says this in the suite's own words).
- **Two units in `g7f95t71` are called "Seven of Eight"** — `18642` in `(7,53)` and `13401` in
  `(26,52)`. Filter and assert on the number.
- **`unitTable.ts` is stored with a byte `grep` treats as binary**, so a plain `grep` over it prints
  nothing and exits 0 — which reads exactly like "not found". Use `grep -a` on that file.
- **`goToUnit` can change the map's level mid-gesture**, and `setLevel` clears the selection when
  it does — which is why `goToUnit` sets the level first and re-selects afterwards. `settleOn` has
  already run by then, so the row's selection is written twice and ends correct. Do not "optimise"
  by calling `travelTo` before `settleOn`: the order is what decision S1 rests on.
- **Travelling changes the selected hex, which changes the `This hex` list — and nothing else.** The
  pick is cleared only when `source` changes and narrowed only when `rowIds` change, and neither
  happens for `All my units`, `Other factions` or an Army when the hex moves. If a future change
  makes a hex-spanning source's rows depend on the selected hex, this bead's rule would start
  clearing the pick underneath the player.
- **Focus must stay on the row after travelling.** The row keeps `tabIndex={0}` because it stays
  selected, and no refocus is armed, so this should hold by construction — but it is the thing to
  look at first if the keyboard walk starts behaving oddly. It is checked by hand, not asserted.
- **WebKit's driver answers `""` for text it considers clipped**, so a Chromium assertion can pass
  while the native shell shows nothing. Nothing here asserts on clipped text, but the `native` job
  is the one that would tell you.
- **The smoke suite rebuilds the web bundle with the service worker disabled**, so a PWA run
  straight afterwards fails with timeouts that look like a broken worker.

## Context

The header can carry **three amber chips at once** — what the engine reported went wrong
(`turn-messages-chip`), what order validation found across the map (`problems-chip`), and what the
parser could not read (`unreadable-chip`) — and each of them costs the strip another 130–200px. This
is the second of two passes at **ah-30hg**; read the parent epic's design first, and then
**ah-30hg.1**'s, because this bead is written against the code `.1` left behind.

**`ah-30hg.1` has merged** (#761, commit `f05127c`). Every line number below was read from that
commit, after the merge, and not guessed at. It took `ATLANTIS HUD`, the words `Turn` and `Faction`
and the turn's date out of the strip, added `data-testid="header-game-state"` to the game-state
group, and made the budget guard wait for the settled header. The header is now **73px on the
desktop shell and 37px on web**, against a `HEADER_BUDGET_PX` that is still 91.

**What is left, and it is exactly 4.75px.** The game-state group is 975.84px of the 1256px
available, so the chips are on one line; the *actions* group (Import/Export/Send/⚙) wants
1260.75px alongside them and so keeps a row of its own on the desktop shell. Folding the three amber
chips into one closes that, with room to spare:

| chip | text | width |
|---|---|---|
| removed — `turn-messages-chip` | `⚠ 1 error · 333 events ▾` | 202.06 |
| removed — `problems-chip` | `⚠ 10 problems ▾` | 131.60 |
| added — the folded chip | `⚠ 11 to check ▾` | 131.60 |
| plus one 14px `gap-3.5` no longer between two items | | 14.00 |
| **saved** | | **216.06** |

975.84 − 216.06 = **759.78**, and with the actions group 1044.73 of 1256 — **211px spare**. So the
header becomes **37px in both projects**, and `HEADER_BUDGET_PX` drops from 91 to **46**, which is
the ~125% rule its own comment states applied to 37. That comment already names this bead as the
one that does it (`tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts:1408-1411`).

Widths are arithmetic on a **7.8286px monospace advance at 13px**, the figure the epic measured
against the live page: a chip is `chars × 7.8286 + 18` (px-2 either side, plus 2px of border), and a
trailing `▾` adds `7.8286 + 4` for its `ml-1`.

**One measured thing that looks like a risk and is not.** The chip is present the moment a report
is, but its *text* still changes ~310ms later, when the async validation pass lands and the count
goes from whatever the engine and the parser contributed to the full total — typically
`Turn report` → `⚠ 11 to check`, two characters wider. That is 15.66px on a group with 496px of
slack, so it cannot wrap the header; the chip no longer appears from nothing, which is what did.

**The second thing this buys, and the reason the navigator chose it over trimming words, is that the
header stops growing.** A fourth advisory would now cost nothing at all: it becomes a tab in a panel
rather than a chip in a strip. That is what ah-csni's budget has been tripped by, repeatedly, from
beads that never mentioned the header.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### New — `packages/shared/src/turnReport.ts`

Every string and every rule the folded chip and its panel need, as pure functions. This is the
pattern `unreadableLines.ts` already establishes and states its reason for: *"`packages/shared` has
no jsdom by decision (ah-nass), so a pure module is the only place the wording can actually be
unit-tested; the panel and the chip render what these return."* Everything the navigator agreed in
the interview is here, and nowhere else.

```ts
/** The four things one turn can want checked, in the order the panel shows them. */
export type TurnReportTab = "problems" | "engine" | "unreadable" | "events";

/** What the four folded sources each amount to, for the chip and the tab row. */
export type TurnReportCounts = {
  /** Order validation, over the whole map - `problemsByHex` summed. */
  problems: number;
  /** "Errors during turn", as the engine printed them. */
  engine: number;
  /** Lines of the loaded report the parser could not read. */
  unreadable: number;
  /** "Events during turn". Counted for its own tab, never as something wrong. */
  events: number;
};

/** Fixable first, then what already happened - the navigator's P1 ordering, round 2. */
export const TURN_REPORT_TABS: readonly TurnReportTab[] = [
  "problems",
  "engine",
  "unreadable",
  "events"
];

/** Things wrong: problems + engine + unreadable. Events are not warnings (navigator, round 2). */
export function turnReportTotal(counts: TurnReportCounts): number;

/** How many rows one tab has. */
export function turnReportCount(counts: TurnReportCounts, tab: TurnReportTab): number;

/**
 * The chip's visible text: `11 to check`, or `Turn report` when nothing is wrong.
 *
 * The `⚠` is a separate `aria-hidden` span at the render site, exactly as the two chips this
 * replaces already do it - so it is not in this string.
 */
export function turnReportChipLabel(counts: TurnReportCounts): string;

/** Whether the chip is amber. True exactly when `turnReportTotal` is above zero. */
export function turnReportIsWarning(counts: TurnReportCounts): boolean;

/** `Problems 10`, `Engine 1`, `Not read 6`, `Events 333`. */
export function turnReportTabLabel(tab: TurnReportTab, counts: TurnReportCounts): string;

/** The panel's one header line, which names the open tab. */
export function turnReportHeading(
  tab: TurnReportTab,
  context: {
    counts: TurnReportCounts;
    /** How many hexes the problems are spread over - the Problems line says it. */
    hexCount: number;
    /** The bare turn number, e.g. "71". Null when the report's header gives none. */
    turnLabel: string | null;
  }
): string;

/** The sentence under the body, or null for a tab that has none. */
export function turnReportFooter(tab: TurnReportTab): string | null;

/**
 * Which tab a fresh open lands on: the remembered one when it still has rows, otherwise the first
 * in `TURN_REPORT_TABS` that has any, otherwise "problems".
 */
export function turnReportOpeningTab(
  remembered: TurnReportTab,
  counts: TurnReportCounts
): TurnReportTab;

/** True when all four counts are zero: no tab row at all, one sentence instead. */
export function turnReportIsSilent(counts: TurnReportCounts): boolean;

/** What a silent turn's panel says instead of a body. */
