## User-facing decisions

**Six rounds with the navigator, 2026-08-27 and 2026-08-28. Rounds 1–3 are recorded in full on the
parent epic ah-30hg and settled the chip's words, the count's definition and the order of the
sections; read them before changing anything visible.** Rounds 4–6 are this bead's, and are recorded
here in full.

Inherited from rounds 1–3, and not to be re-opened:

- **The chip reads `⚠ N to check`**, and **`Turn report`** when nothing is wrong — rejected:
  `⚠ N problems` ("problems" today means order problems only, and this chip is wider than that),
  `⚠ N warnings` (accurate but says nothing about whether you can act), `⚠ N` alone (widest margin,
  but a bare number beside a triangle names nothing).
- **The chip is always present once a report is loaded**, and drops its amber rather than vanishing —
  rejected: `333 events`, hidden when the turn was silent, because the chip's width would move with
  the count and it would appear and disappear under the pointer.
- **The count is things wrong: order problems + engine errors + unreadable lines.** Turn events are
  not warnings and are not counted; they keep a section.
- **Sections read fixable-first**: Order problems, The engine said, Lines not read, Turn events —
  rejected: the turn's own order (engine, events, then orders), which matches how the two panels read
  today but puts the only still-changeable thing last.

### Round 4 — how one panel shows four sections

`docs/ui/ah-30hg-r4-panel.html`, all three at true size (512px) with the real turn-71 numbers.

- **S3 — the four sections are tabs, one body at a time. CHOSEN.** Each body is exactly the panel it
  is today; the panel keeps one height and one footer, and the footer is the open tab's own.
- **S1 — every section in full, one scroll. Rejected**: on a busy turn the events are hundreds of
  rows below the fold, so you scroll past what you have read to reach what you have not.
- **S2 — collapsing sections, all four headings always on screen. Rejected**: reading the engine's
  one error becomes a click it is not today, and the panel's height moves under the pointer.

Also asked in round 4, and answered:

- **The panel opens on whichever tab you had open last.** Rejected: always Order problems (a turn
  with none opens onto nothing), and always the first non-empty one.
- **A completely silent turn says "This turn reported nothing, and your orders look sound."**
  Rejected: "Nothing to check in this turn." (shorter, says less about why), and hiding the chip
  (which reverses round 2).
- **One chip, one tab stop.** Tab reaches the chip, Enter opens it, focus lands in the panel as
  every popover already does, and the tabs are reached inside it. Rejected: a chord that opens it
