**Two effects**, both of which only ever shrink the pick, so their order does not matter:

```tsx
// D2 E1: the filter narrows the pick. `narrowedTo` answers with the identical object when nothing
// was dropped, so this settles in one render.
useEffect(() => {
  setPick((current) => narrowedTo(current, rowIds));
}, [rowIds]);

// A turn load goes through this same effect: `visible` is rebuilt from the new report, so a
// picked unit the new turn does not mention is dropped, which is the right answer and needs no
// case of its own.
// Round 3, G1: changing the source clears the pick outright. Not merely narrowed — two sources can
// share rows (every `This hex` row is also an `All my units` row), and a pick that survived the
// jump would be a pick the player did not make on the list they are now looking at.
useEffect(() => {
  setPick(NO_PICK);
}, [source]);
```

**The keyboard**, in `onRowKeyDown` (`:476`), which already guards `event.target !== event.currentTarget`
and so never fires for the buttons inside a row:

- `Shift+ArrowUp` / `Shift+ArrowDown` → `moveSelection(index ∓ 1, { extend: true })`. `moveSelection`
  (`:466`) gains that option and, when set, also dispatches `{kind:"extend", unitId: target.unitId}`.
  Plain arrows keep today's behaviour **and** dispatch `{kind:"plain", …}`, so arrowing away from a
  pick collapses it.
- `Mod+a` → `event.preventDefault()`, `afterGesture(pick, {kind:"all"}, rowIds)`. **Only here**, on
  the row's own handler, which is why `Ctrl/Cmd+A` inside the filter box still selects the filter's
  text: that input is a different element and this handler never sees its keys. Do not add it to
  `shortcuts.ts`.
- `Escape` → `afterGesture(pick, {kind:"plain", unitId: selectedUnitId}, rowIds)`, and
  `event.preventDefault()`. Round 3: Escape narrows the pick back to the cursor row.
- `Enter` and `Space` keep calling `selectUnit` as they do today (`:487-489`) **and** dispatch
  `{kind:"plain", unitId}` alongside it, so choosing a row from the keyboard collapses a pick
  exactly as a plain click does. Round 3 does not mention them; this is the only answer consistent
  with the click.

**The press**, on the row. `UnitRow` gains `picked: boolean`, `onPress`, and `onContextMenu`, and
**loses its `onClick`** (`:1428`) — selection moves to the pointer, which is what lets a press on a
row already in the pick defer its collapse. **`onSelect` stays**: `onRowKeyDown` still calls it for
`Enter` and `Space`, and the unit-id button inside the row still calls it too.

The row also gains `data-picked={picked}`, which is what the smoke suite asserts the pick on, and
its `aria-selected` becomes `picked || selected` while the `<table role="grid">` (`:687`) gains
`aria-multiselectable={true}`. In a grid, `aria-selected` **is** the selection and focus is the
cursor, so this is the correct reading of both. Nothing observable changes for a pick of one,
where `picked` and `selected` name the same row, and no existing test asserts `aria-selected` on a
unit row.

```tsx
const pressRow = (event: ReactPointerEvent<HTMLTableRowElement>, unit: ReportUnit, rowTarget: string) => {
  if (event.button !== 0) {
    return;
  }
  const outcome = onPress(pick, unit.unitId, { shift: event.shiftKey, mod: isMac ? event.metaKey : event.ctrlKey }, rowIds);
  const settle = (next: UnitPick) => {
    setPick(next);
    selectUnit(rowTarget);
  };
  if (outcome.now) {
    settle(outcome.now);
  }
  // Mouse only. A row is a scrollable surface on a touch screen, and `touch-none` on it would cost
  // the table its scrolling; `selectionGuard.ts` records that touch is not a platform this app
  // serves. A tap still picks, through `outcome.now` above.
  if (!outcome.draggable || !byMouse(event)) {
    return;
  }
  beginDrag(event, unit, outcome.onRelease ? () => settle(outcome.onRelease!) : undefined);
};
```

`beginDrag` follows `ColumnReorderHandle.onPointerDown` (`ColumnReorderHandle.tsx:59-133`) step for
step, and every step of it is there for a reason that file states:

1. `const releaseSelection = guardSelection();` — a drag across rows must not read as a text
   selection.
2. `const layer = pushDismissLayer();` — so Escape means *cancel this drag* even under an open
   dialog's own capture-phase listener.
3. Window-level `pointermove`, `pointerup`, `pointercancel`, and a capture-phase `keydown` that acts
   only when `isTopDismissLayer(layer)`.
4. **A threshold**: the drag has not begun until `Math.hypot(dx, dy) >= 4` from the press point.
   `ColumnReorderHandle` needs none because it lives on a dedicated grip; a row is also a click
   target, so without this every click would be a one-pixel drag. Below the threshold a `pointerup`
   runs the deferred `settle` and nothing else; the chip is never created.
5. On crossing it: resolve the carried rows **once** — `pick.ids.has(unit.unitId) && pick.ids.size >= 2`
   ? `pickedIn(pick, visible)` : `[unit]` — create the chip with its label, and compute
   `full` = every Army whose `alreadyIn(army, carriedIds) === carriedIds.length`.
6. Each `pointermove` calls `chip.moveTo(clientX, clientY)` and hit-tests with
   `document.elementFromPoint(...)?.closest("[data-drop-army],[data-drop-new]")`. `setDrag` is
   called **only when the target actually changes**, so a move within one entry re-renders nothing.
7. `pointerup` over a target: `{kind:"army"}` → `void actions.addUnits(armyId, carried)`;
   `{kind:"new"}` → `dispatch({ type: "new-clicked", withUnits: carried })`. Over nothing, or on
   Escape or `pointercancel`: nothing at all.
8. `end` removes every listener, removes the chip, releases the selection guard, pops the dismiss
   layer and `setDrag(null)` — once, and safe to run twice.

**The right-click**, on the row:

```tsx
onContextMenu={(event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  // The same rule the press follows: a row outside the pick is picked alone first, so what the
  // menu says and what is washed always agree (D1 D4).
  const outcome = onPress(pick, unit.unitId, { shift: false, mod: false }, rowIds);
  const next = outcome.now ?? outcome.onRelease;
  if (next) {
    setPick(next);
    selectUnit(rowTarget);
  }
  setMenu({ at: "pointer", point: { x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY } });
}}
```

The menu renders when `menu?.at === "pointer"` and `acting.length > 0` and `canEditArmies` and
`currentTurn !== null` — the same three conditions that already disable the header trigger
(`:581`). A right-click with no game open, or on a report naming no turn, opens nothing.

**The row's wash** (`:1453`) becomes:

```tsx
selected ? "bg-select/25 text-ink" : picked ? "bg-select/15 text-ink" : unit.own ? "text-ink" : "text-ink-soft"
```

Round 3's numbers exactly. `bg-select/15` is written as one whole literal — Tailwind's scanner reads
source text, so a class assembled from pieces is a class that never ships (`popover.tsx:56-59`).

**Two inner controls need `onPointerDown={(event) => event.stopPropagation()}`**: the per-row
`Remove` (`:1405`) and the warned-silver button (`:1367`). They already stop the *click*; with
selection moved to the pointer, a press on either would otherwise pick the row and arm a drag
underneath them. The unit-id button (`:1243`) deliberately does **not** — selecting the row is what
