## The test plan

`pnpm run check:fast` runs every unit suite; the browser suites are CI's, and this bead is almost
entirely browser behaviour, so run `pnpm run test:smoke` locally as well.

**Unit** — the new `unitPick.test.ts`, `UnitBulkLine.test.tsx`, `unitDragChip.test.ts`,
`UnitContextMenu.test.tsx`, plus new cases in `armies.test.ts`, `armiesStore.test.ts`,
`AddToArmyMenu.test.tsx`, `UnitSourceRail.test.tsx` and `UnitTableDock.test.tsx`, all named above.

**Browser** — new cases appended to `tests/smoke/armies.spec.ts`, which already has the fixture, the
`workspace(page)` helper and the `newArmy(page, name)` helper this needs
(`tests/smoke/armies.spec.ts:13`, `:19-24`, `:26-32`). Nine, and every one is a rule from the interview:

- `"shift-clicking picks the run between two rows, and ctrl-clicking takes one back out"`
- `"the bulk line appears at two picked and takes the header button out of play"` — assert
  `unit-bulk-line` is absent with one row picked, present with two, and that `add-to-army` is
  `disabled` while it is.
- `"ctrl+A picks every row the filter is showing, and typing in the filter narrows the pick"` —
  the D2 E1 rule, and the one most worth pinning: pick everything, type a filter, and assert the
  line's count is the number of rows now drawn.
- `"escape narrows the pick back to the cursor row"`
- `"dragging a picked run onto an Army lifts its count by the number of new units"` — built with
  `page.mouse.move` / `down` / `move` / `up`, moving twice so the 4px threshold is crossed; assert
  the chip `unit-drag-chip` is visible mid-drag and gone after.
- `"a drag that starts on a row outside the pick carries that row alone"` — the D2 E2 rule.
- `"an Army that already holds every dragged unit is not a drop target"` — the D3 W3 rule: assert
  the entry has no `data-drop-army` mid-drag and that its count does not move on release.
- `"dropping on + New Army opens the name field and the units join on Enter"`
- `"right-clicking a row outside the pick picks it alone and opens the menu at the pointer"`

**Existing suites must pass untouched**, and two of them are the real guard here:
`tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts` reads Faction as `td:nth-child(4)`, Men as `td:nth-child(5)` and
Silver as `td:last-child`, and it clicks rows to select them. **If a row click stops selecting, this
bead broke it** — moving selection to `pointerdown` is the change most likely to do that, and
Playwright's `click()` does fire `pointerdown`, so it should hold; if it does not, the fix is the
code.

**Fixtures:** `aReportUnit` (`packages/core-client/src/builders.ts:37`), `aReportRegion` (`:91`) and
`aParsedReport` (`:135`) for the unit tests; `@atlantis/fixtures`' `readReport` for the smoke suite,
as `armies.spec.ts:13` already does.

## User-facing decisions

Thirteen. **G1** is carried in from the epic's round 3 and is restated rather than re-decided;
**D1–D4**, **E1–E4** and **W1–W4** were taken with the navigator on 2026-08-27 over three rounds.
The mockups are `docs/ui/ah-1mpx.4-d1.html`, `-d2.html` and `-d3.html`; the family's earlier ones are
`docs/ui/ah-1mpx-p3.html`, `-r2.html`, `-r3.html` and `ah-1mpx.2-r5/6/7.html`.

**G1 — the gestures, settled in round 3 (`docs/ui/ah-1mpx-r3.html`, W3) and NOT reopened.**

| Gesture | What it does |
|---|---|
| Click | Picks that row alone, as today. |
| Shift+click | Extends from the anchor row to this one. |
| Ctrl/Cmd+click | Adds or removes one row. |
| Shift+↑ / ↓ | Extends by one row from the keyboard. |
| Ctrl/Cmd+A | Picks every row the filter is currently showing. |
| Esc | Narrows the pick back to the cursor row. |
| Right-click | The Army menu, at the pointer. |
| Drag | Onto an Army in the rail. |

Picked rows take a **15%** wash; the cursor row keeps today's **25%** and is the one that fills the
Unit panel. **Changing the source clears the pick** — a selection that survived a jump from one hex
to another would act on rows nobody can see.

**D1 — a drag may be dropped on an Army, and on `+ New Army`.** Dropping on `+ New Army` opens the
rail's own inline name field holding the carried units; they join on Enter, and Escape abandons the
name and the units together — which is what `New Army…` in the popover already does
(`ah-1mpx.2` U2). Assembling a force from scratch is then one gesture instead of two.
*Rejected:* Armies only, with everything else silently inert — nothing then tells you before you
release that a target will refuse; and Armies only with the rest visibly dimmed — half the rail moves
on every drag, and dimming `This hex` collides with U4, where brass and dim already mean something
else.

**D2 — every bulk action lives in one standing line above the table.** `Add to army…` and
`Remove from <the Army>` sit together there rather than in two different places, and its buttons are
ordinary tab stops where the per-row controls are deliberately not.
*Rejected:* relabelling the per-row `Remove` to `Remove 3` — a button on one row silently removing
three, with no keyboard route at all since that button is `tabIndex={-1}`, and bulk *adding* still
homeless; and dragging units out of an Army — "out" is not a place to drop on, and it is a
destructive action with no label.

**D3 — the pick's count lives in that same line**, and the pane header does not move. Nothing in the
header resizes, so the narrow window is unaffected.
*Rejected:* relabelling the header button to `Add 3 units to army ▾` — its width then changes as the
pick does, in the row that already holds the filter box; and putting the count in the pane's grey
hint — a third clause, the one that changes constantly, and it gives the bulk actions no home.

**D4 — right-click opens the popover `ah-1mpx.2` already shipped, at the pointer.** One flat list,
one naming line, `New Army…` last. Right-clicking a row that is not in the pick picks it alone
first, so what the menu says and what is washed always agree.
*Rejected:* a true nested `Add to army ▸` menu as round 3 drew it — hover intent, open and close
delays, two-dimensional arrow traversal and edge flipping, none of which exists in this codebase,
two menus on screen for one choice, and it would be the first thing in a fully keyboard-navigable
dock that a keyboard could not reach; and no right-click at all, which round 3 promised and which
would cost more as its own bead later.

**E1 — the filter narrows the pick.** A row that leaves the view leaves the pick, and clearing the
filter does **not** bring it back. The count and the wash always agree, and nothing off screen can
be acted on — the same rule and the same reason as G1's *changing the source clears the pick*.
*Rejected:* keeping the pick and saying `5 units picked, 2 not shown` — the buttons then act on rows
you cannot check, and `Ctrl/Cmd+A` means "everything the filter is showing", which would either have
to add to the pick or contradict its own wording; and keeping it silently, where three rows are
washed and the line says five.

**E2 — a press on a row outside the pick picks it alone first**, so only that row travels and the
chip names it. A press on a row that *is* in the pick carries the whole pick. One rule covers the
drag and the right-click alike.
*Rejected:* a drag always carrying the pick — you press one row and three others move, it
contradicts D4's rule, and there is then no way to drag a single unit while a pick stands.

**E3 — the bulk line is drawn at two or more picked, and the header's `Add to army ▾` is disabled
while it is.** At nought or one, nothing changes at all from what `ah-1mpx.2` shipped. Exactly one
live way in at any moment.
*Rejected:* leaving both live, which needs the header button to relabel and so reopens D3; and
drawing the line always, which spends 26px of a 330px dock restating what the cursor's own wash
already says, and leaves the header button with no reason to exist.

**E4 — an Army holding some of the pick is offered, with a note**, and choosing it adds only the ones
that are missing. An entry is ticked and inert only when there is genuinely nothing to add, which
keeps `ah-1mpx.2` U3 exactly as it was.
*Rejected:* offering it and saying nothing — you choose it expecting three and the count moves by
two, with nothing anywhere having said why; and a tri-state `−` that is inert, which makes the
commonest case impossible: one of your three is already in, and now you cannot add the other two to
the Army you meant.

**W1 — the bulk line's words.** Exactly:

> `3 units picked.`  ·  `Add to army…`  ·  `Remove from Northern Host`  ·  `Clear`

Never singular — the line is not drawn below two. The removal names what it removes from, so it
cannot be read as *delete these units*, and it matches the delete confirmation already shipped,
which also names the Army.
*Rejected:* `3 selected` with a bare `Remove` — *selected* is this application's word for the cursor
row that drives the Unit panel and the map, and round 3 chose *picked* precisely to keep the two
apart; and naming the units instead of counting them, which truncates to nothing useful at twelve
and states the count twice.

**W2 — the drag chip reads `3 units` for a pick and the unit's own name for one** — `Vanguard`,
never `1 unit`; and the name field a drop on `+ New Army` opens is the **same empty field**
`+ New Army` always opens, with no placeholder about what it is holding.
*Rejected:* a `Name for 3 units` placeholder — the field would then have two appearances where U2
was chosen to have exactly one, and a 172px rail truncates it at double figures; and always
counting, where `1 unit` tells you nothing you cannot see, and the column-reorder chip already
carries the column's *name*.

**W3 — a drop that would add nothing is refused, visibly.** An Army holding every carried unit does
not light as a drop target and takes the same `✓` the menu gives it, so the rail and the menu say
the same thing about the same Army in the same mark. It is the only option where you learn before
letting go, and nothing has to be said afterwards.
*Rejected:* accepting it and reporting `Coastal Watch already has all 3.` on the header status line —
a message for something that is not a failure, on a line that never expires; and accepting it
silently, which is exactly what `ah-1mpx.2` S4 was decided against.

**W4 — the empty-Army line is unchanged**, and ships exactly as `ah-1mpx.2` wrote it:

> **Northern Host has no units yet.**
> Add units to it with Add to army, on any unit in any list.

It is still true: `Add to army` is on the pane header, in the bulk line and in the right-click menu,
and it is the one route a keyboard can take.
*Rejected:* `Drag units onto it, or use Add to army on any unit in any list.` — it is read while
looking at the Army's own empty table, so *it* is the rail entry off to the left rather than the
space the sentence sits in.

**Four are the planner's, recorded so nobody invents a fifth.** None has an interview behind it;
each follows a rule the codebase already states.

- The menu's naming line for several rows is `` `${n} units into…` ``, beside the existing
  `` `${unit.name} (${unit.unitId}) into…` `` for one — the same shape, the same place.
- The note on an Army holding some of the pick is `` `${n} already in` ``, right-aligned in the row.
  One string, no singular variant: `1 already in` reads correctly.
- A failed bulk save says `` `could not save ${the Army's name}` ``, unchanged from `ah-1mpx.2` S4,
  because a bulk write is one write and rolls back whole.
- **Escape cancels a drag in flight before it does anything else**, through the dismiss stack, which
  is how `ColumnReorderHandle` already arbitrates Escape. With no drag in flight, Escape closes an
  open menu, and with neither it narrows the pick.

**Every string above ships exactly as quoted. Nothing here is a paraphrase.**

## Out of scope

- **Foreign units in Armies as a first-class thing, and the faction-dossier popover folded into the
  rail.** `ah-1mpx.5`. Nothing here restricts what may be picked or dragged — `ah-1mpx.2` never
  filtered on `own` and this bead does not start.
- **Skills recovered from battle rosters.** `ah-1mpx.6`.
- **The export.** `ah-1mpx.3`, in flight. This bead adds no `Export…` button anywhere, and the bulk
  line offers no export even though a picked run is exactly what one would want. If that is wanted
  it is a new bead.
- **Reordering an Army's members, or picking across two sources at once.** An Army is a set; it has
  no order to arrange, and the pick belongs to the list on screen.
- **Auto-scrolling the rail or the table during a drag.** The rail is short and wholly visible, and
  a drag that begins in the table ends in the rail. If a rail ever grows past its box this comes
  back as its own bead.
- **Dragging by touch.** The drag is mouse-only, deliberately — a row is a scrollable surface on a
  touch screen, and `selectionGuard.ts` already records that touch is not a platform this
  application serves. Picking works by touch, because a tap is a plain press.
- **Dragging *out* of an Army.** Removal is the bulk line and the per-row button; the navigator
  rejected the drag for it.
- **Any change to the pane's title or its grey hint.** They are `headerFor`'s, exactly as
  `ah-1mpx.2` left them, and the pick is not counted there — that was D3's rejected option C.
- **Persisting the pick.** It is not remembered across a reload, a source change, or a turn load.
- **An undo for a bulk removal.** The application has none and this bead does not invent one.
- **Any change to storage, the schema, the Tauri wire, the web store or the backup.** The one
  addition below the components is `addUnits` on `useArmiesStore`, which is a store action built out
  of the helper that file already has, not storage. Anything that looks like it needs more is a
  question for the navigator through the `human` queue.
- **Adding the three source-dependent columns to `UNIT_COLUMNS`,** or making them resizable. Left
  exactly as `ah-1mpx.2` left them, for the reason in *Known traps*.

## Validation

```bash
pnpm run check:fast
pnpm run test:smoke
```

`check:fast` runs lint, typecheck (including `cargo check --workspace`), every vitest suite,
`pnpm run test:tooling`, `cargo test --workspace`, `pnpm run check:generated`, `cargo fmt --check`
and `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`. It runs all six legs even after one
fails, so read the whole summary.

Four checks a person has to make, none of which any suite can:

- **A plain click still selects a row and fills the Unit panel**, on every source, with no pick
  standing. This is the regression the bead most risks, because selection moved from `click` to
  `pointerdown`.
- **The column preferences of an existing workspace survive.** Drag a column narrower, drag another
  to a new place, reload, and confirm both held. `UNIT_COLUMNS` is untouched by this bead and this
  is the check that says so.
- **The drag chip is visible over the rail**, not clipped at the pane's edge, and it disappears on
  release, on Escape, and when the pointer leaves the window.
- **The right-click menu opens fully on screen from the last row of the dock** and from a row at the
  far right of the table — that is `placeTooltip` doing its job, and it is the case a portal was
  chosen for.

## Known traps

- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom, by decision (`ah-nass`), and this bead is almost entirely
  gesture work — so this trap governs the whole test plan.** A component test here renders with
  `renderToStaticMarkup`, which **runs no effects, attaches no refs and fires no timers**, and shows
  a zustand store only its module-load default. Four beads paid about half an hour each finding
  that. Every rule in this bead therefore lives in a pure module (`unitPick.ts`, `alreadyIn`) and is
  tested there; the components are tested only for what a static render can see; and anything about
  pressing, dragging, focus or popovers is a smoke test.
  `packages/shared/src/testing/README.md` has the pattern, and store state goes through
  `testing/renderWithStoreState`.
- **A `fixed` position inside the units pane is trapped in it.** `CollapsiblePanel` is
  `overflow-hidden` with `backdrop-blur` (`CollapsiblePanel.tsx:78`), and a blurred ancestor is what
  a fixed position resolves against — `UnitTooltip.tsx:10-13` records exactly this and portals to
  the body to escape it. **Both** new floating surfaces must leave the pane: the drag chip is
  appended to `document.body`, and the context menu is a `createPortal` into it. A chip created
  inside the table's scroller could not reach the rail at all.
- **`rowTarget` is not the row's unit.** `UnitTableDock.tsx:1221` is
  `silver?.formed?.formedBy ?? unit.unitId`, so a row for a unit that was formed by another selects
  the *forming* unit. The pick must key on `unit.unitId` and the cursor must keep using `rowTarget`,
  which is why `UnitPick` carries its own `anchor` instead of deriving one from `selectedUnitId`.
  Getting this wrong picks one row and adds a different unit to the Army.
- **Moving selection to `pointerdown` puts every control inside a row in the way.** The per-row
  `Remove` (`:1405`) and the warned-silver button (`:1367`) stop the *click* today and must now stop
  the *pointerdown* too, or pressing either picks the row and arms a drag beneath it. The unit-id
  button (`:1243`) deliberately must not.
- **`ah-1mpx.3` is in flight and edits `ArmyStrip` in this same file.** It adds an `Export…` button
  first in the strip's right-hand group, and its own dialog state to the dock. Expect a conflict
  around `UnitTableDock.tsx`'s strip and the dock's state block; **both changes are wanted** — the
  strip ends up with `Export…`, `Rename`, `Delete`, and the bulk line is a separate row below it.
  Take the update from GitHub (`implement-bead`'s *Merging*), not a local rebase-and-regate.
- **The table is virtualised**, `visible.slice(start, end)` (`:786`). A shift range and `Ctrl/Cmd+A`
  must be computed over **`visible`**, the whole sorted and filtered list, never over the rows in
  the DOM — a range across a scroll boundary is otherwise silently short. `document.elementFromPoint`
  is only ever used against the *rail*, which is not virtualised.
- **The units table is one tab stop per row with a roving `tabIndex`** (`:1447`), and
  `AppShell.tsx:3736-3741` and `UnitTableDock.tsx:1393-1399` both record the decision that a control
  inside a cell is `tabIndex={-1}`, so a three-hundred-row table does not acquire three hundred
  extra tab stops. The bulk line's buttons
  are outside the table and **are** ordinary tab stops; nothing inside a row gains one.
- **The smoke suite reads three columns by position.** `tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts` uses
  `td:nth-child(4)` for Faction, `td:nth-child(5)` for Men and `td:last-child` for Silver, all on the
  `This hex` source. This bead adds no column, so if one goes red the cause is elsewhere and the fix
  is the code, not the selector.
- **`columnOrderFromStorage` rejects a stored order whose length is not `UNIT_COLUMNS.length`**
  (`unitTable.ts:530`), all-or-nothing and by design — which is why `ah-1mpx.2` kept `hex`, `seen`
  and `remove` out of `UNIT_COLUMNS`, and why this bead adds no column either.
- **`grep` treats `unitTable.ts` as binary.** It contains bytes that make `grep` report
  `Binary file matches` instead of the line. Use `grep -a` on it, or a symbol that is there will
  look as though it is not.
- **Tailwind's scanner reads source text**, so `bg-select/15` and every other class must be written
  as a whole literal and never assembled from pieces (`popover.tsx:56-59`). A class built at runtime
  is a class that is never generated.
- **`usePopoverDismiss` hears Escape in the bubble phase, deliberately, and not through the dismiss
  stack** (`popover.tsx:12-18`). The drag uses the dismiss stack in the capture phase, as
  `ColumnReorderHandle` does. That is why Escape cancels a drag before it closes a menu, and the two
  mechanisms must not be swapped.
- **`ChipPopover` restores focus only when the popover still held it and nothing else has claimed
  it** (`popover.tsx:161-185`), and deliberately does not restore after a press outside. Use it for
  the two button-anchored menus rather than hand-rolling dismissal; the context menu is not a chip
  and uses `usePopoverDismiss` directly.
- **The header status line never expires** (`ah-1mpx.2`'s own trap). A failed save stands until the
  next `setStatus`, and that is correct; do not add a timeout.

## Context

Second child of the **Armies** epic (`ah-1mpx`), and the bead where Armies become usable. `ah-1mpx.1`
built the whole storage stack and shipped no interface at all; this one puts the interface on top of
it and adds no storage.

`UnitTableDock` stops meaning "the units in the selected hex". It gains a **source rail** down its
left edge — *This hex*, *All my units*, then each Army — and an Army becomes one more source in the
table that already exists, with its columns, sorting, filtering and virtualisation unchanged. In this
bead an Army can be created, renamed, deleted, and have units added and removed **one at a time**.

**Blocked on `ah-1mpx.1`, which is in flight on branch `ah-1mpx.1-store-armies`.** Everything this
bead consumes from it — `ArmyRecord`, `ArmyMemberRecord`, `packages/shared/src/armies.ts`,
`packages/shared/src/armiesStore.ts` — is specified in that bead's `design` and, at the time of
writing, exists on that branch exactly as specified. Signatures are quoted below from it; treat them
as **a seam a blocker creates**, not as code already on main. If any of them has moved by the time
this is built, `ah-1mpx.1`'s own plan is the authority for what it should be, and a genuine
disagreement is a question for the navigator through the `human` queue — not something to design
around.

Two later siblings matter for scope here, because two decisions exist only because this bead ships
before them:

- **`ah-1mpx.3`** adds the export. So the `Rename` / `Delete` strip carries **two** buttons in this
  bead, not the three the round-3 mockup drew.
- **`ah-1mpx.4`** adds multi-select, drag, and a right-click menu. So the only way a unit joins an
  Army here is the **"Add to army" button in the pane's actions slot**, and the empty-Army line is
  worded so it never has to be rewritten when dragging arrives.

The interview behind every user-facing decision is in *User-facing decisions* below, and in the
epic's own `design`. The mockups are committed: `docs/ui/ah-1mpx-p3.html` (the layout),
`docs/ui/ah-1mpx-r2.html` and `-r3.html` (behaviour and every string of the busy case), and
`docs/ui/ah-1mpx.2-r5.html`, `-r6.html`, `-r7.html` (this bead's own three rounds).

**Not split further, deliberately.** The obvious seam — "the rail with the two built-in sources"
then "Armies on top" — would ship a source rail with two entries and no reason to exist, which a
release note would have to describe. The increments below are ordered so the work is still done in
that order; it merges as one bead.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### What `ah-1mpx.1` leaves behind, and is consumed unchanged

From `packages/core-client/src/index.ts` (hand-written beside `HexNoteRecord`, line 517):

```ts
