**Every one of those mutates optimistically and rethrows when the save fails**, having already put
the old value back (`replaceAndSave` in `armiesStore.ts`). That rethrow is what this bead's failure
message is built on: **`await` them, `catch`, and report**. Do not swallow.

`load`, `clear` and `refreshFor` are already wired into `AppShell` by `ah-1mpx.1`. **This bead adds
no effect to `AppShell` for loading Armies** — that work is done.

### The design of the code

Three new files and four edited ones. The rail is a component of its own rather than another 300
lines inside `UnitTableDock.tsx`, and every rule it obeys lives in a pure module beside it, because
`packages/shared` has no jsdom (see *Known traps*).

#### 1. `packages/shared/src/workspace/unitSource.ts` — new, pure

The whole vocabulary of "which list is the table showing", and every rule that depends on it.

```ts
/** Which list of units the dock is showing. */
export type UnitSource =
  | { kind: "hex" }
  | { kind: "own" }
  | { kind: "army"; armyId: string };

export const HEX_SOURCE: UnitSource = { kind: "hex" };
export const OWN_SOURCE: UnitSource = { kind: "own" };

export function sameSource(left: UnitSource, right: UnitSource): boolean;

/**
 * The source to fall back to when the current one has gone — an Army that was deleted, or any
 * source at all once the game closes. Returns `HEX_SOURCE` unless `source` is still available.
 */
export function sourceStillThere(source: UnitSource, armyIds: readonly string[]): UnitSource;

/**
 * Which extra columns this source warrants, in render order.
 *
 * `hex` for any source that spans hexes, because for `This hex` it would repeat itself on every
 * row. `seen` and `remove` only for an Army: they are meaningless for a list read straight out of
 * the report, where every unit is in it and none can be removed from anything.
 */
export function extraColumnsFor(source: UnitSource): ExtraColumn[];
// `ExtraColumn` is imported from `../unitTable`, where it is declared beside `UnitColumn`. It must
// NOT be declared here: `unitTable.ts` needs it for `EXTRA_COLUMN_SHARES`, and importing it back
// from this file would make the two modules import each other.
//   { kind: "hex" }   -> []
//   { kind: "own" }   -> ["hex"]
//   { kind: "army" }  -> ["hex", "seen", "remove"]

/**
 * The pane's header for a source: the fixed title, and the grey hint beside it.
 *
 * `This hex` is byte-identical to what `UnitTableDock` builds today, so the default view's header
 * does not change at all.
 */
export function headerFor(args: {
  source: UnitSource;
  armyName: string | null;
  unitCount: number;
  shownCount: number;
  hexHint: string | undefined;   // today's `— plain (7,53), 6 units`, already built by the dock
}): { title: string; hint: string | undefined };

/**
 * Whether a sort column still has a column to sort. Changing the source must not leave the table
 * sorted on a column that is no longer drawn.
 */
export function sortSurvives(sort: SortState, source: UnitSource): boolean;
```

`headerFor` produces exactly these, and nothing else:

| Source | title | hint |
|---|---|---|
| `This hex` | `Units in hex` | today's, unchanged — `— plain (7,53), 6 units` |
| `All my units` | `Units` | `— all my units, 38 units` |
| an Army | `Units` | `— Northern Host, 12 units` |

The `, N shown` suffix that today's hint appends when a filter is narrowing the list is appended by
`headerFor` for the two new sources too, in the same words and the same place.

`sortSurvives` is false only for `sort.column === "seen"` on a non-Army source. The caller resets to
`DEFAULT_SORT` when it is false — a sort on an invisible column is a table in an order nothing on
screen explains.

#### 2. `packages/shared/src/workspace/armyRows.ts` — new, pure

Turns an Army into rows the existing table can draw, and this is the load-bearing idea of the bead.

```ts
/** An Army's members as table rows, with everything the extra columns need alongside. */
export type ArmyRows = {
  /** One row per member, ready for `filterUnits` and `sortUnits`. */
  rows: ReportUnit[];
  /** Each member's `seenTurn`, for the Seen column and for sorting on it. */
  seen: Map<string, number>;
  /** How many members this turn's report does not mention. */
  missing: number;
};

/**
 * An Army's members as rows.
 *
 * A member the report shows is the **live `ReportUnit`**, not the snapshot — so its structure, its
 * weight, its long order and its silver are all as good as any other row's. A member the report
 * does not show is rebuilt from the snapshot, which carries less: `structureId`, `weight`,
 * `capacity` and `menByRace` are unknowable for a unit nobody can see, and are `null`/`[]`.
 * `menEstimated` is `true` on a rebuilt row, because a figure from three turns ago is exactly
 * that.
 */
export function armyRows(
  army: ArmyRecord,
  unitsById: ReadonlyMap<string, ReportUnit>,
  currentTurn: number | null
): ArmyRows;

/** The line above the table when members are missing, or null when none are. `ah-1mpx.2` W1. */
export function staleLine(missing: number): { text: string; button: string } | null;
//   0 -> null
//   1 -> { text: "1 unit was not in this turn's report.", button: "Remove it" }
//   n -> { text: `${n} units were not in this turn's report.`, button: "Remove them" }

/** What the Seen column reads for one row. */
export function seenLabel(seenTurn: number | undefined, currentTurn: number | null): string;
//   seenTurn === currentTurn, or either is unknown -> "now"
//   otherwise                                      -> `turn ${seenTurn}`
```

`currentTurn` is `parsed.header.turnNumber`, which is **`number | null`** (`ReportHeaderInfo`). When
it is null every member reads `now` and `missing` is 0 — with no turn to compare against, calling a
member stale would be a guess. `judgeReportUsable` already refuses a report naming no turn, so this
is a type-driven guard rather than a case that arises.

**Why rows and not a wrapper type.** `packages/shared/src/unitPreview.ts:31` sets the precedent and
states the reason: `PreviewedUnit = ReportUnit & { …all optional… }` is "an extension of `ReportUnit`
rather than a wrapper, so everything that already handles units — sorting, filtering, the row cap,
the tooltip — keeps working without knowing the preview exists". Because every added field is
optional, a `PreviewedUnit[]` passes through `filterUnits` and `sortUnits` (which are declared over
`ReportUnit[]`) and comes back assignable to `PreviewedUnit` again, with no cast anywhere. Army rows
are plain `ReportUnit`s for the same reason, and `seenTurn` travels beside them in a map rather than
on them — which is also how `longOrders` and `silver` already reach `sortUnits`.

#### 3. Who owns the editing, and who owns the writes

Three surfaces in this bead can change an Army — the rail (`+ New Army`, inline naming), the strip
above the table (`Rename`, `Delete` and its confirmation) and the popover (`New Army…`). They are in
three different places on screen and must share one editing state, or `Rename` in the strip cannot
put the rail's row into edit.

**So `UnitTableDock` owns both, and all three surfaces are presentational.** It holds the editing
state and it holds the write callbacks; the rail, the strip and the menu receive plain props and
render. This is what lets every one of them be tested with `renderToStaticMarkup`, which is the only
kind of component test this package has.

`packages/shared/src/workspace/railEditState.ts` — new, pure. The editing state, modelled exactly on
`packages/shared/src/workspace/regionNotesState.ts`, which exists for this reason.

```ts
export type RailMode =
  | { kind: "idle" }
  /** A new Army being named. `withUnit` is set when the popover's "New Army…" started it. */
  | { kind: "creating"; draft: string; withUnit: ReportUnit | null }
  | { kind: "renaming"; armyId: string; draft: string }
  | { kind: "deleting"; armyId: string };

export type RailEvent =
  | { type: "new-clicked"; withUnit: ReportUnit | null }
  | { type: "rename-clicked"; armyId: string; name: string }
  | { type: "draft-changed"; draft: string }
  | { type: "cancelled" }
  | { type: "committed" }
  | { type: "delete-clicked"; armyId: string }
  | { type: "delete-cancelled" }
  | { type: "deleted" };

export function reduce(mode: RailMode, event: RailEvent): RailMode;

/** A name is committable when it has something in it once trimmed. Duplicates are allowed. */
export function canCommit(draft: string): boolean;

export type RailKeyAction = "commit" | "cancel" | null;
/** Enter commits, Escape cancels. A rail name is one line, so Enter is not a newline here. */
export function keyToAction(event: { key: string }): RailKeyAction;
```

`keyToAction` deliberately differs from `regionNotesState.ts:39`, where plain Enter is a newline and
Cmd/Ctrl+Enter saves — a note is a paragraph and an Army's name is one line. Say so in a comment;
the difference is a decision, not an oversight.

`packages/shared/src/workspace/useArmyActions.ts` — new. The writes, with the failure message in one
place instead of five.

```ts
/** What the rail, the strip and the popover call. Every one has already reported its own failure. */
export type ArmyActions = {
  create: (name: string, withUnit: ReportUnit | null) => Promise<void>;
  rename: (armyId: string, name: string) => Promise<void>;
  remove: (armyId: string) => Promise<void>;
  addUnit: (armyId: string, unit: ReportUnit) => Promise<void>;
  removeUnit: (armyId: string, unitId: string) => Promise<void>;
  removeUnits: (armyId: string, unitIds: readonly string[]) => Promise<void>;
};

/**
 * The Army writes, each one awaiting the store, catching the rethrow it makes on a failed save,
 * and reporting it — the store has already put the old value back by then, so there is nothing to
 * undo here and nothing to say but what failed.
 *
 * Returns a no-op set when `game` is null, so the callers need no null check of their own.
 */
export function useArmyActions(args: {
  client: CoreClient;
  game: OpenedGame | null;
  currentTurn: number | null;
  onFailure: (message: string) => void;
}): ArmyActions;
```

The message is `` `could not save ${name}` `` for every one of them, where `name` is the Army's
name — the words S4 settled. `now` is `new Date().toISOString()`, taken inside each call, exactly as
`AppShell.tsx:1241-1250` does for a stored turn. `create` calls the store's `create` and then, when
`withUnit` is not null, its `addUnit` with the returned Army's id — which is how the popover's
`New Army…` puts the unit in.

`addUnit` needs a turn: it is `currentTurn`, and when that is null the action is **not offered at
all** (the `Add to army ▾` trigger is disabled), because `snapshotOf` would have no `seenTurn` to
stamp. A report naming no turn is already refused by `judgeReportUsable`, so this is a type-driven
guard rather than a case that arises.

`removeUnits` is a **new action on `useArmiesStore`**, added by this bead to
`packages/shared/src/armiesStore.ts` beside `removeUnit`, and built on the same `replaceAndSave`
helper that file already has:

```ts
/** Several members dropped in one write, for the standing "Remove them" line. `ah-1mpx.2`. */
removeUnits: (
  client: CoreClient, game: OpenedGame, armyId: string,
  unitIds: readonly string[], now: string
) => Promise<void>;
```

One save and one rollback rather than n of each. Calling `removeUnit` in a loop would write the
Army n times and leave it half-changed if the third write failed, which is exactly the state the
optimistic-with-rollback pattern exists to prevent.

