## The test plan

`pnpm run check:fast` runs every unit suite. The browser suites are CI's.

**Unit** — `packages/shared/src/workspace/unitSource.test.ts`, `armyRows.test.ts`,
`railEditState.test.ts`, `UnitSourceRail.test.tsx`, `AddToArmyMenu.test.tsx`, plus the new cases in
`packages/shared/src/unitTable.test.ts`, `packages/shared/src/armiesStore.test.ts` and
`packages/shared/src/workspace/UnitTableDock.test.tsx` named above.

**Browser** — a new `tests/smoke/armies.spec.ts`, modelled on `tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts` and
using `tests/smoke/gameSetup.ts`'s `createGame`, `loadReport`, `selectHex` and `selectUnit`. Six
tests, and every one of them is a rule from the interview:

- `"a new Army is named in the rail and appears among the others"` — `+ New Army`, type, Enter, and
  the row is there; then Escape on a second one and it is not.
- `"a unit is added from the header menu and the Army's count follows"` — select a row, open
  **Add to army ▾**, choose the Army, and the rail's count goes up by one; reopen the menu and that
  Army is ticked and disabled.
- `"choosing an Army shows its units, and the pane says so"` — the header reads
  `Units — <name>, N units`, and the Hex, Seen and Remove columns appear.
- `"clicking a hex while an Army is the source keeps the Army and lights This hex"` — the table does
  not change, and `This hex` carries the new hex's count.
- `"This hex draws exactly the columns it drew before"` — count the header cells on the default
  source and assert ten, which is the test that the persisted column machinery was left alone.
- `"deleting an Army asks first, and Cancel leaves it there"`.

**Existing suites must pass untouched.** `workspace.spec.ts` reads Men and Faction as
`td:nth-child(5)` / `td:nth-child(4)` and Silver as `td:last-child`, all on the `This hex` source.
**If any of those goes red, the design has been broken, not the test** — see *Known traps*.

**Fixtures:** build units with `aReportUnit` (`packages/core-client/src/builders.ts:37`),
`aReportRegion` (`:91`) and `aParsedReport` (`:135`) for the unit tests; `@atlantis/fixtures`'
`readReport` for the smoke suite, as `workspace.spec.ts:40-56` does.

## User-facing decisions

Twelve, taken with the navigator over three rounds specific to this bead. The mockups are
`docs/ui/ah-1mpx.2-r5.html`, `-r6.html` and `-r7.html`; the rest of the family's decisions are in the
epic `ah-1mpx`'s design and in `docs/ui/ah-1mpx-p3.html`, `-r2.html`, `-r3.html`.

**S1 — the rail while Armies are loading: complete from the first paint.** *This hex*, *All my
units*, the *Armies* heading and *+ New Army* are all live before storage answers; Armies appear
under the heading when they arrive. Nothing is ever disabled and the word "loading" never appears.
*Rejected:* a dimmed `loading…` line with `+ New Army` disabled — a word that flickers past on every
game open for a wait nobody notices, and a click landing in the gap that does nothing.

**S2 — the strip above the table carries two buttons, `Rename` and `Delete`.** `Export…` is not
drawn at all until `ah-1mpx.3` builds it. *Rejected:* drawing it disabled, so the strip is its final
shape from day one — a dead control, and no honest tooltip for one that is not an apology for the
backlog.

**S3 — the empty-Army lines, written once to be true after `ah-1mpx.4` as well:**

> **Northern Host has no units yet.**
> Add units to it with Add to army, on any unit in any list.

*Rejected:* naming right-click now and rewriting it when dragging lands; and the bare fact with no
second line, which is the one place the application had the navigator's attention and said nothing.

**S4 — a save that fails says so on the header status line**, in these words:
`could not save <the Army's name>`. Every mutation is optimistic and the store has already rolled it
back, so the count returns to what it was; without the line the only available conclusion is that
the application drops units. *Rejected:* silence; and a red line inside the 172px rail, which would
have to decide when to leave and would push the Armies below it down while it was there.

**S5 — removing, both ways.** The per-row `Remove` sits in a **trailing action column**, fixed
width, empty on every row but the selected one, drawn only while an Army is the source — so nothing
reflows as the selection moves. *Rejected:* floating it over the selected row's last cell, which
costs no width but covers what that row was showing. The standing bulk line **does not confirm**:
the rows are on screen, named, and sorted where they can be seen, and removing a unit from an Army
destroys nothing — the unit is untouched and can be added back from any list. *Rejected:* a
confirmation matching the delete-an-Army one, which is a dialog for something reversible by hand on a
bar you pass every turn.

**T1 — a unit joins an Army through `Add to army ▾` in the pane's actions slot**, beside the filter
box, opening a popover whose first line names the unit: `Outriders (9977) into…`. *Rejected:*
building the right-click menu round 3 assumed — **this application has no context menu anywhere**
(the only right-click in it recentres the map), so that gesture is an entirely new component, and it
would be the first thing in a fully keyboard-navigable dock that a keyboard cannot reach. It is not
abandoned: it belongs in `ah-1mpx.4` with drag and multi-select, where it can say "Add 3 units to
army" and where the popover this bead builds is the list it will show.

**T2 — the source is not remembered.** Reopening the application, and opening another game, both
land on *This hex*; loading next month's report leaves the source alone. This is what the workspace
store already does with `selectedRegionId` and `selectedUnitId` (`workspaceStore.ts:529-532`).
*Rejected:* remembering it per game — you would reopen to a list unrelated to the hex on the map, so
the two disagree before you did anything to cause it, and a remembered id that no longer exists is a
new case to get right in a store that persists only numbers and flags.

**U1 — the pane keeps one identity across sources**; only the grey hint moves. The three headers are
in the table under `headerFor` above, and `This hex` is unchanged from today. *Rejected:* making the
title the source name, which reopens the round-3 Army line and would ship "Northern Host" as
NORTHERN HOST, since the panel header uppercases.

**U2 — `New Army…` chosen from the popover closes it and drops into the rail's own inline editor**,
with the unit joining on Enter and nothing created on Escape. One naming control in the whole
feature, in one place, behaving one way. *Rejected:* a second name field inside the popover — two
implementations of one rule, each owing the same empty-name refusal and the same Escape.

**U3 — an Army the unit is already in reads ticked and inert.** A menu called *Add to army* only
ever adds, so nothing destructive can happen by mis-clicking in it. *Rejected:* click-to-remove, one
click from a tick to a silent removal; and no mark at all, which silently changes nothing and is
indistinguishable from broken.

**U4 — `This hex` is brass with a ● whenever the source is not `This hex`**, carrying the selected
hex's count. The mark means *the table is not about the hex on the map*, with no history in it.
*Rejected:* lighting only once the hex changes underneath you, which needs a remembered hex and
rules for what clears it on a turn load and a game open.

**Carried in from round 3, and not to be re-opened** (epic `ah-1mpx`, `docs/ui/ah-1mpx-r3.html`):
inline naming, with `+ New Army` putting the cursor in a new row, Enter committing, Escape
abandoning a new Army and reverting a rename, an empty name refused with the row staying in edit, and
**duplicate names allowed**; the delete confirmation
`Delete <name>? The <n> units in it are not affected.` with **Delete** and **Cancel**; the stale line
naming no turn — `2 units were not in this turn's report.` / `Remove them`, and
`1 unit was not in this turn's report.` / `Remove it`; the **Seen** column reading `now` or
`turn 68` and sortable; the **Hex** column dimmed for a remembered member and **not** sortable;
Armies scoped to the game.

**Two strings are the planner's, and are recorded here so nobody invents a third.** Both are
degenerate empty states with no interview behind them, written to the pattern the dock already ships
at `UnitTableDock.tsx:395-400` (`No units reported in this hex.`):

- *All my units*, with none: `No units of your own in this turn's report.`
- *All my units* or an Army, with no report loaded at all: `No report loaded.`

**Every string above ships exactly as quoted.** Nothing here is a paraphrase.

