```

Two checks a person has to make, neither of which any suite can:

- **The column preferences of an existing workspace survive.** Drag a column narrower and drag
  another to a new place, reload, and confirm both held — then switch to an Army and back to
  *This hex* and confirm they are still exactly as set. This is the check that `UNIT_COLUMNS` was
  genuinely left alone.
- **A narrow window.** Below about 900px the 172px rail costs too much of the table. This bead does
  **not** build the fold-into-the-title picker that `docs/ui/ah-1mpx-r3.html` W5 draws — that is
  future work — so confirm what actually happens at 800px: the rail keeps its width, the table
  columns narrow with it, and nothing overflows the pane or is drawn past its right edge. If
  anything overflows, that is a defect in this bead and not a missing feature.

## Known traps

- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom, by decision (`ah-nass`), and this is the trap this bead is most
  exposed to** — it is almost entirely component work. A component test here renders with
  `renderToStaticMarkup`, which **runs no effects, attaches no refs and fires no timers**, and shows
  a zustand store only its module-load default. Four beads paid about half an hour each discovering
  that. So: every rule in this bead lives in a pure module (`unitSource.ts`, `armyRows.ts`,
  `railEditState.ts`) and is tested there; the components are tested only for what a static render
  can see; and anything about clicking, focus, popovers or scrolling is a smoke test.
  `packages/shared/src/testing/README.md` has the pattern, and store state goes through
  `testing/renderWithStoreState`.
- **The smoke suite reads three columns by position.** `tests/smoke/workspace.spec.ts` uses
  `td:nth-child(4)` for Faction (`:2678`), `td:nth-child(5)` for Men (`:2510`, `:2537`, `:2810`) and
  `td:last-child` for Silver (`:2624`, `:4703`). Every one of them runs on the `This hex` source,
  which this design leaves with exactly today's ten columns in today's order — **so if one of these
  goes red, a new column has leaked into the default view, and the fix is the code, not the
  selector.**
- **`columnOrderFromStorage` rejects a stored order whose length is not `UNIT_COLUMNS.length`**
  (`unitTable.ts:470-472`), all-or-nothing and by design. Adding a column to `UNIT_COLUMNS` would
  therefore throw away every user's arranged column order, silently, on the first run of the new
  build. That is why the three new columns are not members of it.
- **Shares must always sum to 1.** `ColumnShares`' own doc (`unitTable.ts:295-311`) records that the
  pixel model this replaced was arithmetically sound per drag and still lost columns off the right
  edge of a `table-fixed` box, where nothing scrolls them back. Drawing a subset of the columns
  breaks that sum unless the remainder is renormalised, which is `sharesFor`'s whole reason to
  exist. A visible-set width computed any other way reintroduces the bug that model was replaced
  for.
- **`sortUnits` and `filterUnits` are declared over `ReportUnit[]` and return `ReportUnit[]`**, yet
  the dock feeds them `PreviewedUnit[]` and hands the result to a component expecting
  `PreviewedUnit`. That compiles because **every field `PreviewedUnit` adds is optional**
  (`unitPreview.ts:31`), so the two types are mutually assignable. Any new row field must be
  optional too, or the pipeline stops type-checking in a place that does not name the cause. This is
  why `seenTurn` travels in a map rather than on the row.
- **`ParsedReport.header`, not `turnHeader`.** `ParsedReport` has `header: ReportHeaderInfo`;
  `turnHeader` belongs to `ReportParseResult`, a different type. And `header.turnNumber` is
  **`number | null`** — `armyRows` and `seenLabel` take the null rather than guessing a turn.
- **`silverKey(regionId, unitId)` keys the silver forecast by region.** A source spanning hexes must
  key each row by **its own** `regionId`, not by the selected hex's, or the Silver column reads the
  wrong forecast for every row not in the selected hex.
- **The units table is one tab stop per row with a roving `tabIndex`** (`UnitTableDock.tsx:980`),
  and `AppShell.tsx:3570-3575` records the decision that a control inside a cell is `tabIndex={-1}`
  precisely so a three-hundred-row table does not acquire three hundred extra tab stops. The
  per-row `Remove` follows that rule: `tabIndex={-1}`, reachable by pointer, with the keyboard route
  being the standing bulk line and the rail. Do not give it a tab stop.
- **`ChipPopover` restores focus only when the popover still held it and nothing else has claimed
  it** (`popover.tsx:161-185`), and deliberately does not restore after a press outside. Use it
  rather than hand-rolling dismissal; `ExportMenu.tsx` is the worked example, including its
  deliberate `role="dialog"` over `role="menu"` (`ExportMenu.tsx:16-18`), which is what excuses it
  from owing an arrow-key contract.
- **The header status line never expires.** There is no timer anywhere in `AppHeader.tsx` or
  `shellStatus.ts`; a line stands until the next `setStatus` or until a game is entered
  (`AppShell.tsx:1802`). That is correct for a failure and is not to be "fixed" with a timeout.
- **`grep` treats `unitTable.ts` as binary.** The file contains bytes that make it report
  `Binary file matches` instead of the line. Use `grep -a` on it, or the search will look as though
  the symbol is not there.

## Context

A unit that writes `SELL ALL FUR` twice in one block is priced differently by the two surfaces that
read that block, and has been since before `ah-q7jd`.

The **ledger** charges once. `semantics::sell` (`crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs:3960`) resolves
`Amount::All` against `actor.early_holding(&tag).min(balance_of(ledger, who, &tag))`, so the first
line takes the whole stock and the second finds a balance of nothing. The ITEMS column, built from
those movements, is right.

The **SILVER column** prices each `Intent::Sell` independently. `silver::forecast_unit`'s `Sell` arm
(`crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs:1064`) resolves `Amount::All { except } => (unit_holds - except).max(0)`,
where `unit_holds` is filled once per item by `forecast_hex`'s `sale` closure
(`semantics.rs:839`, `ordered.early_holding(&line.tag.to_ascii_uppercase())`). Both lines therefore
earn, and the month shows twice the silver the unit can actually get.

The **settlement** sides with the wrong column. `market_shares_for` (`semantics.rs:650`) sums a
unit's claims per tag — `claims[index] = claims[index].saturating_add(want)` — so the unit claims
twenty of a line it can only supply ten into. `split_pool` returns claims unchanged when the line is
not oversubscribed, so nothing caps it; when the line *is* short, the inflated claim wins the unit a
larger share than it is owed and can push the line into a false `region-pool-oversubscribed`.

There is a **second defect in the same family**, found while planning this and fixed here because
one running total closes both: neither surface decrements the *settled share* between a unit's own
sell lines. With a share of 3 and two `SELL ALL FUR` lines, `quantity_sold(asked, holds, 3)` is
applied twice and the unit sells 6 of a line it was allotted 3 of — in the ledger as well as the
column. Fixing only the holdings side would leave the two surfaces agreeing on a number that is
still wrong.

**What changes when this lands.** A block's `SELL` lines are priced against a running per-tag total,
so a second line can only move what the lines above it left — of the unit's stock and of its settled
share alike. All three surfaces then agree. A line that finds *nothing* of its goods left carries
the existing `nothing-left-to-sell` warning, as a third cause beside the gift and the take, and that
warning now fires on any `SELL` rather than only on a `SELL … ALL`.

The chosen design, the two follow-up rounds and everything rejected are in
`docs/ui/ah-vw8e-sold-twice.html`, `docs/ui/ah-vw8e-round2-shapes.html` and
`docs/ui/ah-vw8e-round3-wording.html` (merged in #732).

Nothing blocks this and it blocks nothing.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### 1. `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs` — one pricing function for both surfaces

Today `sell` computes `quantity_sold(asked, holds, allowed)` and then calls
`price_sale(asked, holds, price, allowed)`, which computes `quantity_sold` again from the same three
inputs; `forecast_unit` calls `price_sale` alone. Replace both with **one** function that resolves
the amount as well as pricing it, so `Amount::All`'s `except` arithmetic — the thing that has to see
the running total — exists once rather than twice.

Add beside `quantity_sold` (`silver.rs:2473`), and **remove `price_sale`** (`silver.rs:2479`); its
two callers and its two unit tests move here. `quantity_sold` stays: this calls it, and its
negative-`asked` test still pins the `max(0)`.

```rust
/// One `SELL` line's outcome.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]
pub struct SoldLine {
    /// How many the order asks for, once `ALL … EXCEPT` is resolved against what is left.
    pub asked: i64,
    /// How many actually move.
    pub quantity: i64,
    /// What that earns.
    pub earns: i64,
}

/// What one `SELL` line of a block does, priced against what the lines above it already took.
///
/// A unit's `SELL` lines draw on one stock and on one settled share of one market line, so a second
/// line for the same goods can only move what the first left - of both. Both surfaces call this,
/// the ledger through `super::semantics::sell` and the Silver column through [`forecast_unit`], so
/// one block cannot be priced two ways (`ah-vw8e`, and the drift `ah-ycuj`'s corpus test guards).
#[must_use]
pub fn price_sale_line(
    amount: &Amount,
    /// What the unit still holds of these goods when this line runs.
    remaining_holding: i64,
    /// What is still left of this unit's settled share of the market line.
    remaining_allowed: i64,
    price: i64,
) -> SoldLine {
    let asked = match amount {
        Amount::Exact(count) => *count,
        Amount::All { except } => remaining_holding - except,
    };
    let quantity = quantity_sold(asked, remaining_holding, remaining_allowed);
    SoldLine {
        asked,
        quantity,
        earns: quantity.saturating_mul(price),
    }
}
```

`asked` is returned unclamped, exactly as `price_sale`'s caller passed it: `quantity_sold` applies
`.max(0)` last precisely because `ALL { except }` can make it negative, and that comment stays true.

**Rust does not accept doc comments on function parameters** — write those two lines as ordinary
`//` comments above each parameter, the way `forecast_hex` and `apply` already annotate theirs.

### 2. `crates/core/src/orders/silver.rs` — the running total in `forecast_unit`

`BTreeMap` and `Amount` are already imported (`silver.rs:13`, `:18`). Before the `for placed in intents`
loop (`silver.rs:1033`), beside `deferred` (`:1011`) and `buy_all` (`:1014`):

```rust
// What this unit's earlier `SELL` lines have already moved, per canonical tag. A block may name the
// same goods twice, and the second line can only move what the first left - of the stock and of the
// settled share alike (`ah-vw8e`).
let mut sold: BTreeMap<String, i64> = BTreeMap::new();
```

The `SaleAnswer::Wanted` arm (`silver.rs:1064`) becomes:

```rust
SaleAnswer::Wanted {
    price,
    market_takes,
    unit_holds,
} => {
    // What this hex's other own sellers left of the line, or the line itself where nothing was
    // settled (`ah-t2pn.3`).
    let allowed = (lookups.market_share)(item, MarketSide::Selling).unwrap_or(market_takes);
    // Keyed by the canonical tag, which is the key the settlement itself uses, so two spellings
    // of one item share one total. A tag nothing resolves is untracked and cannot double-count:
    // `resolve_item` walks the unit's own inventory, so a `None` here means the unit holds none
    // of these goods and every such line sells nothing whether tracked or not.
    let key = (lookups.item_tag)(item);
    let already = key.as_ref().and_then(|tag| sold.get(tag)).copied().unwrap_or(0);
    let line = price_sale_line(
        amount,
        (unit_holds - already).max(0),
        (allowed - already).max(0),
        price,
    );
    income = income.saturating_add(line.earns);
    if let Some(tag) = key {
        *sold.entry(tag).or_default() += line.quantity;
    }
}
```

`Lookups::item_tag` (`silver.rs:586`) is `&dyn Fn(&str) -> Option<String>`; `forecast_hex` fills it
with `resolve_item`, which already uppercases (`semantics.rs:4239`). No new lookup is added and
`SaleAnswer` is untouched — it is `Copy` and lifetime-free, and giving it a borrowed tag would push a
lifetime through `Lookups::sale` for nothing.

### 3. `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` — the ledger's running total, and what it records

`Ledger` (`semantics.rs:2227`) is private to the module; add two fields beside `balance` and
`charged_at`:

```rust
/// How many of one tag a unit's own `SELL` lines have already moved this month, and how many of
/// those lines moved any. A block may name the same goods twice, and the second line can only draw
/// on what the first left of the unit's settled share of the market line (`ah-vw8e`). Keyed as
/// `balance` is, and with the same caveat: two units carrying one id share an entry.
sold: BTreeMap<(String, String), SoldSoFar>,
/// Every `SELL` line that found none of its goods left when it ran, recorded where the running
/// total already lives so `check_emptied_sales` need not compute the same fact a second time
/// (`ah-vw8e`) - which is how this column and that warning drifted apart before (`ah-ycuj`).
dead_sales: Vec<DeadSale>,
```

with, beside the struct:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Default)]
struct SoldSoFar {
    /// How many of the tag those lines moved.
    quantity: i64,
    /// How many lines moved any. Only the sentence's verb reads it.
    lines: i64,
}

/// A `SELL` line with nothing left to sell, and what the lines above it had taken.
struct DeadSale {
    unit_id: String,
    tag: String,
    placed: PlacedIntent,
    sold_before: SoldSoFar,
}
```

`charged_at` already stores a `PlacedIntent` by value, so cloning one here follows the module's own
precedent. Both new fields join `Ledger`'s initialiser in `ledger_for` (`semantics.rs:2402`, `let mut ledger = Ledger {`);
`Ledger` has no `Default`, so both must be named there — `BTreeMap::new()` and `Vec::new()`.

`sell` (`semantics.rs:3960`) then reads:

```rust
let tag = demand.tag.to_ascii_uppercase();
let before = ledger
    .sold
    .get(&(who.clone(), tag.clone()))
    .copied()
    .unwrap_or_default();
// The market phase can only sell what the phase boundary itself leaves the unit
// (`rules/sequenceofevents`), so the early holding caps what `ALL` resolves against - and the
// running ledger balance still caps it too, which is what catches a `GIVE` this month's projection
// could not follow, and what already nets off this unit's earlier sales (`ah-q7jd`).
let remaining_holding = actor.early_holding(&tag).min(balance_of(ledger, who, &tag));
// What this hex's other own sellers left of the line, or the line itself where nothing was settled
// (`ah-t2pn.3`), less what this unit's own earlier lines have already taken out of it.
let allowed = standing
    .share_of(&tag, MarketSide::Selling)
    .unwrap_or(demand.amount);
let line = price_sale_line(
    amount,
    remaining_holding,
    (allowed - before.quantity).max(0),
    demand.price,
);

charge(ledger, who, &tag, line.quantity, placed);
credit(ledger, who, SILVER, line.earns);

let entry = ledger.sold.entry((who.clone(), tag.clone())).or_default();
entry.quantity = entry.quantity.saturating_add(line.quantity);
if line.quantity > 0 {
    entry.lines += 1;
}
if remaining_holding == 0 {
    ledger.dead_sales.push(DeadSale {
        unit_id: who.clone(),
        tag: tag.clone(),
        placed: placed.clone(),
        sold_before: before,
    });
}
if line.quantity != 0 {
    ledger.movements.push(ItemMovement { /* unchanged */ });
}
```

**`remaining_holding` must not subtract `before.quantity`.** The balance is already net of this
unit's earlier sales — `charge` took each one off it — so subtracting again would halve the second
line. The share is the opposite case: `standing.share_of` is a fixed per-unit total that nothing
decrements, which is why `before.quantity` comes off *that*. This asymmetry is the single easiest
thing to get wrong here; it is in *Known traps* as well.

The column cannot use the balance the same way — it has none — which is why `price_sale_line` takes
two remainders rather than computing them itself.

### 4. `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` — the claim a doubled sale makes

In `market_shares_for` (`semantics.rs:650`), before the `for (index, ordered)` loop:

```rust
// A unit's `SELL` lines draw on one stock, so a second line for the same goods can only claim what
// the first left. Without this a unit writing `SELL ALL FUR` twice claims twenty of a line it can
// supply ten into, wins a share it is not owed, and can push the line into a false
// `region-pool-oversubscribed` (`ah-vw8e`).
let mut claimed: BTreeMap<(usize, String), i64> = BTreeMap::new();
```

and the `Sell` arm of the `want` match becomes:

```rust
(Intent::Sell { amount, .. }, _) => {
    let already = claimed.get(&(index, tag.clone())).copied().unwrap_or(0);
    let holds = (ordered.early_holding(&tag) - already).max(0);
    match amount {
        Amount::Exact(count) => (*count).min(holds),
        Amount::All { except } => (holds - except).max(0),
    }
}
```

with, immediately after `want` is computed and **before** `tag` is moved into
`wants.entry((tag, side))`:

```rust
if matches!(side, MarketSide::Selling) {
    *claimed.entry((index, tag.clone())).or_default() += want.max(0);
}
```

Concretely: between `let want = match (&placed.intent, side) { … };` and the
`if want <= 0 { continue; }` guard below it. Putting it after the guard is equivalent (adding zero
changes nothing); putting it after `let claims = wants.entry((tag, side))` is **not**, because `tag`
has been moved by then, and the compiler will point at the entry call rather than at the mistake.
`matches!` rather than `==` so nothing depends on which comparison traits `MarketSide` derives.

Only the selling side is tracked. A `BUY` claim is not capped by holdings, and `Amount::All` already
claims the whole pool; the buying side has its own doubled-claim defect and it is out of scope here
(see *Out of scope*).

### 5. `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs` — the warning

`check_emptied_sales` (`semantics.rs:2884`) is rewritten to read `ledger.dead_sales` instead of
walking the hex itself, and gains a `ledger: &Ledger<'_>` parameter. Its call site
(`semantics.rs:457`) already has `ledger` in scope — `check_resources` on the line above takes it —
so the change is `check_emptied_sales(hex, ledger, ruleset, &plurals, &options, &mut findings)`.

Three of today's gates disappear because the new source has already applied them, and this is the
point of moving the source: `sell` returns early when `market(&hex.region.wanted, …)` is `None`, so
no dead sale is ever recorded for goods this market does not want or nothing could identify — which
is exactly what today's `MarketAnswer` gate and its comment about `not-traded-here` are for. The
`Amount::All`-only gate goes with them, deliberately (see *User-facing decisions*, Q1 of round 2).

What remains, per entry, in this order:

1. `options.emits(codes::NOTHING_LEFT_TO_SELL)` — one early return at the top, as today.
2. `let Some(ordered) = hex.find(&dead.unit_id) else { continue };` — `hex.find` is what `emptied_by`
   already uses; it returns the first unit of that id, which is the same limitation `balance` and
   `sold` carry.
3. `if ordered.holdings_unknown() { continue; }` — a transfer the projection could not follow means
   "it was moved away" is exactly what cannot be said. Unchanged, and load-bearing: the balance can
   reach zero from a gift `early_holding` never saw.
4. `let report_holding = ordered.holding(&dead.tag); if report_holding <= 0 { continue; }` — a unit
   that never held any was never going to sell any, and nothing this month caused that. Unchanged.
5. The cause:
   - `dead.sold_before.quantity > 0` → the new sentence, built below.
   - otherwise → `emptied_by(hex, &dead.unit_id, &dead.tag, ruleset)`; `None` → `continue`; `Some`
     → the two shipped sentences, unchanged word for word.
6. `findings.push(ordered.finding(hex, codes::NOTHING_LEFT_TO_SELL, message, Some(&dead.placed)));`

Step 5's ordering **is** the precedence the navigator chose: a sale that took anything names itself,
and the gift is named only when no sale took anything. `emptied_by`'s own `OwnGive`-beats-
`AnotherTake` rule is untouched below it.

The new sentence, from `dead.sold_before` and `report_holding`:

```rust
let SoldSoFar { quantity, lines } = dead.sold_before;
// The count is what the earlier lines actually sold, never the report's figure: a month where a
// gift took part of the stock would otherwise claim all of it was sold.
let goods = if quantity == 1 {
    if quantity == report_holding {
        format!("its only {}", item_name(&dead.tag, hex, ruleset))
    } else {
        format!(
            "the only {} it had left",
            item_name(&dead.tag, hex, ruleset)
        )
    }
} else if quantity == report_holding {
    format!(
        "all {quantity} of its {}",
        plural_name(&dead.tag, hex, ruleset, plurals)
    )
} else {
    format!(
        "the {quantity} {} it had left",
        plural_name(&dead.tag, hex, ruleset, plurals)
    )
};
let message = if lines == 1 {
    format!("an earlier order sells {goods}, so this sells nothing")
} else {
    format!("earlier orders sell {goods}, so this sells nothing")
};
```

`item_name` and `plural_name` (`semantics.rs:2872`) are the two the shipped sentences already use,
and the singular/plural split follows theirs exactly.

`quantity > report_holding` cannot arise: `quantity` is a sum of `quantity_sold` results, each capped
by a `remaining_holding` that starts at `early_holding` and is only ever spent down, and a unit whose
`early_holding` exceeds its report holding got there through a `TAKE` — which the `holdings_unknown`
and `emptied_by` paths do not reach here, since `sold_before.quantity > 0` short-circuits step 5. The
`else` branch reads "the N … it had left", which stays true in that case anyway.

### Where state lives

Nothing is added to any store, any component or any serialised type. `UnitSilver`, `Finding`,
`TurnReview`, `CheckOptions` and `codes::ALL` are all unchanged, so
`packages/core-client/src/coreVocabulary.generated.ts` and the generated-TypeScript test are
untouched and `packages/shared/src/workspace/SettingsDialog.tsx:517` keeps its existing entry. Both
running totals are locals or private `Ledger` fields that live for one hex's pass and are dropped
with it. **No TypeScript changes at all.**

