PILLAGE is a term in one of the two economy surfaces and not the other.

The ledger credits it (semantics.rs:1046-1052):
  Intent::Pillage => match hex.region.tax_base {
      Some(base) => credit(ledger, who, SILVER, base.saturating_mul(2)),
      None => { ledger.doubted.insert(who.clone()); }
  },

forecast_unit has no Pillage arm at all - it falls through the trailing '_ => {}' at silver.rs ~622. Grepping Pillage across crates/ finds intents.rs, semantics.rs:520 and semantics.rs:1046, and nothing in silver.rs.

So a unit that pillages and then spends is shown a red, negative month-end silver while the not-enough-silver warning stays quiet, because the warning credits the pillage and the column does not. Two surfaces built from the same orders disagree in front of the player, and the column is the one that is wrong. No test covers pillage in the forecast.

design:
## Context

`ah-j0e` (merged, PR #341) warns on a `SAIL` when a fleet is overloaded or short of sailors. It
weighs the units aboard at **the weights the report printed for them**, which means the usual way a
fleet gets overloaded is still silent: you load it this month and then sail it.

```
  + Ship [329] : Cog; Load: 110/750; Sailors: 6/6; MaxSpeed: 4.
    * Deckhands (11125), 6 gnolls [GNOL], sailing 3. Weight: 60.
  * Granary (8801), 1 leader, 500 grain [GRAI]. Weight: 2510.

  unit 8801
  GIVE 11125 500 GRAI      <- 2500 of weight, phase 4
  unit 11125
  SAIL N N                 <- phase 9. The Cog leaves at 2610/750 and does not sail.
```

This bead prices this month's orders into those weights, so the load compared against capacity is
the load at sailing time.

**A correction to the bead's own description, agreed with the navigator on 2026-08-17.** The bead
says to count `GIVE`/`TAKE` but not `BUY`/`SELL`, "which the server runs after movement". *The
server runs them before movement.* The rules' own Sequence of Events, scraped and committed at
`tests/fixtures/ruleset/neworigins-rules.html:7709-7845`, is unambiguous:

| phase | orders | line |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `LEAVE`, then `ENTER` | 7743, 7746 |
| 4 | `GIVE` and `TAKE` | 7775 |
| 5 | `DESTROY`, `PILLAGE`, `TAX`, `GUARD 1` | 7787-7799 |
| 7 | `SELL`, then `BUY`, then `WITHDRAW` | 7819, 7822, 7832 |
| **9** | **`ADVANCE`, `MOVE` and `SAIL`** | 7843 |
| 10 | `TEACH`, `STUDY`, `PRODUCE`, `BUILD`, `WORK` | 7853-7875 |

So everything bought this month is aboard when the fleet sails, and everything sold is already
gone. The navigator chose to count **everything the resource ledger already prices** — gifts, takes,
purchases and sales — which is both more correct and *less* code than the filtered version the bead
described, because the ledger already applies all four. Taxes, claims and studies move silver, which
weighs 0 in the ruleset; production and building happen in phase 10, after the fleet has gone.
Neither needs excluding: reading the ledger whole is right.

**What changes for a player.** A fleet loaded by gift or from the market this month is now warned.
A fleet whose cargo was sold this month stops being warned falsely. And the number in the message
splits into what the report printed and what this month changed — see *User-facing decisions*.

Nothing new appears in the interface: same `fleet-overloaded` code, same `Overloaded fleets` toggle
under `Sailing`, no change to `codes::ALL`, no vocabulary regeneration, no `SettingsDialog` change.
The crew half of the check is untouched.

**Read `ah-j0e`'s plan first** (`bd show ah-j0e --json`): it built `check_sailing`, `is_aboard`,
`could_captain`, `mode::cargo_capacity` and `mode::fleet_label`, and this bead changes one block
inside it.

**A defect found while planning this, and deliberately not fixed here: `ah-paj`.** `check_sailing`
counts sailing levels once per unit where the engine counts one per man, so a fleet crewed by
multi-man units is falsely warned `fleet-undercrewed`. It bites the very fixture this plan uses for
its look-once step. It is the crew half of the check; this bead is the load half and must not touch
it. Expect to see that spurious line in step 5 and leave it alone.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

Everything is in `crates/core/src/orders/semantics.rs`. No other crate, no TypeScript, no
regenerated file.

### The seam: one ledger per hex, read by two checks

`check_resources` (`semantics.rs:379-408`) builds a `Ledger` (`:363-377`), applies every intent to
it, calls `report_shortfalls`, and drops it. `check_sailing` needs exactly that ledger. Hoist the
construction into `check_turn` so both read one object.

Add, next to `check_resources`:

```rust
/// Everything the hex's units hold, with this month's orders applied.
///
/// Built once per hex and read by two checks: `check_resources` asks whether the sums go negative,
/// `check_sailing` asks what the change of stock weighs. One ledger rather than two, because the
/// answer has to be the same one - a fleet judged against a different set of transfers from the
/// set that produced the shortfall warnings would be two models of the same turn.
fn ledger_for<'a>(hex: &Hex<'_>, ruleset: Option<&'a Ruleset>) -> Ledger<'a> {
    let mut ledger = Ledger {
        ruleset,
        balance: BTreeMap::new(),
        doubted: BTreeSet::new(),
        charged_at: BTreeMap::new(),
    };

    for ordered in &hex.units {
        for item in &ordered.unit.items {
            ledger.balance.insert(
                (ordered.unit.unit_id.clone(), item.tag.to_ascii_uppercase()),
                item.amount,
            );
        }
    }

    for ordered in &hex.units {
        for placed in ordered.intents {
            apply(&mut ledger, hex, ordered, placed, ruleset);
        }
    }

    ledger
}
```

That body is `check_resources`'s first three statements, moved verbatim. `Ledger`'s only lifetime
parameter is the ruleset it borrows — `balance`, `doubted` and `charged_at` are all owned — so the
returned `Ledger<'a>` does not borrow `hex`, and the two lifetimes are written separately above for
that reason.

`check_resources` then becomes the one line it has left:

```rust
fn check_resources(
    hex: &Hex<'_>,
    ledger: &Ledger<'_>,
    ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
    options: &CheckOptions,
    findings: &mut Vec<Finding>,
) {
    report_shortfalls(ledger, hex, ruleset, options, findings);
}
```

and `check_turn` (`:151-180`) gains one line and changes two:

```rust
        let start = findings.len();
        let ledger = ledger_for(&hex, ruleset);
        check_resources(&hex, &ledger, ruleset, &options, &mut findings);
        check_guard(&hex, &options, &mut findings);
        check_teaching(&hex, ruleset, &options, &mut findings);
        check_forms(&hex, &options, &mut findings);
        check_sailing(&hex, &ledger, ruleset, &options, &mut findings);
```

Keep `check_resources` as a wrapper rather than calling `report_shortfalls` from `check_turn`: the
list of five `check_*` calls is the shape of this function and is worth more than the saved line.

### What a unit weighs when the fleet sails

New free function, immediately above `check_sailing`:

```rust
/// What one unit weighs when the fleet sails: the weight the report gave it, plus everything this
/// month's orders move into or out of it that the ruleset can price.
///
/// The ledger is read whole rather than filtered, because every order that changes an item balance
/// runs before the fleet does: GIVE and TAKE in phase 4, SELL and BUY in phase 7, movement in
/// phase 9. TAX, CLAIM, PILLAGE and STUDY move silver, which the ruleset weighs at 0; PRODUCE,
/// BUILD and WORK are phase 10, after the fleet has gone, and touch no balance here anyway.
///
/// An order the ledger could not price changed no balance at all - `transfer`, `buy` and the
/// WITHDRAW arm record their doubt and return before charging anything - so it contributes nothing
/// here and the unit keeps the report's weight for that part. That is the navigator's answer to
/// "silence or fall back": fall back. `None` only when the report never said what the unit weighs.
fn weight_at_sailing(
    ordered: &Ordered<'_>,
    ledger: &Ledger<'_>,
    ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
) -> Option<i64> {
    let mut weight = ordered.unit.weight?;

    for ((unit_id, tag), balance) in &ledger.balance {
        if unit_id != &ordered.unit.unit_id {
            continue;
        }
        let moved = balance - ordered.holding(tag);
        if moved == 0 {
            continue;
        }
        if let Some(item) = ruleset.and_then(|ruleset| ruleset.find_item(tag)) {
            weight = weight.saturating_add(moved.saturating_mul(item.weight));
        }
    }

    Some(weight)
}
```

Everything it leans on exists and is verified:

- `Ordered::holding(&self, tag: &str) -> i64` (`semantics.rs:330`) reads the report's own item
  amount for a tag, case-insensitively, and returns 0 for a tag the unit does not hold — which is
  exactly right for a tag the unit was only given this month.
- `Ledger::balance` is keyed `(unit_id, TAG)` with the tag uppercased at every write (`credit`
  `:706`, `charge` `:713`, and the seeding loop). `holding` is case-insensitive, so the two agree.
- `Ruleset::find_item(&self, text: &str) -> Option<&ItemEntry>`
  (`crates/core/src/movement/rules.rs:551`) resolves a tag or a name; `ItemEntry.weight: i64`
  (`rules.rs:182`) is not optional. Use `find_item` rather than indexing `ruleset.items` directly:
  the map happens to be keyed by uppercase tag today, and `find_item` does not depend on that
  staying true.
- `saturating_add`/`saturating_mul` match the house style (`apply`'s `TAX` arm, `buy`, `study`) and
  keep a garbage report from panicking a debug build.

A tag with no ruleset entry is skipped, not counted — `resolve_item` (`:658`) falls back to the
hex's own inventories when the ruleset has no match, so a balance key can name an item the
catalogue cannot weigh. With no ruleset at all, `find_item` is never reached and every unit keeps
its report weight, so a fleet is judged exactly as `ah-j0e` judges it today.

The scan over the whole balance map is deliberate. It is a few dozen entries per hex; a
`BTreeMap::range` keyed on the unit prefix would be faster and less obvious, and this is not a hot
path.

### The load, and what the message says about it

Inside `check_sailing` (`:1207`), replace **the load block only**: lines `1271-1289`, from
`if aboard.iter().all(|ordered| ordered.unit.weight.is_some()) {` through its closing brace.
Everything above it stays exactly as it is — the fleet loop, the captain, the `aboard` set, the
three doubt cases, and `let label = fleet_label(fleet);` at `:1269`. Everything below it stays too:
the crew block begins `let levels: i64 = aboard` at `:1291` and is not touched by this bead.

```rust
        // What the report says is in the hull now, before this month's ENTER and LEAVE. On a real
        // report this is the first number of the ship line's `Load: H/N`: the server computes it
        // the same way, as the sum of the weights of the units standing in the hull. Checked
        // against two committed reports - Longship [329] states 110 and holds 50 + 50 + 10
        // (neworigins-3.0.0-g3-f42-t41.rep:2018), Raft [235] states 220 and holds 20 + 200
        // (neworigins-3.0.0-g5-f21-t24.rep:1359). Summed here rather than read off the `Load:`
        // line so that a fleet whose report states no load still gets a first number, and so that
        // a report disagreeing with its own units cannot make the message claim that weight moved
        // when nothing did.
        let reported: Option<i64> = hex
            .units
            .iter()
            .filter(|ordered| {
                ordered.unit.structure_id.as_deref() == Some(fleet.structure_id.as_str())
            })
            .map(|ordered| ordered.unit.weight)
            .sum();

        let sailing: Option<i64> = aboard
            .iter()
            .map(|ordered| weight_at_sailing(ordered, ledger, ruleset))
            .sum();

        if let (Some(load), Some(capacity)) = (sailing, cargo_capacity(fleet, ruleset)) {
            if load > capacity && options.emits(codes::FLEET_OVERLOADED) {
                let overload = match reported.map(|reported| (reported, load - reported)) {
                    Some((reported, change)) if change > 0 => format!(
                        "{reported} aboard plus {change} loaded this month, \
                         on a capacity of {capacity}"
                    ),
                    Some((reported, change)) if change < 0 => format!(
                        "{reported} aboard less {} unloaded this month, \
                         on a capacity of {capacity}",
                        -change
                    ),
                    // Nothing moved, or a unit in the hull whose weight the report never gave, so
                    // there is no honest before-and-after to draw. `ah-j0e`'s own sentence.
                    _ => format!("{load} aboard on a capacity of {capacity}"),
                };
                findings.push(captain.finding(
                    hex,
                    codes::FLEET_OVERLOADED,
                    format!("{label} is overloaded: {overload}, so it will not sail"),
                    Some(sail_placement),
                ));
            }
        }
```

`Option<i64>` sums with `.sum::<Option<i64>>()` and short-circuits to `None` on the first `None`,
which is how `sailing` reproduces `ah-j0e`'s `aboard.iter().all(weight.is_some())` guard without a
separate pass. `reported` being `None` — a unit in the hull the report gave no weight for, which
`ah-j0e` tolerated when that unit was leaving — falls to the third arm rather than suppressing the
finding, so no warning that ships today is lost.

`check_sailing`'s signature gains the ledger, second, matching `check_resources`:

```rust
fn check_sailing(
    hex: &Hex<'_>,
    ledger: &Ledger<'_>,
    ruleset: Option<&Ruleset>,
    options: &CheckOptions,
    findings: &mut Vec<Finding>,
)
```

Its doc comment (`:1201-1206`) promises work this bead is doing. Replace it wholesale with:

```rust
/// Every fleet in the hex that one of our units orders to SAIL: is what is aboard within what the
/// hull carries, and is enough sailing skill aboard to sail it? Aboard means the report's units in
/// the fleet, plus those that ENTER it this month, minus those that LEAVE - the instant orders the
/// server runs before anything moves. Each of them is weighed at what this month's orders leave it
/// holding (`weight_at_sailing`), not at what the report printed, because the server runs every
/// transfer and every market order before it moves a fleet. Skills are still the report's own.
/// Anything the report or the ruleset cannot price silences the fleet - never a guess.
```

**`check_turn` is the only caller of either check** (verified: `grep -rn "check_resources\|check_sailing" crates/`
returns the two call sites, the two definitions, and one doc-comment mention in `intents.rs:599`).
So both signature changes are local, and nothing outside this file needs touching. Five parameters
is under `clippy::too_many_arguments`'s threshold of seven.

### Imports

`use std::cmp::Ordering;` is **not** needed — the `match` above is on an `Option` with guards, not
on a comparison. Nothing else new is imported: `Ruleset` and every helper are already in scope at
`semantics.rs:19-27`.

### Nothing else changes

- `codes::ALL` stays `[Code; 12]`. No new code, so `crates/core/tests/generated_ts.rs` and
  `packages/core-client/src/coreVocabulary.generated.ts` are untouched, and `check:generated` stays
  green without regeneration.
- `packages/shared/src/workspace/SettingsDialog.tsx` is untouched: the `Sailing` group and its two
  toggles already exist.
- `packages/shared/src/workspace/ProblemsPanel.tsx` is untouched. Checked, not assumed: the message
  renders at `ProblemsPanel.tsx:83-85` in a plain flex row with no `truncate` and no
  `whitespace-nowrap`, so the longer sentence wraps onto a second line and nothing is hidden at any
  width.
- `docs/ui/` — see *User-facing decisions*: the chosen mockup is committed there by the planning
  session, not by this one.

## Increments

Run `cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core` throughout. **Not every increment below opens with a red
test, and the ones that do not say so** — this is a small change against a check that already
exists, so several of the tests in the table pin behaviour the earlier increments have already
produced. Where an increment says a test should be green on arrival, a red one is a real defect,
not a test to adjust.

**1 — One ledger per hex.** A pure move, no new test: add `ledger_for`, reduce `check_resources` to
its call to `report_shortfalls`, and thread `&ledger` from `check_turn` into `check_resources`.
**Do not give `check_sailing` its parameter yet** — `rustc`'s `unused_variables` warns on an unused
function parameter, and `check:fast` runs clippy with `-D warnings`, so a `check_sailing` that
takes a ledger it does not read cannot pass the gate. It gains the parameter in increment 2, where
it starts using it. This increment's gate is that the **existing** suite stays green, all of it,
unchanged; if any test needs editing here, the move was not pure.

**2 — The load counts what the ledger moved.** RED:
`a_gift_loaded_aboard_this_month_overloads_the_fleet`, asserting the code only — today nothing
warns, because unit 11125's report weight of 50 is under the Longship's 150. GREEN by adding
`weight_at_sailing`, giving `check_sailing` its `ledger` parameter, and computing the load from it.
Still emit `ah-j0e`'s single sentence; the message forms come next. Every existing test must pass
untouched — see *Known traps*.

**3 — The message says what changed.** RED: two tests, each asserting one exact sentence —
`a_fleet_loaded_this_month_says_how_much_was_loaded` (which after increment 2 reads
`200 aboard on a capacity of 150` and must read `50 aboard plus 150 loaded this month, …`) and
`a_fleet_lightened_this_month_says_how_much_was_unloaded`. GREEN by adding `reported` and the
three-armed `match`. Then add `a_transfer_elsewhere_in_the_hex_leaves_the_fleet_alone` and
`a_unit_leaving_this_month_is_unloaded_weight`, which pin the two directions the match's `_` and
negative arms cover; the first is **green on arrival** and is there to catch a later change that
lets an unrelated transfer leak into the sum.

**4 — The rest of the surface, pinned.** The remaining rows of the table. **All of them are green
on arrival** — `weight_at_sailing` reads the whole ledger from increment 2, and `buy` (`:571`) and
`sell` (`:598-599`) already write into it, so the purchase and sale cases need no further code.
They are written because nothing else in the suite would notice if a later change filtered the
ledger, or started counting silver, or stopped falling back when an item cannot be weighed.

**5 — Gate and look once.** `pnpm run check:fast`, then the manual step in *Validation*.

## The test plan

All in `semantics.rs`'s `mod tests`, in the `--- sailing ---` section (`:2746`). Reuse the existing
helpers: `region`, `unit`, `with_item`, `with_silver`, `with_men`, `longship`, `aboard`, `sail`,
`check`, `codes`, `only`, `ruleset`. `check` runs with the committed ruleset and
`CheckOptions::default()`.

Two shapes recur. A fleet with a market:

```rust
let mut hex = ReportRegion {
    structures: vec![longship("329")],
    ..region(vec![aboard("11125", "329", 50, 4), with_silver(unit("8801"), 10_000)])
};
hex.for_sale.push(MarketItem { amount: 100, name: "horse".to_string(), tag: "HORS".to_string(), price: 1 });
```

and a giver that actually holds what it gives — **required**, or `check_resources` emits
`not-enough-items` alongside and `only(...)` fails on the count.

`longship("329")` states `Load: 110/150; Sailors: 4/4`, so capacity is 150 and the crew requirement
4. Give the captain `sail(4)` — `aboard(id, fleet, weight, 4)` — in every test that is not about the
crew, or `fleet-undercrewed` arrives as a second finding.

Item weights, read from `config/public/ruleset.json` and verified: `GRAI` 5, `HORS` 50, `GNOL`
(gnoll, a man) 10, `SILV` **0**. There is **no `VIKI`** in the catalogue — the man tags are `LEAD`,
`WELF`, `HELF`, `IDWA`, `HDWA`, `UDWA`, `ORC`, `GNOM`, `CTAU`, `HUMN`, `LIZA`, `GBLN`, `GNOL`,
`DRLF`. `MOON` is confirmed absent, which is what makes the unweighable-item test work.

**Several of these tests are written so the fleet is over capacity on the report's weights alone,
and the assertion is on the sentence rather than on silence.** A test that is silent both before and
after proves nothing; one that warns with `200 aboard on a capacity of 150` proves the gift
