### The whole-turn test, which is where a mistake here would really show

`crates/core/tests/validate_real_orders.rs:91`
(`the_committed_turn_has_no_semantic_problems_either`) runs `check_turn` over turn 71's real report
and its own template — 477 units — and asserts **zero findings**; `:168` re-asserts it fifty times.
It is not in `semantics.rs` and is easy to miss.

**It stays green, and here is why, so a red there is recognised as a real defect rather than
accepted as expected churn.** That template holds exactly one `@sail`, no `BUY`, no `SELL`, no
`TAKE`, and 136 `@give 0 all <item>` discards. A discard is `Party::Discard`, which `party_id`
(`:495`) maps to `None`, so it charges the giver and credits nobody: every priced movement in that
turn makes a unit **lighter**. A load that only falls cannot cross a capacity it was already under,
so a silent fleet stays silent. The damaged-template case at `:128` injects
`GIVE 13401 999999999 SILV`, and `SILV` weighs 0.

If that test goes red, the most likely cause is a sign error in `moved` — crediting where the code
should charge — and the message will say `loaded this month` where it should say `unloaded`.

- Rust: `cargo test -p atlantis-hud-core`, which includes `validate_real_orders`.
- TypeScript: nothing to run beyond `pnpm run check:fast`'s own suites — no TS file changes.
