## Known traps

- **Every existing sailing test passes unchanged, and that is a check on the work rather than a
  coincidence.** If one goes red, the implementation is wrong — do not edit the test. Why each
  survives, with `reported` = sum of report weights of units the report shows in the hull:
  `a_fleet_over_capacity_...` has `reported` 190 and load 190, so `change` is 0 and the plain
  sentence with `190 aboard` is emitted, exactly as asserted; `stated_numbers_beat_the_ruleset` has
  `reported` 5401 and load 5401, so `capacity of 5400` still appears; `both_problems_...`,
  `a_bare_sail_is_a_sail` and `a_unit_entering_this_month_counts_aboard` assert codes only; every
  silent test has no transfers, so no weight moves. The one edit is the added message assertion on
  `a_unit_entering_this_month_counts_aboard`.
- **`clippy::comparison_chain` is on by default and `-D warnings` makes it fatal.** Writing the
  three message forms as `if change == 0 {} else if change > 0 {} else {}` fails the gate. The
  `match` on `Option` with guards given above does not, and neither would `match change.cmp(&0)`.
- **`rustc` warns on an unused function parameter**, and `check:fast`'s
  `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` turns that into a failure. This is why increment 1 stops short of
  giving `check_sailing` its ledger.
- **A hull the report shows empty gives `reported == Some(0)`, not `None`** — `Option`'s `Sum` over
  an empty iterator is `Some(0)`. A fleet everybody boards this month therefore reads
  `0 aboard plus 250 loaded this month, on a capacity of 150`, which is correct and agrees with a
  report stating `Load: 0/150`. Do not "fix" it into the plain form.
- **A test whose giver does not hold what it gives gets two findings**, because `check_resources`
  emits `not-enough-items` from the same ledger, and `only(...)` then panics on the count. Stock
  every giver.
- **A captain without `sail(4)` gets `fleet-undercrewed` as a second finding**, for the same reason.
  `longship("329")` states `Sailors: 4/4`.
- **`Ordered::holding` is the report's amount, `Ledger::balance` is the amount after orders.** The
  difference between them is the whole of what this bead adds. Do not read `unit.items` as if it
  were the balance, and do not read the balance as if it were the holding.
- **Silver weighs 0**, so a test built on a silver gift proves nothing about weight — and neither
  does one whose fleet is silent both before and after. Use grain (`GRAI`, 5), horses (`HORS`, 50)
  or gnolls (`GNOL`, 10, a man). **There is no `VIKI` in this catalogue**, whatever Atlantis
  elsewhere calls its vikings; the fourteen man tags are `LEAD WELF HELF IDWA HDWA UDWA ORC GNOM
  CTAU HUMN LIZA GBLN GNOL DRLF`. A tag the ruleset does not carry is silently weighed at nothing,
  so a test built on one passes for the wrong reason.
- **`ledger_for` must apply intents unit-major, exactly as `check_resources` does today** — outer
  loop over `hex.units`, inner over that unit's intents. That is not document order across units,
  and it changes what `GIVE ALL` resolves to. It is pre-existing behaviour that the shortfall
  warnings already depend on; inheriting it is correct, and "fixing" it here would change findings
  this bead has nothing to do with.
- **The ledger applies orders in document order, which is not the server's phase order.** A unit
  that writes `BUY 10 GRAI` and then `GIVE 8801 ALL GRAI` gives away grain the server would not have
  bought yet. Rare, bounded, and pre-existing in the shortfall check; do not engineer around it.
- **The bead's description is wrong about the turn order.** If it and this plan disagree, the plan
  is right and the citation is in *Context*. The bead text was not rewritten.
- **`ah-paj` will show up during the look-once step** as a spurious `Undercrewed fleets` line on the
  Raft. It is a separate, already-filed bug. Do not fix it here, and do not let it fail the manual
  check.

## Context

The second half of the magic study tree (epic `ah-gjbs`). `ah-gjbs.1` builds the dialog, the ten
branch cards and the pure `magicTree.ts` module that computes them; this bead adds **the layered
whole-graph view**, **the `Branches` / `Whole graph` toggle** that reaches it, and the session
memory that only becomes meaningful once there are two views to remember.

**`ah-gjbs.1` blocks this bead and has a full plan of its own — read it first** (`bd show ah-gjbs.1`).
Everything below that names `magicTree.ts`, `MagicTreeDialog.tsx`, `MagicTree`, `MagicSkillNode`,
`skillCount` or a `magic-tree-*` test id is **a seam that bead creates**, not a file on disk today.
Where this plan needs one of those to be shaped a particular way, it says so out loud.

**Nothing new is parsed and no new data is read.** The whole graph is derived from the `MagicTree`
`ah-gjbs.1` already builds. No Rust crate changes, no `CoreAdapter` method, and
`config/public/ruleset.json` is not touched.

Facts about the picture, all measured against the shipped ruleset rather than recalled:

- **70 magic skills, in 5 tiers of 4 / 15 / 38 / 11 / 2** by longest path to a root. Four roots —
  `FORC`, `PATT`, `SPIR` and `MANI`.
- **102 prerequisite edges**, of which **8 span more than one tier**: `ARTI` reaches forward to
  `CRRI`, `CRTA`, `CRRU`, `CPOR`, `CRAG`, `CRWC`, `CRBX` and `CRHS`, all from tier 1 to tier 3.
  Those are the lines that travel, and they are drawn dashed.
- **The drawing is 1366 × 1100 world units** at the node size chosen with the navigator. It cannot
  reflow, which is why it pans and zooms at every window width rather than having a narrow-window
  layout.
- `create ring of invisibility` (`CRRI`) is at **tier 3**, and its lineage back to the roots is
  exactly seven skills — `CRRI`, `ARTI`, `INVI`, `ILLU`, `FORC`, `PATT`, `SPIR` — over eight edges.
  It is the worked example in the mockup and in the tests.

**The design is `docs/ui/ah-gjbs.2-whole-graph.html`**, merged by PR #747. It is drawn from the
shipped ruleset at real size and is the reference for everything in *User-facing decisions*.

**The pan-and-zoom arithmetic already exists and is reused wholesale.**
`packages/shared/src/workspace/mapViewport.ts` was written for the map but its transform half is
domain-free: `Viewport`, `scaleOf`, `zoomAt`, `wheelPixels`, `accumulateWheel`, `transformString`,
`MIN_STEP`, `MAX_STEP`, `STEPS_PER_DOUBLING` and `PIXELS_PER_STEP`. Nothing in that list mentions a
hex. **Do not write a second pan/zoom**: the module's own doc-comment (`mapViewport.ts:14-34`)
records three failures it was written to refuse — scale drift on round trips, guessing what a wheel
gesture meant, and moving somewhere unreversible — and a fresh implementation would meet all three
again. What is *not* reusable is the hex-shaped half: `worldOf`, `coordinateAt`, `fitTo`, `centreOn`
and `neighbour` all take a `Coordinate`, so this bead writes rectangle-shaped equivalents in its own
module.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### New — `packages/shared/src/workspace/magicGraphLayout.ts`

The whole rule, as a pure function of the `MagicTree`. **No React, no DOM.** This is what the tests
target; the component renders what it returns and measures the browser.

```ts
import type { MagicTree } from "../magicTree";
import type { Viewport } from "./mapViewport";

/** Node box geometry, in world units. The view transform scales these; they never change. */
export const NODE_WIDTH = 208;
export const NODE_HEIGHT = 22;
export const NODE_GAP = 5;
/** Horizontal distance between one tier's left edge and the next. */
export const TIER_PITCH = 268;
export const PAD_LEFT = 26;
export const PAD_TOP = 50;

/** How far one arrow press moves the view, in screen pixels. */
export const PAN_STEP = 80;

/** How a node's box is drawn. `MANI` is set apart because it is not a Foundation. */
export type NodeKind = "foundation" | "apprenticeship" | "skill";

export type GraphNode = {
  tag: string;
  name: string;
  /** The game data dictionary id, e.g. `skill:CRRI`. Taken straight from `MagicSkillNode.id`. */
  id: string;
  depth: number;
  kind: NodeKind;
  /** Top-left of the box, in world units. */
  x: number;
  y: number;
};

export type GraphEdge = {
  /** The prerequisite's tag. */
  from: string;
  /** The tag of the skill it unlocks. */
  to: string;
  /** The level `from` must reach. */
  level: number;
  /** True when it crosses more than one tier — drawn dashed. Eight of the 102 do. */
  long: boolean;
  /** A cubic bezier, right edge of `from` to left edge of `to`, in world units. */
  path: string;
  /** Where the level label sits, in world units. */
  labelX: number;
  labelY: number;
};

export type GraphTier = {
  depth: number;
  /** `Foundations`, `One step`, `Two steps`, … — see *User-facing decisions*. */
  title: string;
  count: number;
  /** Left edge of the column, in world units. */
  x: number;
};

export type MagicGraph = {
  nodes: readonly GraphNode[];
  edges: readonly GraphEdge[];
  tiers: readonly GraphTier[];
  /** World size of the whole drawing. 1366 × 1100 for the shipped ruleset. */
  width: number;
  height: number;
};

/**
 * The layered drawing of `tree`.
 *
 * Returns an empty graph — no nodes, no tiers, zero size — for a tree with no branches, which is
 * what `buildMagicTree` returns for a ruleset scraped before `magic` was added. Never throws.
 */
export function buildMagicGraph(tree: MagicTree): MagicGraph;

/** Every skill and every edge on every path from `tag` back to a root, `tag` itself included. */
export function lineageOf(
  graph: MagicGraph,
  tag: string
): { skills: ReadonlySet<string>; edges: ReadonlySet<string> };

/** How an edge is named in `lineageOf`'s set and in its test id: `${from}>${to}`. */
export function edgeKey(from: string, to: string): string;

/** The whole graph fitted into a box of screen pixels, at a whole zoom step. */
export function fitGraph(graph: MagicGraph, width: number, height: number): Viewport;

/** `node` brought to the middle of a box of screen pixels, keeping `viewport`'s zoom. */
export function centreNode(
  node: GraphNode,
  viewport: Viewport,
  width: number,
  height: number
): Viewport;

/** What a node's box can carry at a given zoom. */
export type LabelBand = "names" | "tags" | "none";
export function labelBand(step: number): LabelBand;
```

**How each piece is computed.** All derived; there is no hand-written table of skills, tiers or
positions, so a `pnpm run atlantis refresh` that adds a skill places it with no code change.

- **Nodes.** Every value of `tree.byTag`, grouped by `depth`, each group sorted by `name` with
  `localeCompare`. Position: `x = PAD_LEFT + depth * TIER_PITCH`,
  `y = PAD_TOP + rank * (NODE_HEIGHT + NODE_GAP)` where `rank` is the index within the sorted group.
- **`kind`.** `depth === 0 && tag === "MANI"` → `"apprenticeship"`; `depth === 0` → `"foundation"`;
  otherwise `"skill"`. **This is the same rule `magicTree.ts` uses to file `MANI` under its own
  branch, restated here rather than read off `MagicSkillNode.branch`** — the branch key is about the
  branch cards, and a graph that read it would break the moment a branch was renamed.
- **Edges.** For every node, `[...node.within, ...node.crossing]` — `MagicSkillNode` splits a skill's
  prerequisites into those two lists and their union is the whole `requires` list, so nothing is
  lost. `long` is `to.depth - from.depth > 1`.
- **`path`.** `M x1 y1 C mx y1 mx y2 x2 y2`, where `x1 = from.x + NODE_WIDTH`,
  `y1 = from.y + NODE_HEIGHT / 2`, `x2 = to.x`, `y2 = to.y + NODE_HEIGHT / 2`, `mx = (x1 + x2) / 2`.
  Numbers rounded to two decimals, so the attribute does not change when the layout does not.
  `labelX = x2 - 12`, `labelY = y2 - 2`.
- **`tiers[].title`.** Depth 0 is `Foundations`. Otherwise the depth in words plus `step` or
  `steps`: `["One","Two","Three","Four","Five","Six","Seven","Eight","Nine","Ten"][depth - 1]`, and
  for a depth past ten the numeral — `"11 steps"`. Ten covers a ruleset far deeper than any real
  one; the numeral fallback exists so a scraped ruleset can never crash the view. **The string is
  written in sentence case and upper-cased by CSS**, so nothing here has to know about presentation.
- **`width` / `height`.** `PAD_LEFT + tiers.length * TIER_PITCH` and
  `PAD_TOP + tallest * (NODE_HEIGHT + NODE_GAP) + 24`.
- **`fitGraph`.** `wanted = min(width / graph.width, height / graph.height)`, then
  `step = clamp(floor(log2(wanted) * STEPS_PER_DOUBLING), MIN_STEP, MAX_STEP)`, then translate so
  the drawing's centre lands on the box's centre. **Floored to a whole step**, for the reason
  `mapViewport.ts:222-254` gives about `fitTo`: a fractional scale is one zooming cannot return to,
  and rounding up clips the outermost node. At the graph dialog's real size — about 1464 × 743
  screen pixels — that gives **step −3, scale 0.5946**.
- **`labelBand`.** Keyed on the **step**, never on the scale:
  ```ts
  const FIRST_NAMES_STEP = -1;   // scaleOf(-1) = 0.841; a 10.5px name renders at 8.8px
  const FIRST_TAGS_STEP = -4;    // scaleOf(-4) = 0.5;   an 11px box still holds a 10px tag
  export function labelBand(step: number): LabelBand {
    if (step >= FIRST_NAMES_STEP) return "names";
    return step >= FIRST_TAGS_STEP ? "tags" : "none";
  }
  ```
  This is `zoomBand` (`mapViewport.ts:82-87`) again, and for the reason its doc-comment gives: a
  jittering trackpad emits a stream of tiny deltas, and a band keyed on the scale would flip twice
  in one gesture. The opening fit is step −3, so **the view always opens in the `tags` band**.
- **`lineageOf`.** Depth-first from `tag` over the incoming edges, memoised by the visited set. It is
  a DAG, so a skill reached by two paths is visited once and both its edges are kept.

### New — `packages/shared/src/workspace/MagicGraphView.tsx`

```ts
export function MagicGraphView({
  graph,
  lit,
  onLight,
  onOpenGameData,
  viewport,
  onViewport
}: {
  graph: MagicGraph;
  /** The current skill, whose lineage is lit. Null lights nothing and dims nothing. */
  lit: string | null;
  /** A first click on a skill, or a click on empty background, which passes null. */
  onLight: (tag: string | null) => void;
  /** A second click on the already-lit skill. Takes the dictionary id, e.g. `skill:CRRI`. */
  onOpenGameData: (entryId: string) => void;
  /** Where the view is, or null when it has never been placed — then it fits on mount. */
  viewport: Viewport | null;
  /** Called once at the end of every gesture, never during one. */
  onViewport: (viewport: Viewport) => void;
}): JSX.Element;
```

**The viewport is written to the DOM, not to React state, while a gesture is in flight** — the same
split `MapCanvas.tsx` uses, and for the same reason: a drag that re-rendered on every pointer move
drags badly.

- `viewRef` holds the live viewport. `applyView()` writes
  `transformString(viewRef.current)` onto the world `<g>` and sets `--graph-scale` to
  `scaleOf(step).toFixed(4)` on the `<svg>` root — exactly `MapCanvas.tsx:444-453`.
- A drag writes `viewRef` and calls `applyView()` directly. On `pointerup` it calls
  `onViewport(viewRef.current)` **once**.
- A wheel step, a zoom button, or a key calls a `commit()` that sets the ref, applies, and calls
  `onViewport`.
- **The label band is derived from the `viewport` prop, not from the ref.** A drag never changes the
  step, so the band cannot need to change mid-gesture; a zoom always commits, so it always updates.
  That is what keeps the markup out of the gesture path.

**Text at a constant screen size.** Node names, tags, tier headings and level labels live inside the
scaled world group, so their `font-size` is divided back out:
`font-size: calc(10px / var(--graph-scale, 1))`. `theme.css:235` already does exactly this for
`.map-label`, driven by `--map-scale`. **Do not counter-transform each `<text>`** — one custom
property on the root, inherited, is the whole mechanism.

Reuse, do not reimplement:

- **The wheel listener must be a manual, non-passive `addEventListener`.** React attaches `wheel`
  passively, so `preventDefault` inside an `onWheel` prop does nothing and the dialog body scrolls
  instead of the graph zooming. `MapCanvas.tsx:619-643` is the shape to copy, including the
  `carryRef` that `accumulateWheel` needs and the `getBoundingClientRect` that turns a client point
  into a point in the host.
- **No `setPointerCapture` on the root.** `MapCanvas.tsx:729-733` explains it: capture retargets the
  click to the capturing element, so no node would ever receive one and the graph would pan but
  refuse to select. Window `pointermove` / `pointerup` / `pointercancel` listeners carry a drag
  outside the element, which is all capture would have bought.
- **`guardSelection()` from `./selectionGuard` (`selectionGuard.ts:27`) while the pointer is down.**
  WebKit — the engine the desktop shell runs in — anchors a native text selection on the SVG, and a
  drag whose pointer crossed the dialog edge leaves the window reading as selected until the next
  click. Release it on `pointerup` **and** on `pointercancel`, or selection stays off everywhere for
  good.
- **A drag-slop guard and a `draggedRef`**: a pan that ends over a node must not read as a click on
  it, and a pan that ends over empty background must not clear the light. `MapCanvas.tsx:80` sets the
  threshold at `const DRAG_SLOP = 4;` — **module-private, so it cannot be imported**; declare the same
  constant with the same value in this module rather than exporting it from a file about the map.
- `zoomAt`, `wheelPixels`, `accumulateWheel`, `scaleOf`, `transformString` from `./mapViewport`.

**Click behaviour**, exactly:

- Click on node `X` with `draggedRef` false: `lit === X` → `onOpenGameData(node.id)`; otherwise
  `onLight(X)`.
- Click on the `<svg>` root itself with `draggedRef` false → `onLight(null)`.
- The background is caught by testing `event.target === event.currentTarget` on the root, the same
  guard `GameDataDialog.tsx:120-124` uses for its backdrop. A click that landed on a node bubbles
  with a different target and is not a background click.

**Centring and fitting happen on mount**, which is what makes the whole memory story fall out with
no epochs or one-shot requests: `MagicTreeDialog` renders **either** the branch view **or** the
graph, never both, so switching view mounts and unmounts them.

```
on mount, in this order:
  1. wait for the first ResizeObserver measurement of the host (see below)
  2. if `lit` is non-null and names a node:
       commit(centreNode(node, { ...base, step: Math.max(base.step, 0) }, width, height))
     else if `viewport` is null:
       commit(fitGraph(graph, width, height))
     else:
       leave it exactly where it is
```

where `base` is `viewport ?? fitGraph(graph, width, height)`. `Math.max(base.step, 0)` zooms **in**
to at least 100% so the skill's name is readable on arrival, and never zooms a reader back out from
200%. **A click inside the graph never centres** — the node is already under the pointer.

The host is measured with a `ResizeObserver`, copying `MapCanvas.tsx:505-518` including the
same-numbers guard, because the dialog resizes to `94vw` as this view mounts and the first paint
would otherwise fit against the branch view's old width.

**Keyboard — K1, one focus stop.** The `<svg>` root carries `tabIndex={0}`, `role="img"` and the
aria-label quoted in *User-facing decisions*. `role="img"` rather than a list of controls is the
honest description: it is a picture, and the label names the branch view as the readable form. The
node `<g>` elements are `aria-hidden` so a reader is not walked through seventy things it cannot
reach. On the root's `onKeyDown`:

| Key | Effect |
|---|---|
| `ArrowLeft` / `ArrowRight` / `ArrowUp` / `ArrowDown` | `commit({ ...view, tx: tx ± PAN_STEP })` / `ty` |
| `+` or `=` or `-` | `commit(zoomAt(view, ±1, width / 2, height / 2))` |
| `0` | `commit(fitGraph(graph, width, height))` |

Every one of them calls `preventDefault`, or the arrows scroll the dialog and `0` types a zero
somewhere. `+`/`=`/`-` and `0` are the same keys and the same branch shape as
`MapCanvas.tsx:800-810`. **`Escape` is not handled here** — `useEscapeToDismiss` owns it, and it
closes the dialog whether or not a skill is lit.

`data-testid`s, following `ah-gjbs.1`'s naming: `magic-graph`, `magic-graph-world`,
`magic-graph-tier-<depth>`, `magic-graph-skill-<TAG>`, `magic-graph-edge-<FROM>-<TO>`.

**Classes from `packages/shared/src/theme.css` tokens only.** The mockup's hex values are literals
in a standalone file; this application has a **light theme** as well as a dark one
(`theme.css:166-186`), so a hard-coded `#2a3a12` would be invisible in it. The mapping:

| Drawn | Token classes |
|---|---|
| an ordinary node box | `fill-panel-raised stroke-edge` |
| a foundation | `stroke-brass`, name `fill-brass-bright`, `font-semibold` |
| `MANI` | `stroke-select`, `stroke-dasharray: 3 2`, tag `fill-select` |
| the lit skill itself | `stroke-brass-bright`, stroke width 2 |
| a skill in the lit lineage | full opacity |
| a skill outside it | `opacity-[0.22]` on the whole `<g>` |
| an edge | `stroke-edge fill-none` |
| a long edge | `stroke-edge`, `stroke-dasharray: 3 2` |
| a lit edge | `stroke-brass`, width 1.6 |
| an unlit edge while something is lit | `opacity-[0.13]` |
| a name | `fill-ink`; a tag `fill-ink-dim` |
| a tier heading | `fill-brass uppercase tracking-wider`; its count `fill-ink-dim` |

Stroke widths inside the world group are divided back out the same way font sizes are —
`stroke-width: calc(1px / var(--graph-scale, 1))` — so a hairline stays a hairline at every zoom.
`MapCanvas.tsx:1050` does this already.

### Changed — `packages/shared/src/workspace/MagicTreeDialog.tsx` (created by `ah-gjbs.1`)

Four changes. Everything else that bead specifies stands.

1. **Two new props**, and the dialog stops owning the view choice:
   ```ts
   /** Which view is showing. Lives in `AppShell` so it outlives the dialog. */
   view: MagicTreeView;
   onView: (view: MagicTreeView) => void;
   /** The graph's pan and zoom, or null before it has ever been placed. Also `AppShell`'s. */
   graphViewport: Viewport | null;
   onGraphViewport: (viewport: Viewport) => void;
   ```
   with `export type MagicTreeView = "branches" | "graph";` in `magicGraphLayout.ts`.

2. **`highlighted` becomes the shared current skill.** `ah-gjbs.1` already holds exactly one field,
   `highlighted: string | null`, seeded with `useState(() => initialTag)`, set by following a
   crossing chip, and dying with the dialog. **That field is unchanged and it is what the graph
   reads and writes**: `lit={highlighted}` and `onLight={setHighlighted}`. Nothing about its
   lifetime changes, so it still dies when the dialog closes — which is the decision recorded below.
   `ah-gjbs.1`'s scroll-into-view effect is already keyed on `highlighted` rather than on
   `initialTag`, and effects run on mount, so **toggling back to Branches scrolls to the current
   skill with no new code at all**. That is the whole of S1 on the branch side.

3. **The frame becomes view-dependent.** The box's className:
   - branches — unchanged: `grid max-h-[80vh] w-[56rem] max-w-[94vw] grid-rows-[auto_auto_1fr] …`
   - graph — `grid h-[80vh] w-[94vw] grid-rows-[auto_auto_1fr] …`

   **`h-`, not `max-h-`, in graph view**: `fitGraph` needs a real height to fit into, and a box that
   shrank to its content would fit the graph into whatever height the graph had just been given.
   `theme.css:374-376` caps every modal at 90vh, but as a `:where()` default at zero specificity
   (`ah-y4zb`), so 80vh simply wins with no `!`.

   The body element is `overflow-auto` in branches and **`overflow-hidden` in graph view** — panning
   is the transform, and a scrollable body would fight it.

4. **The header strip.** A segmented toggle immediately after the `Magic study tree` label in both
   views; in graph view, three zoom buttons after it, and — while `highlighted` is non-null — the
   lit strip before the count. The bar is `flex-wrap` so the lit strip drops to a second line on a
   narrow window rather than anything being dropped. Test ids: `magic-tree-view-branches`,
   `magic-tree-view-graph`, `magic-tree-zoom-in`, `magic-tree-zoom-out`, `magic-tree-zoom-fit`,
   `magic-tree-lit`, `magic-tree-show-all`.

   The toggle buttons carry `aria-pressed={view === "…"}`. The zoom buttons carry the same
   `aria-label`s and the same glyphs the map's already use — `Zoom in` `+`, `Zoom out` `−`,
   `Zoom to fit` `⤢` (`MapViewControls.tsx:75-83`) — so the two view surfaces in this application
   do not disagree about what a zoom control looks like.

   The zoom buttons need the graph's own actions, which live below them. Hoist them the way
   `MapCanvas` does: `MagicGraphView` takes a `ref` and exposes
   `{ zoomBy(steps: number): void; fitAll(): void }` through `useImperativeHandle`, exactly as
   `MapCanvas.tsx:790` does for `MapViewControls`. **Do not lift the viewport into the dialog to
   avoid this** — that puts a value written on every wheel step above the component that owns the
   gesture.

### Changed — `packages/shared/src/workspace/AppShell.tsx`

Two `useState`s beside the `magicTreeOpen` one `ah-gjbs.1` adds at `:462`, and four props passed
down:

```ts
const [magicTreeView, setMagicTreeView] = useState<MagicTreeView>("branches");
const [magicGraphViewport, setMagicGraphViewport] = useState<Viewport | null>(null);
```

**Component state, not a `workspaceStore` field** — a deliberate departure from this bead's own
description, which said "the store field lands here". `workspaceStore` is a persisted store whose
non-persisted fields (`game`, the selection, `mapView`, `planner`) are each read by several panels;
these two are read by one component tree, must not survive a reload, and would need adding to
neither `Persisted` nor `partialize` (`workspaceStore.ts:346-359`, `:533-544`) — two omissions that
are invisible when correct and silent when wrong. A `useState` in `AppShell` is *already* exactly
"remembered for the app session, not across a reload", which is the decision. **No change to
`workspaceStore.ts` at all.**

The dialog render gains `view={magicTreeView} onView={setMagicTreeView}
graphViewport={magicGraphViewport} onGraphViewport={setMagicGraphViewport}`.

**Nothing else in `AppShell` changes.** No second palette entry (decided below), no second shortcut,
no change to the `magicTree === null` gate or to `onOpenGameData`.

### Changed — `packages/shared/src/index.ts`

Add `export * from "./workspace/magicGraphLayout";` and
`export * from "./workspace/MagicGraphView";`. Nothing in `packages/shared/src/workspace/` is
visible to the apps unless it is re-exported here.

