## User-facing decisions

Settled with the navigator over four rounds. The chosen design is
**`docs/ui/ah-gjbs.2-whole-graph.html`** (merged, PR #747). The three round-one alternatives and the
round-two and round-three variants were mockups in the planning scratchpad and are not committed —
what was rejected is recorded here instead, which is the part that has to survive.

**Round 1 — the frame.** Three were offered. **Chosen: C, the box grows for the graph.** Branches
keeps the `56rem` box it was drawn at in `ah-gjbs.1`; the whole graph opens the dialog out to
`94vw × 80vh`. Rejected: **A, one modest box** — `56rem` in both views, which never resizes but
shows about 40% of the graph at 1:1 and always needs panning on both axes. Rejected: **B, one big
box** — `94vw` in both, which fits the graph's full width but changes the branch view that was
already chosen and drawn at `56rem`, and leaves a reference dialog covering nearly the whole window.
The cost of C is understood and accepted: **the box visibly resizes when the toggle is clicked.**

**Round 1 — what a click on a node does.** **Chosen: behaviour 3.** A first click lights the skill
and every path back to the foundations, dimming everything else; a **second click on that same lit
skill** opens the game data dictionary; clicking a different skill moves the light. Rejected:
**1, a door and nothing else** — a click opens the dictionary and the graph never changes, which
leaves the one question the picture exists for answerable only by tracing a line across 1366px.
Rejected: **2, light only, with the dictionary reached from a button in the header** — it puts the
door somewhere other than the thing it opens. The cost of 3 is that the same click does two things
depending on state, which is why the header says out loud what a second click will do.

**Round 1 — where it opens.** **Chosen: fitted**, the whole graph visible at once. Rejected: 1:1 at
the top left, and 1:1 centred on the landing skill.

**Round 1 — pan and zoom across a toggle.** **Chosen: kept.** Toggling `Branches → Whole graph →
Branches → Whole graph` brings the graph back exactly where it was left. Rejected: refitting on
every switch.

**Round 2 — clearing the light.** **Chosen: a click on empty graph background, and a `Show all`
button in the header while anything is lit.** Two ways, one discoverable and one reliable. Rejected:
the button alone, which makes a stray background click harmless but leaves the graph feeling inert.
Rejected: the background alone, which nothing on screen advertises. Rejected: **Escape clearing the
light and only closing on a second press** — it breaks the workspace's own rule that Escape closes
the topmost layer, which `dismissLayer.ts:1-10` states and every dialog obeys. **Escape closes the
dialog, lit or not.**

**Round 2 — arriving on a named skill.** **Chosen: lit on arrival.** `Show in study tree` in the
unit pane, or reopening with a skill named, arrives with that skill's lineage already lit and the
skill centred — because showing what the mage's skill stands on is why the link was clicked.
Rejected: arriving merely picked out and undimmed, which costs an extra click for the thing you came
for.

**Round 2 — the words in the lit strip.** **Chosen: set A** — the skill's name, then the
instruction, then the button. Rejected: **B**, `Showing what … stands on`, which reads better but
says nothing about the second click. Rejected: **C**, name plus `6 of 70 lit` plus `Clear`, which is
compact but silent about the behaviour that most needs explaining. (C's count was also wrong: the
lineage of `CRRI` is seven skills, not six.)

**Round 2 — the keyboard.** **Chosen: K1, one focus stop.** The graph is a single tab stop; arrows
pan, `+` and `−` zoom, `0` fits, and individual skills are not keyboard-reachable inside the graph.
Rejected: **K2**, every node an ordinary button — seventy tab stops inside a surface that has to be
panned, with no way to pan by keyboard at all. Rejected: **K3**, one stop with arrows walking node
to node — everything reachable, but the most code of the three and it takes panning away from the
arrows.

**Round 3 — the labels when zoomed out.** **Chosen: Z2** — below a zoom threshold the long names are
dropped and the four-letter tags are drawn at a **constant screen size**, so the shape stays legible
all the way out; the names return as you zoom in. Rejected: **Z1**, everything shrinking together,
which is simpler but renders a 10.5px name at 6.2px in the opening fitted view.

**Round 3 — the narrow header.** **Chosen: N1**, the lit strip wraps to a second line. Nothing is
dropped, and the sentence saying what a second click does survives at every width — a narrow window
being exactly where that behaviour is hardest to discover. Rejected: **N2**, dropping the
instruction and then the count. Rejected: **N3**, collapsing the toggle to one button, which hides
which view you are in.

**Round 3 — one current skill, or two.** **Chosen: S1, one shared skill.** The branch view's
picked-out row and the graph's lit skill are the same thing, so the toggle is the same question
drawn differently: light `create ring of invisibility` in the graph and Branches opens scrolled to
it with the row picked out; follow a `+INVI 3` chip in Branches and the graph comes up centred on
`invisibility` with its lineage lit. Rejected: **S2**, each view keeping its own, which never
surprises but loses the thread a toggle was following.

**Round 3 — the words on the graph.** **Chosen: W1.** Rejected: **W2**, `1 STEP AWAY` /
`2 STEPS AWAY` with the count left at `70 skills`. Rejected: **W3**, `TIER 1` … `TIER 5`, which says
nothing about what a tier means.

**Round 4 — what survives closing the dialog.** **Chosen: the view choice and the graph's pan and
zoom; the light is dropped.** Reopening on a named skill still lights and centres that one. Rejected:
everything surviving, including the light. Rejected: only the view choice, refitting the graph on
every open. This matches `ah-gjbs.1`, where the branch view's own highlight already dies with the
dialog — so **no change to that field's lifetime is needed**.

**Round 4 — what a screen reader hears.** **Chosen: the graph names itself and points at the branch
view.** Rejected: naming itself only, which leaves the reader to find the toggle. Rejected: a
visually-hidden list of all 70 skills and their prerequisites — a second copy of the branch view's
content, which would drift from it.

