## Known traps

- **`ah-gjbs.1` must be merged first.** Every `magicTree.ts` symbol, every `magic-tree-*` test id and
  the whole `MagicTreeDialog.tsx` file are that bead's. If it has not landed, this bead cannot start;
  the `bd dep` edge says so.
- **`packages/shared` has no jsdom, by decision (`ah-nass`), and there will not be one.** Component
  tests call `renderToStaticMarkup`, which **runs no effects, attaches no refs and fires no timers**.
  This bead is unusually exposed to it: the viewport, the `ResizeObserver` fit, the wheel listener,
  the drag, the keyboard and the mount-time centring are **all** effects, and **no unit test may
  assert any of them**. Increments 6–10 assert only what is in the markup; increment 12 is what
  covers the rest. Read `packages/shared/src/testing/README.md` before writing a test here. Find
  elements with `findByTestId` / `queryByTestId` from `src/testing/elementTree.ts`; a component using
  hooks cannot be entered by that walk. Nothing in `src/testing/` may be imported by production code.
- **`MagicGraphView` must render correctly with `viewport === null`**, because that is the only state
  a static test ever sees. Transform `translate(0.00,0.00) scale(1.0000)`, band `names`.
- **React attaches `wheel` passively.** `preventDefault` inside an `onWheel` prop does nothing, so
  the dialog body scrolls instead of the graph zooming. It has to be a manual
  `addEventListener("wheel", …, { passive: false })` — `MapCanvas.tsx:619-643`.
- **No `setPointerCapture` on the SVG root.** It retargets the click to the capturing element, so no
  node would ever receive one — `MapCanvas.tsx:729-733`.
- **`guardSelection()` while the pointer is down, released on `pointercancel` as well as
  `pointerup`.** WebKit anchors a text selection on the SVG and leaves the whole window reading as
  selected; and a gesture the browser takes over would otherwise leave selection off for good —
  `MapCanvas.tsx:735-740`, `selectionGuard.ts:27`.
- **Zoom is a whole number of steps, through `zoomAt`, never a multiply.** `mapViewport.ts:29-34`
  records the bug: multiplying by 1.1 and 0.9 loses 1% per round trip and never recovers.
- **`labelBand` keys off the step, not the scale**, for the reason `zoomBand`'s doc-comment gives
  (`mapViewport.ts:44-49`): a trackpad emits a stream of tiny deltas, and a band keyed on the scale
  flips twice in one gesture.
- **Text and stroke widths inside the world group are divided by `--graph-scale`**, set on the
  `<svg>` root exactly as `MapCanvas.tsx:452` sets `--map-scale`, and read as
  `calc(10px / var(--graph-scale, 1))` — `theme.css:235` and `MapCanvas.tsx:1050` are the shipped
  examples. The `, 1` fallback matters: a static render sets no custom property, and without it
  every `font-size` computes to `NaN` and the test sees no text at all.
- **The graph's dialog box is `h-[80vh]`, not `max-h-[80vh]`.** `fitGraph` needs a real height; a box
  that sized to its content would fit the graph into the height the graph had just been given.
  `theme.css:374-376` caps modals at 90vh as a `:where()` default at zero specificity (`ah-y4zb`), so
  80vh wins with no `!` — the same note `GameDataDialog.tsx:135-142` carries about the branch view's
  `pt-[10vh]` / `max-h-[80vh]` pair, which is unchanged.
- **The graph's body must be `overflow-hidden`.** Panning is the transform; a scrollable body fights
  it, and the arrow keys would scroll the body instead of panning.
- **This application has a light theme** (`theme.css:166-186`). Use the tokens named in *Files to
  change*; the mockup's hex literals are for a standalone file and several of them are invisible on
  a light ground.
- **`MagicSkillNode.branch` is not the graph's `kind`.** The branch key is about which card a skill
  is filed under and would change if a branch were renamed; the graph decides `foundation` /
  `apprenticeship` / `skill` from the depth and the tag, which is what it actually means.
- **A skill's full prerequisite list is `within` concatenated with `crossing`.** `magicTree.ts` never
  exposes the raw `requires`, and reading only one of the two lists silently drops edges — the
  depth-1 skills would lose every foundation edge, because that bead deliberately files a depth-1
  skill's foundations under `within`.
- **A drag that ends over a node is not a click on it**, and one that ends over background is not a
  click on the background. A 4-pixel slop and a `draggedRef` — `MapCanvas.tsx:80` and `:744` are the
  shape, and `DRAG_SLOP` there is module-private, so this module declares its own.
- **Every new `data-testid` is required before the smoke suite can find anything** — every smoke
  assertion in this repository locates by test id.
- **`packages/shared/src/index.ts` must re-export both new modules**, or the apps cannot see them and
  the failure is an import error in `apps/web`, far from the change.
- **One Copilot review per PR**, requested with `gh pr edit <n> --add-reviewer @copilot` when the PR
  opens, and never again after a fix or a rebase.

## Context

A faction may only conduct trade activity — which the rules define to include producing — in so
many regions a month. Order production in one region too many and the engine refuses that whole
region's orders: `PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many regions.`, five times across three
turns of the committed corpus.

**The arithmetic was checked by hand and works exactly.** In
`tests/fixtures/reports/neworigins-3.0.0-g3-f42-t41.rep`:

```
:52  Faction Status:
:53  Tax Regions: 14 (15)
:54  Trade Regions: 15 (15)
…
:62    Smiths (12256): PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many regions.
:63    Miners (11127): PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many regions.
```

Its orders template (from `:2058`) carries 40 depth-0 `PRODUCE` orders across **16 distinct
regions**. 16 − 15 = one region refused; that region is `tundra (35,9) in Bedba`; and it holds
exactly the two units the report errors on. Confirmed from the other end too — the report's own
`Produces N x in <region>` events name 15 regions, and the one template region missing from them is
`tundra (35,9)`.

**Every `PRODUCE` counts, whatever it makes.** The rules say trade activity "includes producing
goods and materials" (`tests/fixtures/ruleset/neworigins-rules.html:623-629`) and draw no
item-type distinction. The refused region proves it: unit 11127 produced `IRON` straight from the
land (a *primary* produce) and 12256 produced `swor` from iron (a *manufacturing* produce), and both
were refused. This matters because it means **the item argument is never needed** and
`Intent::MonthLong("PRODUCE")` as it stands is enough — no parser change, no new intent.

("Trade goods" in the rules are something else entirely: `:3055` says "All items except silver and
trade goods are produced with the PRODUCE order", so they are the items that cannot be produced at
all. Do not go looking for a trade-goods flag in the ruleset; there is none, and none is wanted.)

**Two report schemas, both live in the corpus, and the check must read both:**

| schema | fixtures | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `Tax Regions: 14 (15)` and `Trade Regions: 15 (15)` | `g2-f42`, all `g3-f42` | two counters, two separate pools |
| `Regions: 10 (10)` | `g4`, all `g5`, `g7`, `g8` | one counter pooling taxing **and** trading |

`report/header.rs:60-63` already anticipates this: *"Key-agnostic: NewOrigins prints `Regions`,
standard Atlantis prints `Tax Regions` and `Trade Regions`. The label is whatever the ruleset
printed."* Under the pooled schema a `TAX` order costs a region the same as a `PRODUCE` does, and
that checks out on `g5-f21-t25.rep`: 9 produce regions ∪ 2 tax-only regions = 11 against
`Regions: 10 (10)`, and the eleventh (`swamp (35,47) in Pangmore`) is refused with both its
producers erroring.

**This is the first finding in the module that belongs to the faction rather than to a hex**, and
that is not a free choice: `Finding.region_id` is a plain `String` (`semantics.rs:135`, doc: *"Every
finding has one; that is what makes them filterable by hex"*), and the client drops a hexless
diagnostic on purpose (`packages/shared/src/orderEditor.ts:194`, `if (diagnostic.regionId === null)
continue;`). A finding with no hex would vanish from the panel **and** from the header count. The
navigator's answer to that is decision 1 below, and it keeps the whole bead in Rust.

