    // land: the first PRODUCE in the whole document, by line. Collected from the loop below rather
    // than in a second pass, because the loop already has each hex's own units and their orders.
    let mut producing: BTreeSet<&str> = BTreeSet::new();
    let mut taxing: BTreeSet<&str> = BTreeSet::new();
    let mut first_produce: Option<(&str, &str, &PlacedIntent)> = None;
```

inside it, after `let hex = Hex::read(region, &ordered);` and the `hex.units.is_empty()` guard:

```rust
        note_trade_activity(&hex, &mut producing, &mut taxing, &mut first_produce);
```

and after the loop closes:

```rust
    check_trade_regions(
        report,
        &producing,
        &taxing,
        first_produce,
        &options,
        &mut findings,
    );
```

with

```rust
/// Which regions this hex's orders spend the faction's region allowance in, and whether any
/// PRODUCE here is the earliest in the document.
///
/// PRODUCE is `Intent::MonthLong("PRODUCE")` and carries no item, which is all this needs: every
/// PRODUCE costs a region, whatever it makes. TAX is collected separately because only the pooled
/// `Regions:` schema charges it against the same allowance.
fn note_trade_activity<'a>(
    hex: &Hex<'a>,
    producing: &mut BTreeSet<&'a str>,
    taxing: &mut BTreeSet<&'a str>,
    first_produce: &mut Option<(&'a str, &'a str, &'a PlacedIntent)>,
) {
    let region_id = hex.region.region_id.as_str();

    for ordered in &hex.units {
        for placed in ordered.intents {
            match &placed.intent {
                Intent::MonthLong("PRODUCE") => {
                    producing.insert(region_id);
                    let earlier = first_produce
                        .as_ref()
                        .is_none_or(|(_, _, first)| placed.line < first.line);
                    if earlier {
                        *first_produce =
                            Some((region_id, ordered.unit.unit_id.as_str(), placed));
                    }
                }
                Intent::Tax => {
                    taxing.insert(region_id);
                }
                _ => {}
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Two details that decide whether this compiles and is right:

- **`Hex<'a>` lends its contents for the whole of `check_turn`.** `Hex::read(region, &ordered)`
  (`:220`) takes `&'a ReportRegion` and `&'a OrderedUnits`, and `Ordered<'a>` holds
  `unit: &'a ReportUnit, intents: &'a [PlacedIntent]` (`:214-217`). Both `report` and the local
  `ordered` outlive the loop, so the `&'a str` and `&'a PlacedIntent` collected here outlive each
  iteration. The `hex` binding itself does not need to.
- **`Intent::MonthLong("PRODUCE")` matches on the string literal**, which is `&'static str`
  (`intents.rs:56-61`) and set at `intents.rs:248`. `BUILD` uses the same variant, so matching the
  variant alone would count building regions too and be wrong.
- **`Option::is_none_or` is stable**; if the toolchain refuses it, write
  `first_produce.as_ref().map_or(true, |(_, _, first)| placed.line < first.line)` — but note
  `clippy::unnecessary_map_or` will then fire under `-D warnings`, so prefer `is_none_or`.

### The check

```rust
/// Whether this document orders trade activity in more regions than the faction may afford.
///
/// A faction may only produce in so many regions a month, and the engine refuses a whole region's
/// PRODUCE orders once the allowance is spent - `PRODUCE: Faction can't produce in that many
/// regions.` Which region loses is the engine's business and not knowable from here: both fixtures
/// lost the last in report order, which is two samples and not a rule, so the warning names the
/// count and the allowance and never a hex.
///
/// It belongs to the faction rather than to any one region, and every finding needs a hex - a
/// hexless one is dropped by the client on purpose (`orderEditor.ts:194`). So it lands on the first
/// PRODUCE order in the document: one line for one mistake, on something the player can click.
fn check_trade_regions(
    report: &ParsedReport,
    producing: &BTreeSet<&str>,
    taxing: &BTreeSet<&str>,
    first_produce: Option<(&str, &str, &PlacedIntent)>,
    options: &CheckOptions,
    findings: &mut Vec<Finding>,
) {
    if !options.emits(codes::TOO_MANY_TRADE_REGIONS) {
        return;
    }

    let (Some((allowance, pooled)), Some((region_id, unit_id, placed))) =
        (trade_allowance(report), first_produce)
    else {
        return;
    };

    let used = if pooled {
        producing.union(taxing).count()
    } else {
        producing.len()
    };
    let used = i64::try_from(used).unwrap_or(i64::MAX);
    if used <= allowance {
        return;
    }

    let message = if allowance == 0 {
        "this faction may not trade in any region, so every PRODUCE order will be refused"
            .to_string()
    } else {
        let excess = used - allowance;
        let regions = if excess == 1 { "region's" } else { "regions'" };
        if pooled {
            format!(
                "PRODUCE and TAX orders in {used} regions; this faction may tax and trade in \
                 {allowance}, so {excess} {regions} orders will be refused"
            )
        } else {
            format!(
                "PRODUCE orders in {used} regions; this faction may trade in {allowance}, so \
                 {excess} {regions} production will be refused"
            )
        }
    };

    findings.push(Finding {
        code: codes::TOO_MANY_TRADE_REGIONS,
        message,
        region_id: region_id.to_string(),
        unit_id: Some(unit_id.to_string()),
        line: Some(placed.line),
        column_start: Some(placed.column_start),
        column_end: Some(placed.column_end),
    });
}
```

`Finding` is built by hand rather than through `Ordered::finding` (`:339`), because that needs a
`&Hex` and the loop that owned the hexes has closed. The fields are exactly what `Ordered::finding`
would have set.

**Appending after the loop is correct, and does not break the ordering.** `check_turn` sorts each
hex's findings inside the loop (`:176`), so this one stays at the end of the returned vector — but
`validate_turn` re-sorts the whole diagnostic list by line before anybody sees it
(`orders/mod.rs:67`, *"Line order across the whole document"*), and `findingsByHex` groups by
region regardless of order. Tests that call `check_turn` directly will see it last; see the test
plan.

