| `producing_in_more_regions_than_the_faction_may_is_warned` | three regions, one own unit each; `Trade Regions` max 2; a `PRODUCE` in each | one finding, code `too-many-trade-regions`, message exactly `PRODUCE orders in 3 regions; this faction may trade in 2, so 1 region's production will be refused` |
| `the_warning_lands_on_the_first_produce_in_the_document` | as above | `unit_id` is the unit whose `PRODUCE` has the **lowest line**, `region_id` is that unit's region, and `line` is that `PRODUCE`'s line. Order the document so the first `PRODUCE` is *not* in the first region, or the test passes by accident |
| `two_regions_too_many_reads_as_a_plural` | four regions producing; max 2 | message ends `so 2 regions' production will be refused` |
| `a_pooled_regions_counter_charges_tax_orders_too` | three regions: two `PRODUCE`, one `TAX` only; label `Regions`, max 2 | one finding, message exactly `PRODUCE and TAX orders in 3 regions; this faction may tax and trade in 2, so 1 region's orders will be refused` |
| `a_faction_that_may_not_trade_at_all_is_told_so` | one region producing; label `Regions`, max **0** | message exactly `this faction may not trade in any region, so every PRODUCE order will be refused` |
| `producing_within_the_allowance_is_silent` | two regions producing; max 2 | `codes(...)` empty — exactly at the allowance is a legal and common turn |
| `tax_orders_do_not_count_against_a_separate_trade_counter` | two regions producing, three more taxing; `Trade Regions` max 2 | `codes(...)` contains no `too-many-trade-regions`. The separate schema has its own tax pool |
| `several_produce_orders_in_one_region_count_once` | one region, three units each producing; max 1 | `codes(...)` empty — the allowance counts regions, not orders |
| `a_report_with_no_faction_status_is_not_judged` | three regions producing, `report(regions)` with no entries; max nowhere | `codes(...)` empty |
| `a_status_block_with_no_region_label_is_not_judged` | three regions producing; a single entry labelled `Quartermasters`, max 2 | `codes(...)` empty. It looks for `Trade Regions`, then `Regions`, and gives up |
| `no_produce_orders_means_nothing_to_warn_about` | three regions of units that only `TAX`; label `Regions`, max 1 | `codes(...)` empty. There is no `PRODUCE` line for the finding to land on, and no production to refuse |
| `a_build_order_is_not_a_produce` | three regions each with `BUILD`; `Trade Regions` max 1 | `codes(...)` empty. `BUILD` shares `Intent::MonthLong`, and matching the variant rather than the string would count it |

**The finding comes back last.** `check_trade_regions` runs after the region loop, so in
`check_turn`'s own output it is the final element regardless of its line. A test asserting
`codes(&findings)` alongside other codes must expect it there; `validate_turn` re-sorts by line
before the panel sees it, and that is not `check_turn`'s job.

**The per-code fixture table** (`:2649`) gains one row. It needs a report, and every other row is
`(Code, Vec<ReportRegion>, &str)` — so **either** extend the tuple to carry an optional allowance,
**or** give this code a row whose regions and orders make it fire under the table's own
`report(regions.clone())` call, which cannot work because that report has no faction status.
**Extend the table**: change the tuple to `(Code, ParsedReport, &str)` and have every existing row
wrap its regions in `report(vec![…])`. That is a mechanical edit of twelve rows and keeps the
"every code has a fixture" guarantee, which is the point of the test.

**The whole-turn test stays green, and this has been checked rather than assumed.**
`crates/core/tests/validate_real_orders.rs:91` asserts zero findings over turn 71
(`g7-f95-t71.rep`), a `Borg TNG (95) (Magic 5)` faction whose status block reads
**`Regions: 0 (0)`** at `:49` — under which *any* `PRODUCE` is an error. Its template contains
**zero** `PRODUCE` orders, indented or not, so nothing fires. Run it at the end of increment 1
anyway: it is the cheapest broad check there is, and a red would mean the allowance comparison is
inverted.

**TypeScript:** `SettingsDialog.test.tsx` as edited.

## User-facing decisions

Interviewed with the navigator on 2026-08-17 against
**`docs/ui/too-many-trade-regions.html`** (PR #364, merged). Read it before starting — it carries the rejected options, including the deferred one, and the three sentences in place.

**1. The warning lands on the first `PRODUCE` order in the document** — one line for one mistake,
in that order's hex, underlined on that order.
*Rejected:* a copy in every producing region, which is impossible to miss but puts sixteen identical
lines in the panel and adds sixteen to the header's problem count for a single mistake. Also
rejected, and explicitly deferred rather than dismissed: **a faction-wide row of its own** in the
panel. It is the honest answer and `ah-oq3`'s quartermaster check is the same shape, but it means
teaching `Finding` an optional hex and changing `findingsByHex`, `findingsForHex`, `ProblemsPanel`
and the header count. That is a second bead, and nobody has filed it.

**2. The three sentences, verbatim:**

```
PRODUCE orders in 16 regions; this faction may trade in 15, so 1 region's production will be refused
PRODUCE and TAX orders in 11 regions; this faction may tax and trade in 10, so 1 region's orders will be refused
this faction may not trade in any region, so every PRODUCE order will be refused
```

Chosen so the player has both numbers and can check them against the report's own status block,
and knows how many regions to drop.
*Rejected:* naming only the surplus — `one region too many is producing this month; drop a PRODUCE
order somewhere` — which is shorter and more actionable but hides the arithmetic.

**Two details of those sentences are mine and the navigator may reverse either.** The mockup drew
the excess as the word *one*; digits are used instead so the sentence reads the same when the
player is three regions over, and `region's` becomes `regions'` then. And the third sentence is used
for both schemas, including the pooled one it does not perfectly describe — the two committed
`Regions: 0 (0)` reports are pooled, and inventing a fourth sentence to say *"may not tax or
trade"* seemed a worse trade than one sentence that is true about the PRODUCE being refused.

**3. The toggle** is `Producing in too many regions`, described
`PRODUCE orders in more regions than the faction's allowance permits.`, in the **existing** *Orders*
group, **on by default**.

**4. Accepted, drawn in the mockup, and not to be re-opened:** exactly at the allowance is silent;
a report with no `Faction Status:` block, or with no label the check recognises, is silent; **which
region loses is never named**, because both fixtures lost the last in report order and two samples
are not a rule; orders inside `TURN` blocks are not counted; and a `PRODUCE` that would fail for
some other reason is still counted, because whether the engine charges a region for it is not
something the corpus settles.

## Out of scope

- **A faction-wide bucket in the Problems panel.** Decision 1's rejected option, and the right
  long-term answer. Not filed.
- **`ah-oq3`, the quartermaster allowance**, which reads the same `Faction Status:` block and is
  already planned separately. If it has landed, `trade_allowance` may have a sibling worth naming
  consistently — but do not refactor its code from this bead.
- **The `Tax Regions` counter on its own.** The separate schema has a tax pool this check does not
  police; a bead for it would be the mirror of this one and is not filed.
- **`TRANSPORT`**, which the rules also count as trade activity in the issuing unit's hex
  (`neworigins-rules.html:3872`). It is not read as an intent, no corpus error names it, and adding
  it would need parser work for no evidenced gain.
- **The `used` figure** in the status block, which describes last month.
- **Which region the engine refuses.** Not knowable; see decision 4.
- **Whether the pooled counter also charges `BUY`/`SELL`.** No fixture separates the hypotheses —
  the one selling region in the corpus was already producing. The rules name only producing and
  `TRANSPORT`.

