## Known traps

- **`Intent::MonthLong` is shared with `BUILD`.** Matching the variant instead of the string
  `"PRODUCE"` counts every building region against the trade allowance.
  `a_build_order_is_not_a_produce` is the test.
- **Two schemas, and the pooled one charges `TAX`.** A check that only ever looks for
  `Trade Regions` is silent on the majority of the corpus, which prints `Regions`. A check that
  ignores `TAX` under the pooled schema under-counts and misses the `g5-f21-t25` case.
- **`Regions: 0 (0)` is real** — two committed reports have it, for a pure-magic faction. Any
  `PRODUCE` at all is then an error, and `used <= allowance` must not swallow it.
- **`used` is last month's spend, not this month's.** Compare the document's own count against
  `maximum`, never against `used`.
- **The allowance counts *regions*, not orders.** Sixteen `PRODUCE` orders in one hex cost one
  region. That is what the `BTreeSet` is for.
- **The finding is appended after the region loop**, so `check_turn` returns it last regardless of
  its line. That is correct — `validate_turn` re-sorts by line (`orders/mod.rs:67`) — but a test
  asserting the order of `codes(&findings)` must expect it at the end.
- **The per-code fixture table needs its tuple widened** to carry a report, because this is the
  first code whose fixture needs a header. Twelve existing rows change mechanically.
- **`ALL`'s length is a type**, and three other planned beads (`ah-djq`, `ah-yfo`, `ah-d8u`) add to the same array, the same
  *Orders* group, the same fixture table and the same settings-test list. Expect small conflicts if
  more than one is in flight; count what is actually there.
- **Turn 71 is a `Regions: 0 (0)` faction**, so it would fire on any `PRODUCE` at all — but its
  template has none, checked by hand, and `validate_real_orders.rs:91` stays green. A red there
  means the comparison is inverted, not that the fixture is at fault.

## Context

Child 1 of epic ah-8m0 (Bishop, weekly sweep 2026-08-15: "the store's rules are written twice, in
SQL and TypeScript, and diverge"). Planned by Beast on 2026-08-16.

The game backup document — one JSON file holding a whole game — is encoded and decoded twice:

- **Desktop:** `crates/core-persistence/src/lib.rs` — the `GameBackup*` serde structs (:213–277),
  `export_game` (:547, five `SELECT … ORDER BY` + `serde_json::to_string_pretty`) and
  `import_game` (:682, `serde_json::from_str::<GameBackup>` + format/version checks + one
  transaction of `INSERT`s, with ~70 lines re-deriving a remembered region's columns out of its
  payload and re-synthesising its label with a hand-copied format string at :852).
- **Web:** `packages/browser-core/src/webCoreAdapter.ts` — five `GameBackup*` TS types (:113–162),
  `GAME_BACKUP_FORMAT`/`CURRENT_GAME_BACKUP_VERSION` twins (:14–15), `parseBackupJson` (:215–397,
  ~180 lines of hand validation with its own error wording), `exportGame` (:704) and `importGame`
  (:800).

They already disagree in four places, none of which anyone decided: the timestamp fallback for a
turn without stamps (`import_game` fills `imported_at` from `created_at`, the web leaves it
`undefined`); the payload checks on a remembered region (desktop refuses a corrupt one, web writes
it through); the label a restored sighting carries (`"terrain (x,y) in province, turn N"` on the
desktop vs `ReportRegion::label()`'s `"terrain (x,y) in province"` everywhere else); and the wording
of a shape error (`"backup file imported turn 3 is missing its faction id"` vs
`"serialization error: missing field …"`). Every persisted concept since #75 has landed in both
codecs (78a2aa7 added hex notes to each).

**After this bead there is one codec, in `atlantis_hud_core::backup`.** It owns the envelope
(`format`, `version`, `exportedAt`), the version bounds, the field set, the timestamp fallbacks,
the region-payload checks and the sort order of every table in the file. The desktop's
`export_game`/`import_game` become *SELECT rows → `encode_game_backup`* and
*`decode_game_backup` → INSERT rows*; the web's become *read five stores →
`encode_game_backup_state`* and *`decode_game_backup_state` → put five stores*. Neither store
knows what a backup looks like any more. The `GameManifest` family moves into the core with it
(the backup carries a manifest), re-exported from `core-persistence` so nothing else moves.

The two sibling children (ah-8m0.2: what a report import writes; ah-8m0.3: how the latest turn
is ranked and lists are ordered) are separate beads and blocked on this one; do not fold them in.

The wasm persistence arm Bishop mentions was deleted by ah-350 (#293); the web keeps its own
IndexedDB store, and this bead does not change that.

## Files to change, and what to reuse

### `crates/core/src/backup.rs` — new; `pub mod backup;` in `crates/core/src/lib.rs` (after `pub mod cache;` at :3)

Everything below is `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]` and `#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq,
Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]` unless said otherwise. The core has no `thiserror`; write `Display`
by hand as `crates/core/src/movement/rules.rs:304` does for `RulesetError`.

```rust
//! One game as one file: the backup document both platforms export and import.
//!
//! The desktop stores in SQLite and the web in IndexedDB, and each used to encode and decode this
//! document itself. Every rule about the file - its envelope, its version bounds, what a row must
//! carry, what an absent stamp means, the order tables are written in - lives here and nowhere
//! else; a store reads its rows and hands them over, or takes rows back and writes them.

pub const GAME_BACKUP_FORMAT: &str = "atlantis-hud-game-backup";
pub const CURRENT_GAME_BACKUP_VERSION: u32 = 1;
/// Moved from core-persistence (:13) with the manifest types; `create_game`/`open_game` still check it.
pub const CURRENT_MANIFEST_VERSION: u32 = 1;

// Moved verbatim from crates/core-persistence/src/lib.rs :74, :88, :96 - field names, docs and derives unchanged:
pub struct GameMetadata { pub game_id: String, pub game_name: String, pub ruleset_id: String }
pub struct ReportSourceRef { pub source_id: String, pub label: String }
pub struct GameManifest { pub manifest_version: u32, pub metadata: GameMetadata, pub report_sources: Vec<ReportSourceRef>, pub created_at: String, pub last_opened_at: String }

// Moved verbatim from :229-277 (wire shapes; the Option stamps stay Options on the wire):
pub struct GameBackupImportedTurn { pub faction_id: String, pub turn_number: u32, pub raw_report: String, pub parsed_payload_json: String, pub warnings_payload_json: String, pub imported_at: Option<String>, pub updated_at: Option<String> }
pub struct GameBackupOrderDraft { pub faction_id: String, pub turn_number: u32, pub order_text: String, pub updated_at: String }
pub struct GameBackupRegionSighting { pub faction_id: String, pub region_id: String, pub last_seen_turn: u32, pub payload_json: String }
pub struct GameBackupMergedReport { pub faction_id: String, pub turn_number: u32, pub merged_faction_id: String, pub merged_faction_name: String, pub merged_at: String }
pub struct GameBackupHexNote { pub id: String, pub region_id: String, pub text: String, pub on_map: bool, pub turn: u32, pub created_at: String, pub updated_at: String }

/// The rows of one game, without the envelope: what a store hands over to be encoded.
pub struct GameBackupContent {
    pub manifest: GameManifest,
    pub imported_turns: Vec<GameBackupImportedTurn>,
    pub order_drafts: Vec<GameBackupOrderDraft>,
    pub region_sightings: Vec<GameBackupRegionSighting>,
    pub merged_reports: Vec<GameBackupMergedReport>,
    pub hex_notes: Vec<GameBackupHexNote>,
}

/// The document as written: the envelope around the content. `#[serde(default)]` on
/// `exported_at` (a file written by hand may omit it; nothing reads it back) and on `hex_notes`
/// (absent in a backup written before ah-o1t.1; such a backup still imports).
pub struct GameBackup {
    pub format: String,
    pub version: u32,
    #[serde(default)] pub exported_at: String,
    pub manifest: GameManifest,
    pub imported_turns: Vec<GameBackupImportedTurn>,
    pub order_drafts: Vec<GameBackupOrderDraft>,
    pub region_sightings: Vec<GameBackupRegionSighting>,
    pub merged_reports: Vec<GameBackupMergedReport>,
    #[serde(default)] pub hex_notes: Vec<GameBackupHexNote>,
}

/// One imported turn as a store writes it after a decode: both stamps resolved, never optional.
pub struct DecodedImportedTurn { pub faction_id: String, pub turn_number: u32, pub raw_report: String, pub parsed_payload_json: String, pub warnings_payload_json: String, pub imported_at: String, pub updated_at: String }

/// One remembered region as a store writes it: the faction that saw it, and the full sighting
/// (coordinate, terrain, province, label, turn, payload) rebuilt from the payload - so the desktop
/// can fill its columns and the web can take `regionId`/`lastSeenTurn`/`payloadJson`, neither
/// reading the payload itself.
pub struct DecodedRegionSighting { pub faction_id: String, #[serde(flatten)] pub sighting: crate::report::sighting::RegionSighting }

/// What `decode_game_backup` hands a store: rows ready to write, and a manifest already stamped
/// with the opening time.
pub struct DecodedGameBackup {
    pub manifest: GameManifest,
    pub imported_turns: Vec<DecodedImportedTurn>,
    pub order_drafts: Vec<GameBackupOrderDraft>,
    pub region_sightings: Vec<DecodedRegionSighting>,
    pub merged_reports: Vec<GameBackupMergedReport>,
    pub hex_notes: Vec<GameBackupHexNote>,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum BackupError {
    /// The text is not JSON at all.                       -> "backup file is not valid JSON"
    NotJson,
    /// JSON, but `format` is missing or not ours.         -> "backup file is not an Atlantis HUD game export"
    NotABackup,
    /// `version` is missing or not a number.              -> "backup file does not say which version it is"
    NoVersion,
    /// `version` > CURRENT_GAME_BACKUP_VERSION.           -> "backup file format version {actual} is newer than this build supports ({max_supported})"
    NewerVersion { actual: u32, max_supported: u32 },
    /// `version` < 1.                                     -> "backup file format version {0} is not supported"
    UnsupportedVersion(u32),
    /// `manifest.manifestVersion` > CURRENT_MANIFEST_VERSION. -> "invalid game manifest version: expected <= {max_supported}, got {actual}"
    UnsupportedManifestVersion { actual: u32, max_supported: u32 },
    /// Envelope was fine but the body is not the shape a backup has; carries serde's own message,
    /// which names the field and its position.            -> "backup file could not be read: {0}"
    Malformed(String),
    /// A remembered region whose payload cannot stand for it. -> "invalid game backup: {0}"
    ///   "remembered region {id} is missing regionId in its payload"
    ///   "remembered region {id} does not match its payload id {other}"
    ///   "remembered region {id} is missing its coordinate"      (also "coordinate x" / "coordinate y" / "coordinate z" when one of them is absent or not an integer)
    ///   "remembered region {id} is missing its terrain" / "… its province"
    InvalidSighting(String),
    /// `encode` could not serialise (never expected; carries serde's message). -> "backup could not be written: {0}"
    Unwritable(String),
}
impl std::fmt::Display for BackupError { /* the strings above, exactly */ }
impl std::error::Error for BackupError {}

/// One game → one document. Sorts every table so two exports of the same game are byte-identical
/// whichever platform wrote them: turns by (faction_id, turn_number); drafts by (faction_id,
/// turn_number); sightings by (faction_id, region_id); merged reports by (faction_id,
/// turn_number, merged_at, merged_faction_id); hex notes by (created_at, id) - the same keys the
/// desktop's five ORDER BYs used. Pretty-printed with two-space indent (`serde_json::to_string_pretty`).
pub fn encode_game_backup(content: GameBackupContent, exported_at: &str) -> Result<String, BackupError>;

/// The wasm-facing twin: the content arrives as JSON text (a store's own records, serialised as
/// they are). Deserialised as `GameBackupContent`, which is what makes it lenient - serde ignores
/// keys it does not know (`databasePath`, `gameId`, anything a store keeps beside the wire fields)
/// and reads an absent `importedAt`/`updatedAt` as `None`. Then `encode_game_backup`.
/// Errors: `Malformed` (content JSON is not `GameBackupContent`), `Unwritable`.
pub fn encode_game_backup_json(content_json: &str, exported_at: &str) -> Result<String, BackupError>;

/// One document → rows to write. Two-stage on purpose: the envelope (`format`, `version`) is read
/// from a `serde_json::Value` and judged FIRST, so a file that is not a backup, or is from a newer
/// version, says so even when its body is also missing fields (the smoke test's version-99 fixture
/// has no `exportedAt`, and must still be refused for its version). Only then is the Value turned
/// into a `GameBackup` (`serde_json::from_value`; failure -> `Malformed`).
///
/// Then, in this order:
///   1. `manifest.manifest_version <= CURRENT_MANIFEST_VERSION` or `UnsupportedManifestVersion`;
///   2. `manifest.last_opened_at = opened_at` (an imported game is opened now);
///   3. each imported turn: `imported_at = turn.imported_at.unwrap_or(manifest.created_at)`,
///      `updated_at = turn.updated_at.unwrap_or(imported_at)` - the desktop's rule (:706-714),
///      chosen because it leaves every restored turn rankable;
///   4. each region sighting: `sighting_from_payload(&region_id, last_seen_turn, &payload_json)`
///      (below), or `InvalidSighting`;
///   5. drafts, merged reports and hex notes pass through unchanged (a note's `game_id` is the
///      store's to add - the file never carries one).
pub fn decode_game_backup(backup_json: &str, opened_at: &str) -> Result<DecodedGameBackup, BackupError>;
```

